chx:Document a doc:ReportSection ; rdfs:label "Bitcoin Core, the reference implementation: chapter 3 of the 3rd edition checked against release 31.1"@en ; skos:definition "This document renews chapter 3 of the 3rd edition by running the real Bitcoin Core release 31.1 and reading its source, so that the book's commands, outputs and numbers are explained and checked against what the software does today." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03/research> ;
    doc:hasSection chx:S_Node, chx:S_Build, chx:S_Operation, chx:S_Interface, chx:S_Data, chx:S_Ecosystem, chx:S_Semantics, chx:S_Computing .
chx:S_Node a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Node"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Node" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 1 ;
    skos:definition "A node is a computer that runs Bitcoin software and takes part in the peer-to-peer network, and this first branch of the chapter is about the most complete kind of node, the full verification node. The chapter opens with the reason such a node exists. People accept money in exchange for their goods and services only if they believe that they will be able to spend that money later, and money that is counterfeit or unexpectedly debased may not be spendable later, so every person who accepts bitcoins has a strong incentive to verify the integrity of what they receive (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The software that performs this verification is the subject of the whole chapter, and the program most operators use for it is Bitcoin Core.\n\nThe branch matters because it decides whom a user has to trust. The book explains that Bitcoin was designed so that software running entirely on your own computer can prevent counterfeiting, debasement and several other critical problems, and that the data such a node reports is authoritative not because a powerful entity designated it as such but because the node independently verified it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Every later chapter of the course examines transactions, blocks and mining, and it does so through the data that a node of this kind provides, so the branch is the practical foundation for everything that follows.\n\nYou meet nodes whenever someone checks a payment or reads the blockchain. Bitcoin Core is the implementation that most full node operators have used since the beginning of the network, and the book uses it throughout the rest of its chapters to create and examine data about the blockchain and the network (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Businesses that build on Bitcoin run nodes as well: the book notes that Bitcoin software companies typically run several of them, and the bitcoin.org page on full nodes adds that most full nodes also serve lightweight clients, which are wallets that do not verify everything themselves (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin.org, 2026).\n\nThe branch has four groups of concepts. The verification group explains what a full node is, why Bitcoin Core is called the reference implementation, how proposals for changes are documented in Bitcoin Improvement Proposals, and how a soft fork changes the rules without splitting the network. The project group explains that Bitcoin Core is open-source software created by Satoshi Nakamoto and now maintained by a community. The resource group describes what a node costs in disk space, time and connections, and what it keeps in memory and on disk. The remaining branches of the chapter then follow the order of the book: building the software, operating it, calling its interface, reading its data, and checking the ecosystem around it.\n\nA full node is a choice with costs, and it should not be confused with other roles. It is not a miner, because it does not need to produce blocks; it is not necessarily a wallet, although Bitcoin Core includes one; and it is not the only implementation of the protocol, as the last branches of the chapter show. Running a node also does not remove every kind of trust, because a user still has to trust the software that was downloaded, which is why the ecosystem branch returns to the verification of releases." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Node> .
chx:S_Verification a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Verification"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Verification" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 2 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Node ;
    skos:definition "Verification is the second level of the node branch and gathers four ideas: the full node itself, the reference implementation, the Bitcoin Improvement Proposals and the soft fork. Together they answer one question, which is how a participant can be sure that what the software enforces is what the Bitcoin system actually requires. The first concept names the program that checks the rules, the second names the program that serves as the model of how to check them, and the last two explain how the written rules are documented and how they are changed (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nChecking rules is only meaningful if everyone checks the same rules. A node that accepted a payment which the others rejected would be living on a different version of the money. For that reason the group does not stop at describing a program; it also describes the documents, the Bitcoin Improvement Proposals, in which the design of each major part of the system is written down, and the mechanism, the soft fork, by which the rules can be tightened while old and new nodes stay on the same chain.\n\nThe sections of the group move from the program to the rules. The full node verifies every confirmed transaction. Bitcoin Core is the reference for how each part should be implemented. A Bitcoin Improvement Proposal describes a feature in a numbered document that anyone can read, and a soft fork is the way a changed rule is introduced. A learner who has read the four sections can explain why a transaction that a node accepts is trustworthy, and also why the word reference does not mean that the code alone defines Bitcoin." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Verification> .
chx:S_FullNode a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Full node"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Full node" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 3 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Verification ;
    skos:definition "A full node, which the chapter introduces as a full verification node, is software that verifies every confirmed Bitcoin transaction against every rule in the system (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The bitcoin.org page on running a node puts it in one sentence: a full node is a program that fully validates transactions and blocks, and almost all full nodes also help the network by accepting transactions and blocks from other full nodes, validating them and relaying them to further full nodes (Bitcoin.org, 2026). The adjective full distinguishes this node from a lightweight client, the kind of wallet described in chapter 1 that asks other computers for answers and checks only a small part of them.\n\nThe reason for verifying everything is trust. The book states that the Bitcoin system was designed so that software on your own computer can perfectly prevent counterfeiting and debasement, and it describes the data that your own node returns as authoritative because your node independently verified it, whatever any powerful entity may say (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A user who relies on someone else's server to say that a payment has arrived has to trust that server. A user who relies on a full node has only to trust the software and the rules, and the rules are public.\n\nThe chapter gives the common reasons for running one: not wanting to rely on any third party to validate the transactions you receive, not wanting to disclose to third parties which transactions belong to your wallet, developing Bitcoin software that needs programmable access to the network and the blockchain, building applications that must validate transactions according to the consensus rules, and supporting the network, which becomes more robust when more nodes validate (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book concludes that a reader who is interested in strong security, superior privacy or developing Bitcoin software should be running their own node.\n\nA full node receives blocks and transactions from its peers and checks each of them against the consensus rules, which chapter 2 listed for transactions: the syntax and data structure must be correct, the lists of inputs and outputs must not be empty, the amounts must be within the allowed range, the outputs being spent must exist and be unspent, the inputs must cover the outputs, and the scripts must validate (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Only what passes is kept and relayed. The start-up log of Bitcoin Core shows the machinery that does this work, with a cache for signatures and a number of additional threads for script verification; the book's example log reports a signature cache of 16 MiB and 3 additional threads (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nVerifying every rule has a price that the chapter states openly: processing more than 500 GB of data at first and about 400 MB of new transactions a day, in the book's figures for 2023 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A full node also does not make a user's money safe by itself, because the wallet that holds the keys is a separate function, and it does not make a user a miner. Finally, the word full is not a promise about storage: a pruned node, described in the resource group, still verifies the whole chain but keeps only the most recent blocks." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#FullNode> .
chx:S_ReferenceImplementation a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Reference implementation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Reference implementation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 4 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Verification ;
    skos:definition "A reference implementation is a program that serves as the model of how a specification should be realised. The book calls Bitcoin Core the reference implementation of the Bitcoin system, meaning that it provides a reference for how each part of the technology should be implemented, and it lists what Bitcoin Core implements: wallets, a transaction and block validation engine, tools for block construction, and all modern parts of the peer-to-peer communication (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The project's own readme describes it more briefly: it connects to the Bitcoin peer-to-peer network to download and fully validate blocks and transactions, and it includes a wallet and a graphical user interface that can optionally be built (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA decentralized network needs participants who agree exactly on what is valid, and agreement is easier when a complete, running program exists that others can compare themselves with. The point is made by the source code itself: the constant that limits the total amount of money is annotated in Bitcoin Core with the remark that its exact value is consensus critical (Bitcoin Core, 2026). If two implementations differed even slightly on a rule of this kind, a block that one accepted could be rejected by the other, which is why the implementation that most nodes run carries so much weight.\n\nThe history explains the word. When Satoshi Nakamoto created Bitcoin, the software was mostly completed before the whitepaper was published, because Nakamoto wanted to be sure that the implementation worked before publishing a paper about it. That first implementation, then known simply as Bitcoin, has been heavily modified and improved and has evolved into Bitcoin Core, named to differentiate it from other implementations (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The continuity is still visible in the source: the first line of Bitcoin Core's file for the proof-of-work rules carries a copyright notice for 2009 to 2010 in the name of Satoshi Nakamoto (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe book includes a figure of the architecture of Bitcoin Core, credited to Eric Lombrozo, beside this list of parts: the wallet, the validation engine, block construction and the peer-to-peer layer belong to one program (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In practice a user meets the program as a small set of executables: a node daemon, a command-line client, a graphical client and tools for transactions and wallets, which the build and operation branches describe. Each version is released under a number, and the project marks stable versions with a release tag in its source repository.\n\nReference does not mean that the code is the specification. The book points out that the whitepaper describes several early parts of the system and that most major parts since 2011 are documented in Bitcoin Improvement Proposals, so the written documents and the code together describe the rules (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It also does not mean that Bitcoin Core is the only implementation: the final section of the chapter lists libraries, toolkits and full-node implementations in many languages, and the book's chapter on the network says that the most common implementation is Bitcoin Core, run by more than 95 per cent of the full nodes, which makes it dominant but not unique (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A learner should treat Bitcoin Core as the most widely used model and not as the only possible one." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#ReferenceImplementation> .
chx:S_ImprovementProposal a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Bitcoin Improvement Proposal"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Bitcoin Improvement Proposal" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 5 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Verification ;
    skos:definition "A Bitcoin Improvement Proposal, abbreviated BIP, is a design document providing information to the Bitcoin community or describing a new feature for Bitcoin or its processes or environment, in the words of the process document that governed BIPs from 2016 (Dashjr, 2016). The chapter explains that most major parts of the system since 2011 have been documented in a set of BIPs and that the book refers to them by number: BIP9, for example, describes a mechanism used for several major upgrades (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Chapter 2 already used the term for the BIP that defines the payment URI, and this chapter shows where such documents sit among the other sources of the system.\n\nThe documents make the design of a decentralized system discussable. The process document says that BIPs are intended as the primary mechanisms for proposing new features, for collecting community input on an issue, and for documenting the design decisions that have gone into Bitcoin, and that the author is responsible for building consensus within the community and documenting dissenting opinions (Dashjr, 2016). Because the documents are text files in a versioned repository, their revision history is the historical record of the proposal. Without such a record, a rule would exist only as a line in somebody's code.\n\nA reader meets BIPs in three places. The book cites them by number in its text and lists the ones that Bitcoin Core has implemented in an appendix (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Bitcoin Core keeps a list in its own documentation that names, for each BIP, the version since which it is implemented; for BIP 9 the list says that multiple soft forks can be deployed in parallel since version 0.12.1 (Bitcoin Core, 2026). And the BIPs repository itself holds the documents, and the process is now described by BIP 3, assigned in 2025, which replaces BIP 2 with a streamlined process (Murch, 2025).\n\nA proposal begins with an idea that a champion discusses publicly, usually on the Bitcoin development mailing list; a draft is then submitted to the BIPs git repository as a pull request, and a BIP editor assigns the number, because authors must not assign numbers to themselves (Dashjr, 2016). Under BIP 3 each document has a preamble of headers, among them the status, which is one of Draft, Complete, Deployed or Closed, and the type, which is Specification, Informational or Process; BIP 9, for example, carries the status Deployed (Murch, 2025) (Wuille et al., 2015). A specification document should be detailed enough to enable any Bitcoin project to create an interoperable implementation (Murch, 2025).\n\nA BIP is a recommendation, not a decree. BIP 3 says in so many words that BIPs do not define what Bitcoin is: individual BIPs do not represent community consensus or a general recommendation for implementation, some may never be adopted, and no formal or informal decision body governs Bitcoin development or decides adoption (Murch, 2025). A document with the status Deployed has been implemented and activated; a document that exists in the repository has only been published. When the course cites a BIP, it cites it for the description of a mechanism, and the number is the stable name by which any reader can find the same text." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#ImprovementProposal> .
chx:S_SoftFork a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Soft fork"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Soft fork" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 6 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Verification ;
    skos:definition "A soft fork is a change to the consensus rules that is backward compatible. The book defines the property precisely: full nodes implementing the change must not accept any blocks that full nodes without the change would consider invalid, so newer nodes may reject blocks that older nodes would accept, which allows them to enforce new rules, but never the other way round (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A change that does not have this property would be a hard fork, which the book describes as a change to the consensus rules that is not compatible with older full nodes. Segregated witness, discussed in the transaction group, was introduced as a soft fork (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe advantage is that the network can be upgraded without splitting. Because older nodes still accept everything that newer nodes accept, an upgrade does not force every participant to move on the same day. The cost is coordination: the miners who produce the blocks and the nodes that enforce the rules have to agree when the new rule applies. The earlier methods of signalling support in the block version, as BIP 90 recounts, used thresholds of 75 and 95 per cent of the last 1000 blocks (Daftuar, 2016), and they allowed only one change to be rolled out at a time (Wuille et al., 2015).\n\nThe chapter mentions the mechanism by name when it introduces BIPs: BIP9 describes a mechanism used for several major upgrades (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Bitcoin Core's own list of implemented BIPs says that the changes allowing multiple soft forks to be deployed in parallel have been implemented since version 0.12.1, and that some deployments have since been replaced by fixed activation heights, which BIP 90 calls burying (Bitcoin Core, 2026) (Daftuar, 2016). The block explorer output of chapter 3 shows the field involved, the block version, with the value 1 for the old block 123456.\n\nBIP 9 reinterprets the version field of a block as a bit vector in which each bit can track an independent change. Each deployment has a name, a bit chosen from 0 to 28, a start time and a timeout, both measured in median time past; its state moves from DEFINED to STARTED, then to LOCKED_IN once enough blocks of one retarget period signal the bit, and finally to ACTIVE, or to FAILED if the timeout passes first (Wuille et al., 2015). Blocks that signal have the top three bits of the version set to 001, so their version lies between 0x20000000 and 0x3FFFFFFF; shifting a signalling version such as 0x20000002 right by 29 bits leaves the value 1, which is the binary 001 (Wuille et al., 2015).\n\nA soft fork is backward compatible only for nodes that do not need to understand the new rule. Older nodes keep accepting blocks, but they no longer verify the new rule themselves, so a user who wants the full protection has to upgrade. The mechanism of signalling is also not a vote of the users: BIP 9 tallies the blocks that miners produce, and the nodes that enforce the rule decide which chain they will accept. The details of how a rule change is chosen and activated belong to the later chapters on mining and on the network." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#SoftFork> .
chx:S_Project a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "The Bitcoin Core project"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The Bitcoin Core project" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 7 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Node ;
    skos:definition "The project group describes where Bitcoin Core comes from and who maintains it. It contains two concepts: open-source software, which is the legal and social form in which the program is published, and Satoshi Nakamoto, the name under which the system was first described and its first software written. Both belong to the first section of the chapter, From Bitcoin to Bitcoin Core, which explains why the program is called what it is (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA student who meets the term full node soon asks who decides what the node software does. The answer given by the chapter is that nobody owns it in the ordinary sense. The source code is available under an open license, it is developed by an open community of volunteers, and it began as the work of a single person. Knowing this explains why the chapter can invite the reader to download the code, build it and even contribute to it, and why the book can treat the program as a public reference and not as a product.\n\nThe group first states the form of publication, with the license that allows anyone to use, change and pass on the code, and the community process by which changes are reviewed and released. It then turns to the origin: the paper of 2008, the software that preceded it, and the withdrawal of its author from public life in 2011, after which the volunteers took over. A learner who has read both sections can explain why the identity of the creator does not matter for the operation of the system (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Project> .
chx:S_OpenSourceSoftware a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Open-source software"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Open-source software" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 8 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Project ;
    skos:definition "Open-source software is software whose source code is available to everyone under a license that allows use, modification and redistribution. The Open Source Initiative, whose definition is widely used, says that open source does not just mean access to the source code: the distribution terms must also allow free redistribution, must include the source code, and must allow modifications and derived works (Open Source Initiative, 2026). The book describes Bitcoin in these terms: it is an open-source project whose source code is available under an open license, the MIT license, free to download and use for any purpose (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nOpenness is what makes it reasonable to trust a program that guards money without trusting its authors. Anyone can read the code, compile it and test it, and the project's own readme asks for exactly that: testing and code review are the bottleneck of development, and because this is a security-critical project, a mistake might cost people lots of money, so changes should be tested by somebody other than the developer who wrote them (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The book also draws a social consequence. Bitcoin is developed by an open community of volunteers, which by 2023 had more than 1,000 contributors, about a dozen developers working on the code almost full time and several dozen more part time, and it adds that anyone can contribute, including the reader (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe source files of Bitcoin Core that were read for this chapter state their terms in the header, with the sentence that the file is distributed under the MIT software license, and the readme repeats that Bitcoin Core is released under the terms of the MIT license (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The license text itself is short. It grants permission, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of the software to deal in it without restriction, including to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense and sell copies, on the conditions that the copyright notice is included and that the software is provided as is, without warranty of any kind (Open Source Initiative, 2026).\n\nThe project's development process shows how the open model works in practice. The master branch is regularly built and tested, but it is not guaranteed to be completely stable; official stable versions are marked by tags created from release branches, a mechanism that the source group of the build branch explains. Developers are strongly encouraged to write unit tests for new code, regression and integration tests written in Python exist as well, and continuous integration systems check that every pull request is tested on Windows, Linux and macOS before it is merged (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nOpen source does not mean that the code has no defects, or that no one is in charge of releases. The license says plainly that the software is provided as is, and the readme warns that the master branch is not guaranteed to be stable (Open Source Initiative, 2026) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Openness of the source also does not by itself prove that a downloaded program was built from that source. For that reason the ecosystem branch of this chapter explains how a release is checked by its checksums and signatures and why some builds are made reproducible." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#OpenSourceSoftware> .
chx:S_SatoshiNakamoto a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Satoshi Nakamoto"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Satoshi Nakamoto" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 9 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Project ;
    skos:definition "Satoshi Nakamoto is the name used by the person or people who designed Bitcoin and created its original reference implementation. The glossary of the book adds that, as part of that implementation, they also devised the first blockchain database and were the first to solve the double-spending problem for digital currency, and that their real identity remains unknown (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter on the history of Bitcoin says that the system was first described in 2008 in a paper written under the alias of Satoshi Nakamoto (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The name is therefore a pseudonym, and it must not be confused with the unit satoshi of chapter 2, which was named after it.\n\nThe paper is the starting point of the technology. Its abstract proposes a solution to the double-spending problem using a peer-to-peer network, in which the network timestamps transactions by hashing them into an ongoing chain of hash-based proof of work, forming a record that cannot be changed without redoing the proof of work (Nakamoto, 2008). The chapter adds a fact about the order of events: the software was mostly completed before the whitepaper was published, because Nakamoto wanted to make sure that the implementation worked before publishing a paper about it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe name appears in several places. The paper is reproduced in an appendix of the book and is available at bitcoin.org (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Nakamoto, 2008). The first block of the blockchain carries the Unix time 1231006505, which the worked example converts to the third of January 2009; a fresh node of release 31.1, in the evidence files of this chapter, reports exactly that number as the time of its only block (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Nakamoto's name is also in the subversion string of the node: the book's output of the network information command shows /Satoshi:24.0.1/, and the book explains that more than 95 per cent of full nodes run versions of Bitcoin Core, which is identified as Satoshi in this string (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe chapter on the history of Bitcoin explains what Nakamoto combined: several prior inventions, such as digital signatures and Hashcash, into a completely decentralized electronic cash system that does not rely on a central authority for currency issuance or for the settlement and validation of transactions. A key innovation was to use a distributed computation system, the proof-of-work algorithm, to conduct a global lottery about every 10 minutes, which lets the decentralized network arrive at consensus about the state of transactions (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The Bitcoin network started in 2009 with a reference implementation published by Nakamoto and since revised by many other programmers (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nTwo points should not be overstated. The first is authority: the book states that Nakamoto withdrew from the public in April 2011, leaving the development of the code and the network to a group of volunteers, and that neither Nakamoto nor anyone else exerts individual control over the system, which operates on transparent mathematical principles, open-source code and consensus among participants (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The second is identity: nothing in the sources of this course identifies the person or people behind the name, and a claim about the identity would need evidence that this course cannot give." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#SatoshiNakamoto> .
chx:S_NodeResources a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Node resources"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Node resources" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 10 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Node ;
    skos:definition "Node resources are the things that a node consumes in order to do its work: time and bandwidth to download the chain, disk space to store it, memory to keep the data it needs often, and network connections to other nodes. The section Running a Bitcoin Core Node of the chapter lists the costs openly before it explains why a reader might still want to bear them, and this group collects the concepts needed to understand those costs (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe costs decide who can run a node, and therefore how widely the verification of the rules is spread. The book's figures for 2023 are over 500 GB of data to download and process at first, about 400 MB of transactions per day, at least 1 TB of disk if all transactions are indexed and the full copy is kept for several years, and upload bandwidth that a metered connection may not afford (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The current download page of Bitcoin Core states a one-time download of about 600 GB plus a further 5 to 10 GB per month, and says that with pruning a node can store as little as 10 GB (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Every option that lowers these costs, such as pruning, is a trade between cost and what the node can offer to others.\n\nFour concepts make up the group. The initial download is the first synchronization with the network. A pruned node keeps only the recent blocks. A peer is another node with which a node exchanges blocks and transactions, and the set of unspent outputs is the compact record of what can still be spent, which the node keeps up to date as it verifies. The figures in the group are orders of magnitude that change from year to year, so the course reads them against the date of their source and not as constants." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#NodeResources> .
chx:S_InitialDownload a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Initial download"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Initial download" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 11 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_NodeResources ;
    skos:definition "The initial download is the first synchronization of a new node with the network, in which it fetches the whole chain of blocks from its peers and validates every transaction in them. The chapter's tip says that Bitcoin Core keeps a full copy of the blockchain by default, with nearly every transaction confirmed since the start of the network in 2009, and that the node will not be able to process transactions or update account balances until the full dataset has been downloaded (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The status data of a node has a field for this phase: the flag initialblockdownload tells whether the node is still in it, and the explanation in Bitcoin Core's source calls it a debug-information estimate (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe download is what turns the claim that a node verifies everything into a fact about a particular computer. Until the node has processed every block, it cannot know the current set of unspent outputs, so it cannot tell whether a payment is valid. The cost is substantial, and the book says it will take some time, perhaps more than a day, for the program to catch up to the current height, with almost a billion transactions to validate at the time of writing (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). That is the reason the chapter asks readers to make sure they have enough disk space, bandwidth and time before they begin.\n\nA reader meets the phase in the output of the status command. The book's example of a node that has just been started shows a chain of 0 blocks and 83,999 headers, and explains that the node first fetches the block headers from its peers to find the chain with the most proof of work and afterwards continues to download the full blocks, validating them as it goes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The same data from a fresh node of the current release, in the evidence files of this chapter, shows 0 blocks, 0 headers and a verification progress near zero (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe same arithmetic applies after any interruption. Bitcoin Core must fetch whatever it missed while it was off: at the book's rate of about 400 MB a day, closing the program for ten days means 400 * 10 = 4000 MB, which is the 4 GB the book names (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The rate itself is not constant. The book's figure corresponds to about 12 GB a month, whereas the current download page speaks of 5 to 10 GB per month, and the bitcoin.org page of about 20 GB a month plus about 740 GB for the first start (Bitcoin Core, 2026) (Bitcoin.org, 2026). The numbers differ because they were measured at different times and under different definitions.\n\nThe download is only the first of two jobs: the node must also validate what it fetches, and on slow hardware validation, not bandwidth, can be the bottleneck. Bitcoin Core's own documentation warns that when the system starts swapping memory pages to disk continuously, a node can slow to a crawl, especially during the initial synchronization, and that a lower database cache makes the initial synchronization much longer (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A learner should therefore treat the figures in the book as a snapshot of 2023 and not as a promise, and should check the current ones on the download page before planning a node." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#InitialDownload> .
chx:S_PrunedNode a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Pruned node"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Pruned node" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 12 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_NodeResources ;
    skos:definition "A pruned node is a full node that deletes old block files after it has verified them, so that it needs far less disk space. The chapter describes the option prune as a way to reduce the disk requirements of the blockchain to a given number of megabytes by deleting old blocks, for use on a resource-constrained node, and it stresses that other parts of the system use disk space that cannot currently be pruned, so the minimum space of the data directory is still needed (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The tip of the same section adds that Bitcoin Core can be configured to discard old blocks but still downloads the entire dataset (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nPruning allows a verifying node on a small machine. The current download page says that with pruning enabled a node can store as little as 10 GB in total without sacrificing any security (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The reason is that verification needs the current state of the chain and a recent stretch of blocks, not every old block: the node checked the old blocks when it first received them and keeps the compact record of unspent outputs, which is the basis for checking new ones. The price is that the node can no longer hand old blocks to other nodes.\n\nThe option is given as -prune=<n> on the command line or prune=<n> in the configuration file. In the book's sample for a resource-constrained node it is prune=5000. Bitcoin Core's help text defines the values: 0 disables pruning, 1 allows manual pruning through a remote procedure call, and any number of at least 550 means that block files are deleted automatically to keep under that target size in MiB (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The constant behind the 550 is MIN_DISK_SPACE_FOR_BLOCK_FILES in the source, and the constant MIN_BLOCKS_TO_KEEP, 288, protects the files that hold the most recent blocks (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nBitcoin Core stores blocks in numbered files of 128 MiB each and deletes whole files until the stored blocks fit under the target, never touching the files that hold the 288 most recent blocks; the block index and the chain state are separate databases and stay (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The node also tells other nodes what it can offer. An unpruned node sets the service bit NODE_NETWORK, which says that it can serve all historical blocks, whereas BIP 159 defines the bit NODE_NETWORK_LIMITED, bit 10, for pruned peers, which must be capable of serving at least the last 288 blocks, about two days of blocks (Bitcoin Core, 2026) (Schnelli, 2017). Because the index of all transactions needs the old blocks, the program refuses to start when both options are given and prints the message that prune mode is incompatible with -txindex (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nPruning cannot be undone cheaply. The help text warns that reverting the setting requires re-downloading the entire blockchain, and that wallets and indexes should be kept active while pruning is enabled so they stay synchronized before old block data is deleted (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A pruned node also cannot look up an old transaction or block that it has deleted, so the tools of this chapter, such as retrieving an arbitrary transaction by its identifier, work only for data that is still stored or that is part of the wallet. For a learner the safe rule is that pruning saves disk space and nothing else: it neither weakens verification nor makes the node faster." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#PrunedNode> .
chx:S_Peer a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Peer and connection"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Peer and connection" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 13 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_NodeResources ;
    skos:definition "A peer is another node of the Bitcoin network to which a node keeps a connection in order to exchange blocks and transactions. The chapter introduces the word when it says that, by default, Bitcoin nodes transmit transactions and blocks to other nodes, called peers, which consumes upload bandwidth (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The network information of a node counts the connections: the book's example shows 10 connections, none incoming and 10 outgoing, and the keys connections_in and connections_out give the two numbers (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nPeers are how a node learns about the world. It has no central server to ask, so every block and every unconfirmed transaction reaches it from a peer, and every block that it has verified is passed on in the same way. Chapter 2 described this spreading of data as gossiping. For the operator, peers are also a cost, since each active connection takes some memory and bandwidth, and a node on a metered connection may want to limit how much it uploads (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe start-up log of a node states the limit it works with: the book's log says Using at most 125 automatic connections, and the same line appears in the log of release 31.1 in the evidence files, whereas the source tree consulted for this chapter defines the default as 200 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Bitcoin Core's guide to reducing memory explains the structure: if inbound connections are enabled, the option maxconnections sets the maximum; otherwise the number of connections is not more than 11, of which 8 are full-relay connections, 2 are block-relay-only connections, and occasionally 1 is a short-lived feeler connection (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA node finds peers from a stored list: the log of the book says that it loaded 63866 addresses from the file peers.dat, and the data directory of a node holds that file next to a list of banned addresses (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). It then opens outgoing connections to some of them and accepts incoming ones, if it is listening. Connections added by hand with the addnode option have a separate limit of 8 and do not count against the maximum (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Eight full-relay and two block-relay-only connections make 8 + 2 = 10 regular outgoing connections, and the 11th is the feeler or an extra block-relay-only connection.\n\nA peer is not a trusted party. Everything it sends is checked by the receiving node against the rules, which is why a node can connect to strangers at all. The number of connections is also not a measure of quality: the node in the evidence files, started with its connections disabled, reports connections 0 and still holds a correct chain of one block (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Finally, the value of the default maximum differs between the release the book printed, release 31.1 and the newest source tree, so a configuration that depends on it should state the number explicitly." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Peer> .
chx:S_UtxoSet a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Set of unspent outputs"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Set of unspent outputs" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 14 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_NodeResources ;
    skos:definition "The set of unspent outputs, in the chapter often called the UTXO set, is the collection of all transaction outputs that have been created and not yet spent. Chapter 2 explained the unspent transaction output as the unit that a payment spends, and Bitcoin Core's documentation describes the database in which it keeps them, called the chain state, as a compact representation of all currently unspent transaction outputs and metadata about the transactions they are from (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The chapter refers to it when it describes the option dbcache as the size of the UTXO cache (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe set is what makes verification fast. To decide whether a new transaction is valid, a node does not need to search the whole history: it needs to know whether each output being spent is in the set. The chapter's checklist for transactions says that the outputs being spent must match outputs in the memory pool or unspent outputs in a block of the main branch (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The set is much smaller than the chain of blocks, because every spent output has left it.\n\nA reader meets it in three places: as a directory called chainstate in the data directory of the node, as the thing that the dbcache option keeps in memory, and as the thing that a reorganization changes. The evidence files of a fresh node of release 31.1 list the entry chainstate next to blocks, and Bitcoin Core's file documentation names it as a LevelDB database (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The book explains that when a node switches to a chain with more work it reorganizes its view of confirmed transactions and available UTXOs (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nEach block changes the set in the same way as the transaction of the example: the outputs that its transactions spend leave, and the new outputs enter. Alice's transaction spent one output of an earlier transaction and created two, the payment to Bob and the change, so verifying it removes one entry and adds two. The worked example shows the operation on a set of outpoint names, each written as a transaction and an output number: removing the spent outpoint 4ac5:1 and adding 4662:0 and 4662:1 leaves the unrelated entry and two new ones.\n\nThe set is derived data, not part of any block, and every node builds it for itself by verifying the chain; two honest nodes get the same set only because they apply the same rules. It is also not a list of balances: the set holds outputs, and the amount a wallet owns is the sum of the outputs it can spend. Finally, the size of the set affects the memory a node needs, which is why the chapter lists the database cache among the options that a resource-constrained node should lower." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#UtxoSet> .
chx:S_Build a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Build"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Build" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 15 ;
    skos:definition "The build branch is about turning the text of Bitcoin Core into a program that can run. The book calls this part of the chapter the Bitcoin development environment: the tools, libraries and support software a developer needs for writing Bitcoin applications, which the chapter walks through step by step. It announces that the chapter is highly technical and that a reader who is not actually setting up a development environment may skip to the next chapter, which is less technical (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The branch follows the sections of the book: obtaining the source code, selecting a release, configuring the build and building the executables.\n\nBuilding from source is the most direct way to know what a node is made of. The source code of Bitcoin Core is public, so a reader who builds it has a program that corresponds to a particular version of the text that anyone can inspect, and can switch features on or off during the build, for example by leaving out the wallet. For a course on software engineering the branch also shows how a large, long-lived project organizes its work: a version control system, numbered releases, a build system that discovers what a computer provides, and an automatic test suite (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe steps appear in the project's own documentation, which has separate build notes for each platform: the book names Android, FreeBSD, NetBSD, OpenBSD, macOS, Unix and Windows, and the current tree has files such as doc/build-unix.md for the platform that the chapter uses (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Most users never build the program, because the project publishes ready-made releases for download, and the ecosystem branch explains how such a download is verified. The branch is nevertheless the reason the course can say what the executables are, where they come from and which options shaped them.\n\nA build moves through four stages. First the source code is obtained and a particular release is selected. Then the build is configured, which means that the build system looks at the computer, checks that the needed libraries are present and records the chosen options. Next the compiler translates the source files into executables, and the project's tests are run to check them. Finally the executables are installed in a directory where the operating system can find them. The book describes these stages for the Autotools build system that Bitcoin Core used at the time of writing; the current project uses CMake with the same stages.\n\nThe most important caution is that the commands of the printed chapter belong to a build system that has been replaced: release 29.0 migrated the build from Autotools to CMake, and the current tree has a top-level CMakeLists.txt and no autogen.sh (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A student who follows the book literally on a current source tree fails at the first command. The second caution is that compiling it yourself does not prove that the program is trustworthy; it shows only that a program was built from the text you chose, and the choice of that text is made by selecting a release tag." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Build> .
chx:S_Source a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Source"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Source" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 16 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Build ;
    skos:definition "The source group contains the concepts needed to obtain the right text of the program: the source code itself, the Git repository in which the project keeps it, the release tag that names a particular stable version, and the release candidate that precedes it. The book teaches these in its sections on compiling Bitcoin Core from the source code and on selecting a release (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe program changes every day, and not every day's version is safe to run. The book explains that by default the local copy of the repository is synchronized with the most recent code, which might be an unstable or beta version of Bitcoin (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The concepts of the group give a learner the means to say exactly which version is meant: a repository holds every version, a tag names one of them, and a suffix tells whether it is meant for testing.\n\nThe sections of the group follow the order of the commands in the chapter. The source code is what the project publishes; the repository is how it is downloaded, with the Git command that clones it; the release tag is how one version is chosen with the Git commands that list tags and check one out; and the release candidate convention explains which tags to avoid on a production system. A learner who has read them can say what the command git checkout v24.0.1 does and why the book recommends it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Source> .
chx:S_ReleaseTag a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Release tag"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Release tag" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 17 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Source ;
    skos:definition "A release tag is a name that the developers attach to one specific snapshot of the code repository in order to mark a release by its version number (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The documentation of Git describes the command that creates such names: git tag adds a tag reference, and annotated tag objects are meant for releases, while lightweight tags are meant for private or temporary object labels (Git project, 2026). In Bitcoin Core a tag has the form v24.0.1, a letter v followed by the major, minor and revision numbers.\n\nTags solve the problem of the moving target. The project's readme explains that the master branch is regularly built and tested but is not guaranteed to be completely stable, and that tags are created regularly from release branches to indicate new official, stable release versions (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A tag therefore lets two people on different continents talk about exactly the same code, and a book can say which version its screenshots belong to.\n\nThe chapter shows the three commands in sequence. The command git tag lists all tags, from v0.1.5 and v0.1.6test1 through v0.10.0 and v0.11.2 to the release candidates of version 0.12; the command git checkout v24.0.1 synchronizes the local copy with the highest release at the time of writing; and git status confirms that HEAD is detached at v24.0.1 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The list of tags of the public repository, read for this chapter on 2026-10-02, now ends with v31.1 for the newest stable release and v32.0rc3 for the next candidate (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe tag names a commit, the repository's own identifier of a snapshot. After the checkout the book shows the message that HEAD is now at b3f866a8d, a shortened commit identifier with the text of the merge that completed version 24.0.1 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The version number in the tag is the same number that the running program reports: the program computes its client version as 10000 times the major number plus 100 times the minor number plus the build number, and for v24.0.1 that is 240001, which is the value of the field version in the book's network information (Bitcoin Core, 2026) (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA tag is a label, not a guarantee. Checking out a tag gives the code that the maintainers tagged, but it does not by itself show that the label was placed by them, and the book's state message warns that the user is now in a detached HEAD state, where changes belong to no branch (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The tag list also contains names that follow no release scheme, such as noversion and v0.1.6test1. Trust in a release comes from the signatures and the reproducible builds described in the ecosystem branch." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#ReleaseTag> .
chx:S_SourceCode a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Source code"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Source code" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 18 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Source ;
    skos:definition "Source code is the text in which a program is written, in a programming language that a person can read, before it is translated into the form that a computer executes. The book says that Bitcoin Core's source code can be downloaded as an archive or by cloning the source repository from GitHub (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The project's own documentation shows what the text consists of: the main program is written in C++, which the notes on the build call a language whose compilers are memory-hungry, and its regression and integration tests are written in Python (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe source is the only complete and exact description of the rules that the node enforces. The whitepaper describes several early parts of the system and the proposals describe later ones, but a question such as how much new money a block may create is answered finally by reading the code. The function GetBlockSubsidy in the file validation.cpp is a short example: it divides the block height by the halving interval, returns zero when the shift would be undefined, and otherwise starts from 50 coins and shifts the amount right by the number of halvings (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA reader meets source code in the repository, whose top level holds the build files, the documentation folder, the source folder and the test folder, and in the documentation that the book tells the reader to review before compiling: the main readme in the repository and the build notes for the platform in the doc directory (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The genesis block, which every node knows from the beginning, is encoded in the source file for chain parameters, and the book points to that file for anyone who wants to see it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nFrom the text to the program takes the steps of the build branch: a compiler translates each source file, a linker combines the results with libraries, and the build system orchestrates the order. A version of the source is identified by its place in the repository, either by a tag for a release or by a commit identifier for any other state. The block subsidy function can be checked by anyone: starting from 50 coins in the smallest unit, which is 5,000,000,000, a right shift by one per 210,000 blocks gives the amounts of the successive eras.\n\nReading the source requires care. The master branch holds work in progress, and a source file read at one commit can differ from the behavior of the release that a user runs, so a statement about the software should name the version it refers to. Also, a program that is open to reading is not thereby correct: the project's readme says that testing and review are the bottleneck and that every mistake might cost people lots of money, which is the reason the project asks for review by someone other than the author (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#SourceCode> .
chx:S_GitRepository a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Git repository"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Git repository" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 19 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Source ;
    skos:definition "Git is a distributed version control system, and a repository is the database in which it keeps a project and all of its history. The book calls Git the most widely used distributed version control system and an essential part of the toolkit of every software developer (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The Pro Git book explains the main idea: Git thinks of its data more like a series of snapshots of a miniature filesystem, taking a picture of what all the files look like each time a project is committed, and everything in Git is checksummed before it is stored and is then referred to by that checksum (Chacon and Straub, 2014).\n\nA repository gives a project a memory and a way to share it. Because every copy contains the whole history, anyone can work offline, compare any two versions and verify that a given version is the one that the maintainers named. The chapter uses exactly this: the command git clone makes a complete local copy of the repository from GitHub, after which the command git tag shows the available release names, and the book's example output counts 245,912 objects received, about 217.74 MiB of data (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe Bitcoin Core repository is hosted on GitHub, and the project's readme says that stable releases are tagged from release branches while the master branch is the integration tree (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The documentation of Git describes the clone command in a sentence: it clones a repository into a newly created directory, creates remote-tracking branches for each branch of the cloned repository, and creates and checks out an initial branch forked from the cloned repository's currently active branch (Git project, 2026). The command git tag creates, lists, deletes or verifies tags (Git project, 2026).\n\nGit names each snapshot, called a commit, by a hash computed from its content. The full name of the commit that the source tree consulted for this chapter is 05bc2f53ce0cb239c17dbdd6b261bd2db7d2a940, forty hexadecimal characters, and the book's output of the checkout command abbreviates its commit to nine characters, b3f866a8d. Chapter 2 explained hash functions and hexadecimal notation, and a commit identifier is an application of both: if the content changed in any way, the name would change as well. A tag is a second name for a commit, and a checkout makes the working files equal to that commit.\n\nTwo misunderstandings are common. A repository is not the same as a hosting service: GitHub stores a copy, and the clone on a user's machine is as complete as the original. And a clone is a snapshot of one moment, not a live view: the highest release at the time of writing was v24.0.1, whereas the public repository now holds the releases up to v31.1 and hundreds of tags in all, so a reader who cloned in 2023 does not see the later releases without updating the clone. The detached HEAD state that the book prints after checking out a tag is normal for reading a release and is a warning only for those who intend to commit changes." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#GitRepository> .
chx:S_ReleaseCandidate a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Release candidate"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Release candidate" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 20 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Source ;
    skos:definition "A release candidate is a version that the developers believe could become the next stable release, published so that it can be tested first. The book gives the naming convention in one sentence: by convention, release candidates, which are intended for testing, have the suffix rc, and stable releases that can be run on production systems have no suffix (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The tag v0.12.0rc2 is therefore the second candidate of version 0.12.0, and v0.12.0 itself would be the release.\n\nThe two-step method lets the project find problems before ordinary users depend on the software. For a program that guards money, the cost of a defect is high, and the project's readme stresses the need for testing by people other than the developers (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The book's rule for readers follows: when selecting a release for compilation, choose the highest version without a suffix, because that is the one meant for production (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe tag list of the book already shows candidates next to releases: v0.11.2 and v0.11.2rc1, and v0.12.0rc1 and v0.12.0rc2 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The list of tags of the public repository read for this chapter on 2026-10-02 contains many more; its newest entries at the time of reading were the stable v31.1 and the candidates v32.0rc1, v32.0rc2 and v32.0rc3, so a reader who checked out the very last tag in an alphabetical or chronological list would have received a candidate and not a release (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nCandidates are numbered in the order in which they are published: rc1, then rc2, rc3 and so on until a candidate is accepted and the same code is tagged without a suffix. The program itself knows whether it is a release: the start-up log of release 31.1 begins with the text Bitcoin Core version v31.1.0 (release build), and the source defines the client version from a major, minor and build number together with a flag that says whether it is a release (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A script can tell candidates from releases by the suffix alone, as the worked example does.\n\nThe suffix is a convention and not a guarantee, and not every unusual tag is a candidate: the first entries of the list include v0.1.6test1, which uses a different suffix. A candidate may also change before the final release, so results that a student obtains from a candidate need not hold for the release. The book itself recommends the plain tag, and a production system should follow that advice." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#ReleaseCandidate> .
chx:S_Toolchain a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Toolchain"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Toolchain" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 21 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Build ;
    skos:definition "The toolchain group gathers the programs and conventions that turn source code into running executables: the build system, which decides how, the compilation, which does the translation, the prerequisites, which must be present, and the executables, which are the result. The book describes the toolchain of its time, built on Autotools; the current project uses CMake; and release 30.0 added a new front door, the bitcoin command (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA build system exists because a large program is not compiled by one command. Bitcoin Core consists of many source files, depends on libraries that differ between computers, and offers options such as leaving out the wallet. The build system looks at the computer, records the choices, and runs the translation steps in the right order and, if interrupted, from where they stopped. Knowing the toolchain also explains why the same program can be obtained in several ways with the same result.\n\nThe six concepts of the group fall into two sets. The Autotools build and the CMake build are the two generations of build system, the first described by the book and the second in use since release 29.0. The compilation, the build prerequisites and the executables belong to every build: the translation step, the things to install first, and the files that come out. The bitcoin command stands for the way in which those files are started in the current release. A learner who knows the group can read the build notes of any version of the project." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Toolchain> .
chx:S_AutotoolsBuild a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Autotools build"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Autotools build" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 22 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Toolchain ;
    skos:definition "Autotools is the family of tools with which Bitcoin Core was built until release 29.0, and the build of the book follows its three typical commands. The first, the script autogen.sh, creates a set of automatic configuration scripts that interrogate the system to discover the correct settings and make sure that all the necessary libraries are present. The second, configure, applies them and creates a customized build script for the computer. The third, make, runs the build (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe method was designed to make a program portable between operating systems without hand-editing. The script configure offers options that override the defaults, and the book lists four: --prefix, which overrides the installation location /usr/local, for example with $HOME on a computer where only the home directory is writable; --disable-wallet, which disables the reference wallet; --with-incompatible-bdb, which allows an incompatible version of the Berkeley DB library when building a wallet; and --with-gui=no, which leaves out the graphical interface that needs the Qt library (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe commands in the chapter are ./autogen.sh, ./configure --help, ./configure, make, make check and sudo make install, in this order, and the book shows output such as the lines beginning CXX that name the object file produced for each source file (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Readers meet the same tool in many older projects, and the release notes of Bitcoin Core 29.0 name the change that ended its use here: the build system has been migrated from Autotools to CMake (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe configuration step creates the build scripts only if the computer passes the checks. The book says that if any library is missing or incompatible, configure terminates with an error instead of creating the build scripts, and that after installing the missing prerequisite one runs it again (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The following make step may take up to an hour depending on the speed of the processor and the available memory, and if it is interrupted it can be resumed by typing make again; the option -j followed by a number sets the number of parallel compile jobs (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe commands of this section no longer work on the current source. The top level of the current tree has no autogen.sh and no configure script, and the option --with-incompatible-bdb has lost its purpose since legacy BerkeleyDB wallets can no longer be created or loaded (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A learner should therefore read the Autotools section as an explanation of the structure of a build, with configure, compile, test and install, and use the CMake commands for any real work." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#AutotoolsBuild> .
chx:S_CMakeBuild a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "CMake build"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "CMake build" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 23 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Toolchain ;
    skos:definition "CMake is the build system that Bitcoin Core has used since release 29.0. The manual of CMake explains its role: a buildsystem describes how to build a project's executables and libraries from its source code using a build tool to automate the process, and CMake lets a project describe its buildsystem abstractly in files written in the CMake language, from which it generates a preferred buildsystem for each user (Kitware, 2026). The release notes of version 29.0 state the change plainly, and the build notes of the project give the three commands: cmake -B build, then cmake --build build, then optionally cmake --install build (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nMoving the project to CMake replaced a collection of generated scripts by a description that works on the platforms the project supports. The release notes list what a user of the old method must change: the minimum required CMake version is 3.22, options are now given as CMake variables, and some defaults changed. For example, one now needs -DWITH_ZMQ=ON to build with the ZeroMQ messaging library and -DBUILD_GUI=ON to build the graphical client bitcoin-qt (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA reader meets the commands in the file doc/build-unix.md, which also says that the command cmake -B build -LH shows the full list of available options and that the build command accepts -j N for N parallel jobs (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The book's options map onto the new ones: --disable-wallet corresponds to -DENABLE_WALLET=OFF, which also removes the dependency on SQLite, and the book's --with-gui=no describes what is now the default, because the graphical client is built only when asked for (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The CMake manual lists an --install-prefix option for choosing the installation directory (Kitware, 2026).\n\nThe build is divided into configuration and construction. The first command creates a build tree, the directory named after the -B option, in which CMake writes its cache with the chosen options. The second command builds the targets in that directory with whatever native tool is behind it. The release notes add that the default build type for single-configuration generators is RelWithDebInfo, in which the optimization flag -O2 is used instead of -O3, which has not been extensively tested with Bitcoin Core (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Building only the node is possible with cmake --build build --target bitcoind (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nCopying options from a book or a blog is the usual source of failure. The names are specific to the build system and the version: an option written for Autotools is silently useless to CMake, and the build notes warn that the memory requirement is at least 1.5 GB for compiling, with advice on reducing it for smaller machines (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Environment variables that the old build honored, such as CXXFLAGS, have corresponding CMake variables with other names, so a build that is configured in a script needs to be rewritten, not merely adapted (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#CMakeBuild> .
chx:S_BitcoinCommand a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The bitcoin command"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The bitcoin command" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 24 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Toolchain ;
    skos:definition "The bitcoin command is a single program, introduced with release 30.0, through which the node and the other tools of Bitcoin Core are started. The installed-files table of the project describes it as the command-line tool for interacting with Bitcoin, which calls the other binaries (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The help text of the program lists its commands: node starts the node, equivalent to running bitcoind; rpc calls an RPC method, equivalent to bitcoin-cli -named; wallet and tx run the wallet tool and the transaction tool; and gui starts the graphical client (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe command exists to hide a change in the structure of the software. The project plans to split Bitcoin Core into separate processes for the node, the wallet and the graphical interface, so that each can run in its own address space for better isolation. A front door that chooses the binary spares users from learning new names for each stage of that change (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nIn the book the user types bitcoind and bitcoin-cli. Those names still work in release 31.1, whose archive holds them in the folder bin together with bitcoin, bitcoin-qt, bitcoin-tx, bitcoin-util and bitcoin-wallet, and holds bitcoin-node, bitcoin-gui and test_bitcoin in a folder named libexec (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The release notes of 30.0 add that the new command makes an experimental interface for mining software available: when the node is started with bitcoin -m node -ipcbind=unix, it listens on a Unix socket for client connections (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe option -m, also written --multiprocess, makes the command run the multiprocess binaries bitcoin-node and bitcoin-gui, instead of the monolithic binaries bitcoind and bitcoin-qt, which are the default behavior. The multiprocess documentation says the multiprocess binaries currently function the same as the monolithic ones except that they support an -ipcbind option, and that they should be started through the bitcoin command and not directly, because they may change or be renamed and are not installed in the path (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA beginner can confuse the new command with the old name. The word bitcoin by itself starts no node; it needs a command such as node. The multiprocess mode is experimental and, in the words of the release notes, the option -m is currently required for the mining interface but will become optional (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The chapter's examples are written with the older names and are still valid for the monolithic binaries." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#BitcoinCommand> .
chx:S_Compilation a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Compilation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Compilation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 25 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Toolchain ;
    skos:definition "Compilation is the translation of source code into executable machine code by a program called a compiler. The CMake manual defines the neighbouring notion of the buildsystem as the description of how to build a project's executables and libraries from its source code (Kitware, 2026). In the chapter, the step is the one the reader starts by typing make, whose output is a long series of lines with the word CXX followed by names of the form bitcoind-bitcoind.o, one for each source file that is compiled (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe step takes a long time and a lot of memory because the program is large. The book says that the compilation can take up to an hour depending on the speed of the processor and the available memory, and the build notes of the project recommend at least 1.5 GB of memory, with advice on reducing the demand, because C++ compilers are memory-hungry (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). These facts matter to an operator who plans a build on a small computer and to a student who wonders why the step cannot be skipped.\n\nThe reader meets compilation after configuring the build. With the Autotools method the commands were make, then make check, then sudo make install; with CMake they are cmake --build build and cmake --install build, and the project's tests are run with ctest (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The unit tests, which the book asks the reader to run to ensure that the linked libraries are not broken in obvious ways, are run in the current tree with ctest, and the regression and integration tests of the project are written in Python (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe work is spread over many small jobs. Each source file is compiled independently into an object file, and a final step links the object files and the libraries into an executable. For that reason the process can be interrupted and resumed, and the book notes that if an error occurs or the compilation is interrupted, it can be resumed any time by typing make again; on a computer with several processor cores the number of parallel jobs can be raised, as in make -j 2 or the option -j N of the CMake build command (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA successful compilation shows that the program was built, not that it behaves correctly, which is why the book tells the reader to run the unit test suite before installing (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Installing needs administrative privileges in the book's example, because the default installation location is /usr/local/bin; the book mentions installing to the home directory as the alternative where the computer does not allow it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Compilation> .
chx:S_BuildPrerequisite a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Build prerequisite"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Build prerequisite" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 26 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Toolchain ;
    skos:definition "A build prerequisite is a tool or library that must be installed on the computer before the program can be compiled. The book tells the reader to review the prerequisites, which it describes as the libraries that must be present on the system before the build can begin, in the first part of the build documentation, and adds that if they are missing the build process will fail with an error (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A library, in this sense, is a collection of ready-made code that the program uses instead of containing it.\n\nA large program is not written from nothing. Bitcoin Core relies on libraries for tasks such as storing the wallet in a database, drawing the graphical interface or sending messages to other programs, and each library has to be present in a version that fits. The documentation of dependencies exists so that a builder can see in advance what to install, and the book says that the build can be resumed from where it stopped after installing a missing prerequisite (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nIn the current build notes, a table lists the required packages for each family of operating systems. For Debian and Ubuntu the required build dependencies are build-essential, cmake, python3 and libboost-dev; the packages for additional features are optional: libsqlite3-dev for the wallet, Cap'n Proto for interprocess communication, ZeroMQ for messaging, and the Qt packages with a QR-code library for the graphical client (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The book's equivalent concerns the Berkeley DB library for the wallet, which the wallet no longer uses.\n\nPrerequisites are checked at configuration time. If a library is missing, the configuration command stops and says so; the user installs the library with the system's package manager and repeats the command. Optional features mean optional prerequisites: building without the wallet with -DENABLE_WALLET=OFF removes the need for SQLite, and building without the graphical interface, which is the default, removes the need for Qt (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe prerequisites differ by platform and by release, so a list copied from a text about another system or version is likely to be wrong. The security perspective is that every library in the build is also part of the program that guards money, which is the reason the project documents the dependencies and offers a self-contained mechanism for building them, the depends system mentioned in its build notes (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#BuildPrerequisite> .
chx:S_Executable a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Executable"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Executable" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 27 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Toolchain ;
    skos:definition "An executable is a file that the operating system can run as a program. The book's installation step copies bitcoind, bitcoin-cli and bitcoin-tx into /usr/local/bin, and the command which asks the system for the path of an executable and confirms the installation (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The project's documentation describes the full set that a build installs: bitcoind as the node and wallet daemon, bitcoin-cli as the tool for making node and wallet calls, bitcoin-qt as the graphical node and wallet, bitcoin-tx for creating and modifying transactions, bitcoin-wallet as the wallet tool, bitcoin-util for miscellaneous utilities, and bitcoin as the command that calls the others (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nEach executable has a separate purpose, so that a user can run the part that is needed. A server needs the node without a graphical interface; an operator who sends commands uses the client; a developer who builds a transaction offline uses the transaction tool. The book chooses to build the daemon, the server version, because the chapter is about a node running in the background (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The project's plan for the multiprocess design is that node, wallet and interface code run in separate address spaces for better isolation (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe release 31.1 archive that was downloaded and run for this chapter shows the layout: the folder bin holds bitcoin, bitcoin-cli, bitcoin-qt, bitcoin-tx, bitcoin-util, bitcoin-wallet and bitcoind, and the folder libexec holds bitcoin-gui, bitcoin-node and test_bitcoin (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The files of a source build depend on the options given: the italicized entries in the project's table, such as the library libbitcoinkernel and the benchmarking tool, are installed only when the relevant CMake options are enabled (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nSeveral executables share most of their code. The node and the wallet are in one program in the default, monolithic, arrangement, and the multiprocess binaries bitcoin-node and bitcoin-gui split the roles as the project moves toward separate processes (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The client bitcoin-cli does not contain the node: it connects to a running node over the network interface that the next branch of this chapter describes, and sends it commands.\n\nThe name of an executable is not an interface guarantee. Names and places have changed between versions: the unit-test program test_bitcoin was moved from the folder bin to libexec in release 30.0, and the project advises starting multiprocess binaries through the bitcoin command and not directly (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A script that relies on a path should therefore state the release for which it was written." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Executable> .
chx:S_PackageManager a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Package manager"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Package manager" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 28 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Toolchain ;
    skos:definition "A package manager is the program of an operating system that installs, updates and removes software, together with the libraries each program needs, from a collection of packages. The build notes of Bitcoin Core say that the builder can either build from self-compiled dependencies or install the dependencies from the distribution's package manager (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The worked example takes the first column of the table of those notes, which names the system and its package manager, and finds the four package managers for which commands are given: apt for Debian and Ubuntu, dnf for Fedora, apk for Alpine and pacman for Arch.\n\nInstalling the prerequisites by hand would mean finding, downloading and compiling each library. A package manager does this with one command and resolves which other libraries are needed. The table of the build notes gives, for each system, the packages for the required build dependencies and then the optional ones for the wallet, the interprocess communication, the messaging and the graphical client, so that a builder chooses the columns that match the features wanted.\n\nThe first column of the table in the build notes is the package manager, and the second lists, for apt, the packages build-essential, cmake, python3 and libboost-dev (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The same word appears in the documentation of the reproducible builds, where the project says it achieves bootstrappability by using Guix as a functional package manager (Bitcoin Core, 2026). In that setting the package manager does not only install the tools but also builds them from source in a controlled way, so that the toolchain is reproducible.\n\nA package manager keeps a list of packages with their versions and their dependencies, and installs a package by installing everything it depends on first. The packages are named differently on each system, which is why the build notes give a row for each: the library for the database of the wallet is libsqlite3-dev on Debian and sqlite-devel on Fedora. A package registry, as the ecosystem branch explains, plays a similar role for the libraries of a programming language.\n\nA package manager trusts the collection it installs from. The software the builder obtains is the software the maintainers of the distribution packaged, in the version they chose. For a security-critical program like a node, the project therefore also offers a way to build the dependencies from source, called depends in its notes (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#PackageManager> .
chx:S_Operation a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Operation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Operation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 29 ;
    skos:definition "Operation is the second large part of the chapter, and it is about what happens after the software has been built or downloaded: how a node is started, how it is configured, how its state is observed, and what kind of wallet comes with it. The book introduces it with the statement that the peer-to-peer network is composed of network nodes, run mostly by individuals and some of the businesses that provide Bitcoin services, and that those who run them have a direct and authoritative view of the blockchain, with a local copy of all the spendable bitcoins independently validated by their own system (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A branch about operation therefore describes the daily practice of that independence.\n\nThe branch matters because a node that is badly run gives little of what it promises. A node that is not given enough disk space cannot store the chain, a node whose cache is too small spends its time reading the disk, and a node whose settings nobody understands may expose services that were meant to stay private. The book lists the reasons for running a node in a short list: not relying on any third party to validate the transactions you receive, not disclosing to third parties which transactions belong to your wallet, having programmable access to the network and the blockchain, validating transactions according to the consensus rules in an application, and supporting the network (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Each of these reasons is only as good as the way the node is operated.\n\nA reader meets operation in the configuration file that sits in the data directory, in the messages that the program prints when it starts, and in the answers of the status commands that the chapter calls just after starting the node. The evidence collected for this version comes from a real run of the release 31.1 as an offline node, whose start-up messages, data directory and answers are quoted in the concepts of this branch (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The book's own example is a node that holds the whole chain, keeps an index of all transactions on a very large disk and sends an e-mail when an alert is raised, together with a second example for a small server.\n\nThe branch has three groups. The configuration group explains the file and the options that shape the node: where the file and the data directory are found, how the database cache and the mempool limit divide the memory, what the transaction index is for, what blocks-only mode saves and how the alert command works. The running group explains how the node is started in the background, what it writes to its log when it starts, and how two status commands show where it stands in the chain and in the network. The wallet group explains the wallet that Bitcoin Core carries, the older kind that can no longer be created, and the descriptions of scripts on which the newer kind is built.\n\nTwo cautions apply to the whole branch. The first is that many of the book's figures belong to the release it printed: the default cache size, the wallet database and the first lines of the log differ in the current release, and the concepts of this branch give both values. The second is the warning in Bitcoin Core's own documentation, which tells users never to make configuration changes that they do not understand and to be wary of accepting any configuration changes provided to them by another source, even if they believe that they do understand them (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A copied configuration file is a set of instructions to a program that holds money-related data." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Operation> .
chx:S_Configuration a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Configuration"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Configuration" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 30 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Operation ;
    skos:definition "Configuration is the group of concepts that describe how a user tells Bitcoin Core how to behave. The book says that the node looks for a configuration file in its data directory on every start, and that the program offers more than a hundred options that modify the behavior of the network node, the storage of the blockchain and many other aspects of its operation (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The group covers the file itself, the idea of an option and the order in which its sources are read, the directory that holds the data, and the options the book singles out as the most important.\n\nA node that runs on a default setting is usable, but the options are how it is adapted to the machine and to the purpose. The book's two sample files show the range: one is for a node that serves as the back end of a Bitcoin application and puts its data on a disk with a lot of room, the other is for a resource-constrained server and limits the cache, the mempool, the stored blocks and the traffic (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Knowing what each option does is what turns a copied example into a deliberate decision.\n\nThe options meet the reader in three forms: as lines of the configuration file, as parameters after the name of the program on the command line, and as entries in the help text, which the version 31.1 prints with 139 option entries, more than the hundred of the book (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The start-up messages of the node list the settings that it has actually read, so the log is the place where an option can be checked. The concepts that follow take the file, the option, the directory and then the individual settings.\n\nThe order of the concepts follows the order of the book. The file and its options come first because everything else is set there, followed by the data directory, the database cache, the transaction index, blocks-only mode, the mempool limit and the alert command. Four of them concern memory and disk, which are the resources the node consumes, one concerns bandwidth and one concerns a message to the owner. Each concept gives the book's value and the value that the current release shows." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Configuration> .
chx:S_ConfigurationFile a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Configuration file"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Configuration file" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 31 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Configuration ;
    skos:definition "The configuration file is a plain text file, named bitcoin.conf by default, in which the owner of a node writes the options that the program should use. Bitcoin Core's documentation describes its format: it consists of option=value entries, one per line, with the whitespace around them removed, an option is written without the leading hyphen that the command line needs, and a value is mandatory (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The book says that Bitcoin Core will look for a configuration file in its data directory on every start (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA file is the practical way to keep a node's settings, because a node is meant to run for months and to restart after every power failure or update. The same options could be typed after the program name every time, but a file records them in one place that can be read, copied, compared and kept under control. It also keeps the settings out of the way of the start-up scripts that the book mentions in the end of the section, which only have to start the program (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe book shows how to find the file: run bitcoind -printtoconsole in a terminal and look at the first lines of the output, one of which begins with Config file followed by the path (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The log of the 31.1 release in the evidence shows the same line, together with one line for each setting that was read from the file, which is how the settings of that offline node (no connections, no seed lookup) were recorded (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The documentation lists the default places for the three common systems and says that the file is not created by the software and must be created by the user (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe parser is simple. Blank lines are ignored, a comment starts with a number sign and runs to the end of the line, and a block with a name in square brackets, such as the one named main, holds options for one network only, which take precedence over options without a section (Bitcoin Core, 2026). An option can be negated with the prefix no, so that nofoo=1 turns off an option called foo, and a second file can be added with the option includeconf (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A small worked example splits the lines of a file at the first equals sign and gives a dictionary of option names and values, as the example shows for the data directory and the transaction index of the book's first sample.\n\nChanges to the file take effect only after the node has been restarted, as the documentation states, so editing the file of a running node changes nothing until then (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The file is not a command language, and an option that the program does not know is not an error but shows up as a warning in the log, so a mistyped name can go unnoticed if nobody reads it. A last caution is that the file may hold secrets, for example the hashed credential of the interface, and should be readable only by the user who runs the node, which the init documentation also recommends for the file and the data directory (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#ConfigurationFile> .
chx:S_ConfigurationOption a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Configuration option"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Configuration option" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 32 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Configuration ;
    skos:definition "A configuration option is a named setting of the program, written in the book and in the help text with a leading hyphen, such as the setting that sets the size of the database cache. Most options take a value, some are switches that are off unless they are set, and almost all of them can be negated with the prefix no, which in general sets a boolean or numeric option to zero and a text option to an empty string, with some exceptions that the documentation names (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The book lists eight of them as the most important and says that Bitcoin Core offers more than 100 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe options are the program's interface for its owner, and the order in which their sources are read decides which value is used. The documentation states that all command-line options except the help, version and conf options may be specified in the configuration file, that all file options except the one that includes another file may also be given on the command line, and that command-line options override values set in the file (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A script that starts the node can therefore change one value for one run without editing the file.\n\nA reader meets the options in the output of the help command of the program, which prints each option with its meaning and its default, such as the line for the database cache that gives a minimum of 4 and a default of 1024 in the current release (Bitcoin Core, 2026). They are also met in the log, where the start-up lines show whether a value came from the file or from the command line, and in the file named settings.json in the data directory, which holds values set at run time through the graphical client or the interface, and which the documentation ranks between the file and the command line (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe precedence can be shown with two dictionaries. The file says 450 for the cache and 1 for the transaction index; the command line says 2048 for the cache. Merging the file's dictionary with the command line's, the second last, gives the cache of 2048 and keeps the index, which is what the example computes. Network-specific options add a second rule: an option in a section for one network, or prefixed with the network's name, takes precedence over the same option without it (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe help text and the book must not be trusted blindly for the defaults, because defaults change from release to release: the book's value for the database cache is 450 and the current one is 1024 for machines with enough memory, and the minimum relay fee dropped in release 30.0 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Another common mistake is to put an option that is valid only on the command line, such as the one that names the file itself, into the file that it names, where it cannot work. A third is to forget that negation exists: a line written with the prefix no switches something off and is not a typing error." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#ConfigurationOption> .
chx:S_DataDirectory a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Data directory"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Data directory" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 33 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Configuration ;
    skos:definition "The data directory is the folder in which Bitcoin Core stores everything it has to remember between runs. The documentation describes it as the default location where the files of the program are stored, and gives the default place for each system: a folder named .bitcoin in the user's home directory on Linux, a folder called Bitcoin under Application Support on macOS, and a folder called Bitcoin under the local application data on Windows (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The book says the same for the .bitcoin folder, and that the option datadir selects the directory and filesystem in which to put all the blockchain data (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe directory is the node's memory, so where it lives decides whether the node can run at all. The book says that, depending on the configuration, it can use from about 10 GB to almost 1 TB as of the writing, and that the maximum is expected to grow by several hundred gigabytes per year (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). For that reason the first sample file puts the directory on a separate disk with plenty of room, and a user who has a small system disk and a large second disk needs this option before the first start.\n\nThe evidence for this version shows what a young node keeps there. A freshly started release 31.1, with no peers, had in its directory a file for the banned addresses, the configuration file written for the run, the process identifier file, the folder of blocks, the folder of the unspent-output database called chainstate, the debug log, the folder of the wallet named demo, the fee estimates, the mempool dump, the peer address database and the settings file (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The documentation explains the others in the layout table: a LevelDB database holds the block index, the files blkNNNNN.dat hold the blocks in network format at 128 MiB per file, and the optional index folders appear when an index is switched on (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nAll content of the directory except the configuration file is specific to one network. The main network uses the directory itself, and the others use subfolders: testnet3, testnet4, signet and regtest, as the lookup in the worked example shows for regtest (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The program keeps a lock file in the directory, writes its process identifier into a file while it runs and creates the cookie file there too, which is a reason why the access to the directory is a security matter.\n\nA directory is not a place to copy files from one running node to another. The documentation warns that a wallet file must not be shared across different node instances, because that can result in key reuse and double spends, and that a backup of a wallet should be made with the backup call so that the file is locked while it is copied (Bitcoin Core, 2026). It also advises against the exFAT filesystem on macOS, because of reports of database corruption (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The cheapest mistake, however, is to fill the disk: a node that cannot write stops, and that is the reason to plan the space." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#DataDirectory> .
chx:S_DatabaseCache a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Database cache (dbcache)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Database cache (dbcache)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 34 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Configuration ;
    skos:definition "The database cache is a region of memory in which the node keeps part of the set of unspent outputs, so that it does not have to read the disk for every output it needs to check. The option that sizes it is called dbcache, and the book describes it as the size of the UTXO cache, with a default of 450 mebibytes, the advice being to increase it on high-end hardware to read and write the disk less often, or reduce it on low-end hardware to save memory at the expense of using the disk more frequently (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A mebibyte is 1024 times 1024 bytes.\n\nThe cache matters most while the node is catching up with the chain. The documentation on reducing memory says that a lower value makes the initial sync time much longer, and that after the first synchronization the effect is less pronounced for most uses, unless fast validation of blocks is important, such as for mining (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The same page warns that when the operating system starts swapping memory to disk continuously, the node can slow to a crawl, which is why a too-large value is also a mistake.\n\nThe current value differs from the book. Release 31.0 raised the default to 1024 MiB from 450 MiB on systems where at least 4096 MiB of RAM is detected, and the release notes say that to keep the previous behavior one sets dbcache to 450 (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The help of the release 31.1 states a minimum of 4 and a default of 1024, and the source shows the rule: the larger value is chosen only on a 64-bit system with the required memory, and the old 450 MiB remains the default in all other cases (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe configured total is divided before it is used. The source gives the block index database up to 2 MiB, which is the smaller of that value and an eighth of the total, then the database of the chain state up to 8 MiB, which is the smaller of that value and half of what remains, and the rest goes to the cache of unspent outputs (Bitcoin Core, 2026). For 1024 MiB the worked example returns 2, 8 and 1014, which are the three numbers that the start-up log of the evidence prints for the block index, the chain state and the in-memory set of unspent outputs (Bitcoin Core, 2026). If the transaction index is on, ten percent of the total goes to it first.\n\nThe memory is shared with the mempool, which the log shows by saying that up to 286.1 MiB of unused mempool space can be added to the 1014 MiB, a figure that the mempool limit concept explains (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Setting the value larger than the memory that is really available is a mistake that containers make easy, because the detected RAM may exceed what the container may use, a case that the release notes mention, and the node may then run out of memory (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The node also logs a warning when it believes a value is too large for the detected memory." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#DatabaseCache> .
chx:S_TransactionIndex a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Transaction index (txindex)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transaction index (txindex)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 35 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Configuration ;
    skos:definition "The transaction index is an optional database that lets the node find any confirmed transaction by its identifier. The book explains that by default Bitcoin Core builds a database containing only the transactions related to the user's wallet, and that to access any transaction with commands like getrawtransaction one has to configure the node to build a complete index with the option txindex set to 1 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The help of the release 31.1 describes it as a full transaction index used by the getrawtransaction call, off by default (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nWithout the index the node still has the whole chain, but it has no quick way from a transaction identifier to the block that holds it. The question matters to programs that look up arbitrary transactions, such as an explorer or the application back end of the book's first sample file. The book adds that if the option is not set at first and later set, one has to wait for the index to be rebuilt, and that the lookup works provided the block containing the transaction has not been pruned (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe index is met in the section that follows, where the book decodes Alice's transaction by asking for it by its identifier, and in the data directory, where the documentation says that it lives in a LevelDB database in a folder named indexes/txindex and exists only if the option is on (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). It is also met in the way the cache is split: when the index is enabled the source gives it ten percent of the total cache, up to one gibibyte, before the remainder goes to the set of unspent outputs; for 1024 MiB that is 102.4 MiB, as the example calculates (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe source of the index describes it as written to a LevelDB database that records the block sequence number and the serialized block offset of each transaction by transaction hash (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A call such as getrawtransaction with an identifier looks the identifier up in the index, reads the block from the disk and returns the transaction. Building the first index means going through all the blocks, which explains the waiting time of the book. The index does not store the transactions again; it only stores where to find them, so its size is far smaller than the blocks themselves.\n\nThe index cannot be combined with pruning. The help text of the pruning option says the mode is incompatible with the transaction index, and the run of release 31.1 in the evidence confirms it: starting with both ends with the error that prune mode is incompatible with txindex (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The help of the current release is more exact than the book about what happens without the index: by default the call for a raw transaction returns a transaction only if it is in the mempool, and if the caller names a block, it returns the transaction when the block is available and contains it (Bitcoin Core, 2026). So a node without the index can still answer if it is told in which block to look, but not otherwise. Finally, the index is a convenience for the owner, and it is not part of the consensus rules." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#TransactionIndex> .
chx:S_BlocksOnlyMode a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Blocks-only mode"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Blocks-only mode" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 36 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Configuration ;
    skos:definition "Blocks-only mode is a setting in which the node does not take part in the relay of unconfirmed transactions. The book describes the option as a way to minimize bandwidth usage by only accepting blocks of confirmed transactions from the peers instead of relaying unconfirmed transactions (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The help text of release 31.1 is more exact: it rejects transactions from network peers, switches off the automatic broadcast and rebroadcast of transactions, unless the source peer has the forcerelay permission, and leaves transactions sent through the RPC interface unaffected (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe mode exists because relaying every transaction is the largest continuing cost of a node after the first download. A node in this mode still downloads and validates every block, so it enforces the same rules and remains a full node, but it receives each transaction only once, inside a block, and it holds no pool of unconfirmed transactions. The documentation on reducing memory says that the mode reduces the default memory use of the mempool to 5 MB, and that the source defines the blocks-only default as 5, against 300 for a normal node (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe book uses the option in its second sample file, together with pruning and small caches, for a node on a resource-constrained server (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It is also useful where the owner pays for each gigabyte of upload, and the book recommends against running a node on a metered connection or running it in a way that constrains its bandwidth (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The documentation on reducing memory names it among the ways to use less memory (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nIn normal operation a node announces and requests unconfirmed transactions from its peers and keeps them in its mempool, from where it can tell a miner or a wallet about them. In blocks-only mode it skips the request, so the mempool stays nearly empty, and the node learns of a payment only when the payment is confirmed in a block. The worked example divides the normal default of 300 megabytes by the blocks-only default of 5, which shows that the pool of a blocks-only node may be sixty times smaller.\n\nThe mode has a privacy cost that the documentation spells out: it must not be used when the node is also used to broadcast transactions, because any transaction sent will stick out like a sore thumb, and when it is combined with the wallet, the wallet should be told not to broadcast and not to spend unconfirmed change, and another mechanism for broadcasting should be used (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Because such a node does not receive transactions that are not yet in a block, a merchant who wants to see a payment before its confirmation cannot rely on it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#BlocksOnlyMode> .
chx:S_MempoolLimit a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Mempool limit (maxmempool)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Mempool limit (maxmempool)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 37 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Configuration ;
    skos:definition "The mempool limit is the maximum size of the pool of unconfirmed transactions that the node holds, set by the option maxmempool. The book describes it as limiting the transaction memory pool to this many megabytes, and says to use it to reduce memory use on memory-constrained nodes; its second sample file sets it to 150 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The help text of release 31.1 gives the default as 300 megabytes, and the documentation on reducing memory says the unit is the megabyte of 1000 bytes, not the mebibyte, and that the minimum is 5 (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA pool that has no limit would let any sender fill the memory of every node with unconfirmed transactions. The limit forces the node to decide which transactions to keep, and the documentation says that a lower maximum means that transactions will be evicted sooner, which affects any use of the node that processes unconfirmed transactions (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Chapter 2 explained that a transaction waits in the mempool until it is mined; this is the bound on how long and how many can wait.\n\nA reader meets the figure in the start-up log. For the default of 300 megabytes, the log of the release 31.1 in the evidence says that the in-memory set of unspent outputs has 1014.0 MiB, plus up to 286.1 MiB of unused mempool space (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The source explains why: the limit in bytes is the number of megabytes times 1,000,000, and the unused part is lent to the cache of unspent outputs, so that the memory the node uses in total stays near the sum of the two settings (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe conversion explains the odd-looking figure: 300 megabytes is 300 times 10 to the sixth bytes, and dividing by 2 to the twentieth bytes per mebibyte gives 286.1 mebibytes, as the worked example computes. The mempool and the cache therefore draw on one memory budget, which is why the documentation recommends limiting the mempool when trying to reduce the total memory use, and why the blocks-only mode, which needs only 5 MB, leaves more for the cache (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe unit is the usual mistake: a number of megabytes of 1000 bytes is smaller than the same number of mebibytes, and the two options that look alike, the mempool limit and the database cache, use different units. A node with a small mempool also forgets low-fee transactions sooner, so a wallet that depends on the node may see its payment dropped from that node's pool while other nodes still hold it. Bitcoin Core also expires transactions after a number of hours, an option that the help lists, so the limit is not the only bound." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#MempoolLimit> .
chx:S_AlertNotify a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Alert notification (alertnotify)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Alert notification (alertnotify)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 38 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Configuration ;
    skos:definition "The alert notification is a hook that lets a node tell its owner that something is wrong. The book describes the option as running a specified command or script to send emergency alerts to the owner of the node, and both its sample files set it to a script that sends an e-mail with the text Alert followed by the message (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The help text says that the command is executed when an alert is raised and that the symbol %s in the command is replaced by the message (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA node runs without anybody watching it, and some events need a person. Bitcoin Core's source shows two situations in which the node raises such a warning: when it finds an invalid chain with more work than its own best chain by more than six blocks of work, which could be due to database corruption or to consensus incompatibility with the peers, and when blocks signal new rules that this version of the software does not know, with the message that unknown new rules were activated and the number of the version bit (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Both are signs that the owner's software may be out of step with the network, a case that the soft fork concept explains.\n\nThe reader meets the option in the book's two sample files and in the help text. In practice it is a line that points to a script, which may send an e-mail, a message to a phone or an entry in a monitoring system. Since the script is the owner's, the node gives no idea of what is done with the message, and it does not wait for it: the source starts the command in a thread that runs free, so a slow script cannot hold up the node (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nWhen a warning is set, the node calls a function that reads the command from the option, takes the warning text, strips every character that is not a letter, a digit, a blank or one of the characters in the string .,;-_/:?@(), puts single quotes around the result and replaces each %s in the command with it (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The worked example copies these steps for a hostile message that tries to close the quote and add a command: the quote itself is removed, so the text stays inside one quoted word and the final command line is harmless. The rules of the shell for single quotes are the reason the technique works.\n\nThe cleaning protects against the message but not against the owner's own script. A script that passes its argument on without quoting it can still be abused, and the %s must be inside the quotes of the command, as the book's sample does. The option also depends on the system providing a command interpreter, since the source compiles the function only if the system has one, and on the script being executable by the user who runs the node (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Finally, the command runs only when a warning is newly set, because the source calls it only if the warning was not already present, so a script that sends e-mail should not be the only way to learn that the node is in trouble." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#AlertNotify> .
chx:S_Running a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Running"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Running" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 39 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Operation ;
    skos:definition "Running is the group of concepts that describe a node while it is alive: how it is started so that it keeps going without a person, what it writes while it starts, and how its owner asks two short questions about its state. The book moves from the configuration to this stage with a test: the node is started in the foreground with the option that sends the log to the console, the owner reads the lines to see whether the right settings are loaded, and interrupts the program once satisfied. Then the node is run in the background as a process and queried with the command line client (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA node is only useful when it runs continuously. The book ends the section with the advice to add Bitcoin Core to the start-up scripts of the operating system, so that it runs all the time and restarts when the operating system restarts, and it points to the examples in the folder contrib/init of the source (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The two status commands that follow tell the owner whether the node is synchronized with the chain and how it stands in the network, which are the two things that decide whether its answers can be relied on.\n\nThe reader meets this group the first time the program is started. The log printed to the console is the first thing seen, the data directory gains the log file and the files that mark a running process, and the owner types the first command to learn the state of the chain. The evidence of this version contains the real log of the release 31.1, the answers of its two status commands, and the directory it created, all from an offline node, which is a node that was told not to look for peers (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe concepts are taken in the order of events. The daemon concept explains how the program leaves the terminal and how the operating system keeps it alive; the start-up log concept explains the lines the program prints while it prepares itself; the blockchain information concept reads the answer about the chain, and the network information concept reads the answer about the node and its connections. Together they show a node that has just been started and has not yet synchronized, which is the state in which the book itself prints them." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Running> .
chx:S_Daemon a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Daemon"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Daemon" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 40 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Running ;
    skos:definition "A daemon is a program that runs in the background of the operating system, without a terminal and without a person typing to it, and waits for work. The installed-files table of Bitcoin Core describes the program bitcoind as the Bitcoin node and wallet daemon, and the book tells the reader how to make it one: to run Bitcoin Core in the background as a process, start it with the daemon option, as bitcoind -daemon (Bitcoin Core, 2026) (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The help of release 31.1 adds a second option, which makes the program wait for the initialization to be finished before it returns, and implies the first (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe daemon form exists because a node has to run for months, through the closing of terminals and the restarting of machines. A program that is attached to a terminal ends when the terminal ends. For this reason the book advises to add the node to the startup scripts of the operating system, and the documentation of the project says that running the program as a daemon does not need any manual configuration, because it creates its own credential for the interface when it starts (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe project ships sample scripts and configuration files for systemd, Upstart, OpenRC, a SysV-style script for CentOS and a launch agent for macOS, all in the folder contrib/init, and the init documentation explains how to install each one: for systemd, copy the file to the services folder, reload the configuration, and start and enable the service with the systemctl command (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The data directory of a running node holds a file with its process identifier, which the evidence shows as a file called bitcoind.pid, created at start and deleted at shutdown (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe systemd unit is a good example of what such a script does. Its start command calls bitcoind with the options for the process identifier file, the configuration file, the data directory, and two commands to run at start-up and before the shutdown, which tell systemd that the node is ready or that it is stopping; the unit does not pass the daemon option, because systemd itself keeps the program in the background (Bitcoin Core, 2026). It declares that the service sends notifications, asks that it be restarted on failure, allows ten minutes for stopping, and runs it as the user bitcoin with several restrictions of the system such as no new privileges and no access to the home directories of other users (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe sample scripts assume paths that may have to be adjusted, such as /usr/bin/bitcoind, /etc/bitcoin/bitcoin.conf and /var/lib/bitcoind, and a user and group called bitcoin that must be created before use (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The init documentation also says that datadir cannot be overridden in the configuration file with the init files as they are, because the command-line options in those files take precedence, and the comment at the top of the unit file says the same of every option named in its start command (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The operating system keeping a program alive does not mean it is healthy: a node that was restarted a hundred times because of a bad setting is still a node with a bad setting, and its log is where to look." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Daemon> .
chx:S_StartupLog a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Start-up log"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Start-up log" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 41 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Running ;
    skos:definition "The start-up log is the stream of messages that the node writes while it starts, and afterwards as it runs. With the option that sends the log to the console it appears in the terminal, and otherwise it goes to a file called debug.log in the data directory, as the documentation of the data directory states (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The book prints the first lines of such a log twice, once to show where the configuration file is and once to show that the chosen settings are loaded, and it advises to interrupt the process once satisfied that the node is loading the correct settings and running as expected (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nReading the log is how an owner checks what the node is really doing. The documentation of the configuration file says that the values in use can be seen in the log and that options in the file that the program does not recognize appear there as warnings, so a mistyped option is visible only to someone who reads it (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The log also records the environment the node found: the machine instructions used for hashing, the sources of randomness, the limits on connections and the memory it assigned to each cache.\n\nThe evidence for this version holds the log of a real start of the release 31.1. It begins with the version, then lines about parameter interaction, since switching off listening switched off several related features, then the warning that the log may contain privacy-sensitive information and should be shared with care, and the choice of the implementation of the SHA-256 hash function that the processor supports (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The same pages show the data directory and the configuration file in use, one line for each setting read from the file, the limit of 125 automatic connections, and the opening of the interface on the local address 127.0.0.1, port 8332 (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nSeveral lines can be compared with the book's log of release 24.0.1. In the book the wallet check prints that BerkeleyDB version 4.8.30 is used, and in release 31.1 it prints that SQLite version 3.50.4 is used, which agrees with the removal of the older wallet format. The book prints that four threads were started for the interface, and the current log says that the server was started with 16 worker threads, the default of the option that sets that number, as the worked example reads from the log (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The authentication line is the same in both: the node generates a random cookie, which the interface concept explains. The log then lists the cache configuration, whose figures the database cache concept explains.\n\nThe log is not a stable interface: the wording and the order of lines change from release to release, and a program that parses them breaks. It is also sensitive: the node itself warns that the log may hold privacy-sensitive information, so an owner who pastes a log into a public forum to ask for help should read it first. A last caution is about size: the help of the release has an option that shrinks the file at start-up, which is on by default when no debugging categories are selected, so an owner who wants a long history has to keep copies of the file himself." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#StartupLog> .
chx:S_BlockchainInfo a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Blockchain information (getblockchaininfo)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Blockchain information (getblockchaininfo)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 42 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Running ;
    skos:definition "Blockchain information is the answer that the node gives to the call getblockchaininfo, the book's first example of monitoring a node: start it in daemon mode and then use the command bitcoin-cli getblockchaininfo to watch its progress and its status (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The answer is a JSON object. Its fields, as the source of the release describes them, include the name of the network, the height of the chain of most work that has been fully validated, the number of block headers the node has validated, the hash of the best block, the difficulty target, the median block time, an estimate of the progress of verification and the total work of the chain (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe answer is the quickest way to learn whether a node can be trusted for a given purpose. A node whose blocks are far fewer than its headers is still downloading and validating, and the field that tells whether it is in the initial block download says so directly. The book explains what its own printout shows: a blockchain height of 0 blocks and 83,999 headers, because the node first fetches the block headers from its peers to find the blockchain with the most proof of work and then goes on to download the full blocks, validating them as it goes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reader meets the call as soon as the node runs. The printout of the book comes from a node that had just started its synchronization, and the evidence file of this version comes from the release 31.1 offline node that had only the first block, so its answer is also the beginning of the chain: the chain is the main network, there are 0 blocks and 0 headers, the best block hash is that of the first block ever mined, and the node reports that it is still in the initial download and is not pruned (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Two fields that the book's printout lacks, the compact target in the field bits and the full target, have been added to the current answer.\n\nSome fields deserve a closer reading. The field with the work of the chain, written in hexadecimal, shows 100010001 at its end for the first block, as in the book's printout, which the chain work concept derives from the target. The median time of the first block equals its own time, since there are no earlier blocks, a point the median time concept explains. The size on disk was 89,087 in the book's printout and 293 in the evidence, because the node of the evidence has one block. And the field for warnings is an empty array, where the book's printout shows an empty string: release 28.0 made it an array of strings listing all active warnings, and the old behavior can be restored with a deprecated-option setting (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe numbers are a snapshot, and a node that is receiving blocks changes them every few minutes. A height equal to the headers does not by itself prove that the node is caught up with the network, since both come from the node's peers, and the verification progress is an estimate between zero and one, not a measure. The field that says that the node is in the initial block download is described by the source as debug information and as an estimate. An owner who needs certainty compares the best block hash with a block explorer or with a second node." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#BlockchainInfo> .
chx:S_NetworkInfo a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Network information (getnetworkinfo)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Network information (getnetworkinfo)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 43 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Running ;
    skos:definition "Network information is the answer to the call getnetworkinfo, one of the commands that the book lists beside the call for the chain, the mempool and the wallet when it says how to get information on the status of the node (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Where the blockchain information describes the chain, this answer describes the node itself as a participant in the network: the version of the software, the version of the protocol, the services it offers to its peers, whether it relays transactions, how many connections it has and over which networks it can be reached (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe answer tells the owner what the rest of the network sees. A node that offers fewer services than expected, or that has no connections, or that reports a network as not reachable, is not doing what its owner thinks. It also shows the settings that decide which transactions the node accepts, the minimum relay fee and the incremental fee, given in bitcoins per thousand virtual bytes, which is why the figure of 0.00001000 in the book's printout means one satoshi per virtual byte, and the figure of 0.000001 in the evidence means 0.1 satoshis per virtual byte, the value that the release notes of 30.0 give for the new default (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe evidence file is from the offline node of release 31.1. It shows the version as 310100, the user-agent string as /Satoshi:31.1.0/, the protocol version as 70016, the local services as the hexadecimal number 0c09, which the list of names expands to network, witness, limited network and the new encrypted transport, no connections at all, and the two networks that are reachable, the internet protocols version 4 and version 6, while the privacy networks Tor, I2P and CJDNS are shown as limited and not reachable (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe version number encodes three numbers: the source defines the client version as 10000 times the major number plus 100 times the minor number plus the build number, so release 31.1.0 is 310100 and the book's release 24.0.1 is 240001, and the user-agent string is built by writing the three numbers between the name Satoshi and slashes, following the specification in BIP 14 (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The services are a bit field: network is bit 0, which means value 1, witness is bit 3, value 8, limited network is bit 10, value 1024, and the encrypted transport of BIP 324 is bit 11, value 2048; their sum is 3081, or 0xc09 in hexadecimal, as the worked example reconstructs; the book's printout, from a node without the new transport, shows 0x409, the sum of the first three (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nService bits are unauthenticated advertisements, as the source itself says, so a peer that claims a service may not provide it, and the code must be robust against that (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The protocol version of the evidence, 70016, is not the constant in the newest source tree, which is 70017, because the tree is newer than the release, so two figures from different dates should not be compared as if they were errors. A last caution is the name of the user agent: it says Satoshi for historical reasons and is not a claim about who runs the node." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#NetworkInfo> .
chx:S_Wallet a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Wallet"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Wallet" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 44 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Operation ;
    skos:definition "The wallet group collects the concepts about the wallet that comes with Bitcoin Core. Chapter 1 introduced the wallet as the application with which a user holds and spends bitcoin, and the book says here that the reference implementation includes wallets next to the validation engine and the tools for block construction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In this software the wallet is optional: the build can leave it out, as the book's option to disable the reference wallet implementation shows, and a node can run without one (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe wallet is where a node meets money. Everything else in the node concerns the shared record, whereas the wallet concerns the outputs that belong to its owner and the keys that spend them. Its storage format therefore matters to users more than most settings: a format that is retired makes old files unusable until they are converted. The concepts in this group give the current format, the retired one and the language of descriptions that the current one is built on.\n\nThe reader meets the wallet in the log, which names the wallet directory and the database in use, in the data directory, where each named wallet has a folder, and in the calls that create and inspect it. The evidence of this version creates a wallet named demo on the real release 31.1 and asks for its information and its descriptions, and it tries to create a retired kind of wallet, which the release refuses with an error (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The documentation of the project adds that since version 0.21 Bitcoin Core no longer has a default wallet, so one is created on request (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe three concepts follow the story of the format. The legacy wallet was the first kind, kept in a Berkeley DB file and described with keys alone. The descriptor wallet replaced it and is the only kind that can be created in the current release. The output descriptor is the language in which the descriptor wallet writes down which scripts belong to it, and in which the node answers when a transaction is decoded, as the later concept on decoding shows.\n\nTwo cautions apply. The first is that this chapter gives only what the node needs in order to run; the book treats keys and wallets in its later chapters. The second is that the file of a wallet holds what is needed to spend the owner's funds, and Bitcoin Core's documentation points out that the file is not encrypted by default and that it must be backed up with the call provided for it, not by copying the file of a running node (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Wallet> .
chx:S_DescriptorWallet a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Descriptor wallet"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Descriptor wallet" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 45 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Wallet ;
    skos:definition "A descriptor wallet is a wallet whose content is a set of output descriptions, each stating one family of scripts that the wallet can receive to and spend from. Bitcoin Core's documentation says that its code internally stores and operates on descriptors to reason about the sets of outputs that belong to the wallet (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The data are held in a file called wallet.dat that is a SQLite database, which the file documentation describes as a personal wallet with keys and transactions, and SQLite is a self-contained database engine that needs no server process and keeps a whole database in a single disk file (Bitcoin Core, 2026) (SQLite, 2026).\n\nThe descriptor wallet is the only kind that a current node can create. The release notes of 30.0 state that legacy wallets can no longer be created or loaded, and the run of release 31.1 in the evidence shows the consequence: asking for a wallet of the older kind fails with error code -4 and the message that the descriptors argument must be set to true because it is no longer possible to create a legacy wallet (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The change makes the wallet's behavior follow from descriptions that can be read, exported and checked, and no longer from a hidden list of keys.\n\nIn the evidence the call that creates a wallet named demo returns that name, and the call for the wallet's information returns the format sqlite, the value true for descriptors, 4000 for the number of keys in the pool of external keys and 4000 for the pool of internal ones, the value true for private keys enabled, and the flags that say a descriptor wallet is in use (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The log of the same release names the wallet directory and the version of SQLite, where the book's log of release 24.0.1 names Berkeley DB 4.8.30 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe list of descriptions of the demo wallet has eight entries: four kinds of script, the old pay-to-key-hash, the script-hash form that wraps a witness key hash, the witness key hash itself and the taproot form, and for each kind one entry for receiving and one for change, called internal. Each entry covers the range from 0 to 999 and is active. One thousand scripts in each of four kinds is the 4000 of the key pool for receiving, and the same count holds for change (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The wallet can therefore hand out addresses of any of the modern types.\n\nThe wallet file is not encrypted by default, and encrypting it needs a passphrase that cannot be recovered; the documentation warns that a lost passphrase loses the coins (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A copy or a backup should be taken with the backup call, not by copying a file that the running node is writing, and a wallet file must not be shared by two node instances (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Finally, a wallet made for the older format is not lost: it can be migrated to this one, as the concept of the legacy wallet explains." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#DescriptorWallet> .
chx:S_LegacyWallet a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Legacy wallet"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Legacy wallet" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 46 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Wallet ;
    skos:definition "A legacy wallet is the kind of wallet that Bitcoin Core used before descriptors: a Berkeley DB database file holding the keys of the owner and the transactions that concern them. The file documentation still lists its parts under legacy files, a wallet.dat that is a Berkeley DB database, a folder with the logging files of the database, an error file called db.log and a lock file (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The book's log of release 24.0.1 shows the kind in use, with the line that BerkeleyDB version 4.8.30 is used, and the book's build options include one for building a wallet with an incompatible version of the Berkeley DB library (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe concept matters for two reasons. The first is historical: most of the books, articles and old backups that a learner will meet describe this wallet, with calls such as the ones for dumping or importing a private key. The second is practical: the release notes of 30.0 state that such wallets can no longer be created or loaded, and that they can be migrated to the descriptor format with the migratewallet call (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A user who owns an old file has to migrate it, because the node will not open it as it is.\n\nThe evidence shows the refusal. On the real release 31.1, a call to create a wallet with the descriptors argument set to false returns an error with code -4, and the text of the message says that the descriptors argument must be set to true because it is no longer possible to create a legacy wallet (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The worked example reads the code from that saved output. The same release notes list the legacy-only calls that were removed, among them the ones to add a multisignature address, to dump a private key, to dump or import a wallet, and to upgrade a wallet (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe reason for the change lies in what a wallet must know. A legacy wallet stored keys and had to guess which scripts the keys might be used in, whereas a descriptor wallet stores the descriptions themselves, so a program that reads the wallet knows exactly which outputs are meant. Migration converts the old content into descriptions, and the release notes say that the wallet tool of the project lost the options that selected the older internal database and the legacy format (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA learner should not treat a command from an older text as current, because commands that the book's era used for the legacy wallet may no longer exist: the release notes name eleven of them as removed (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A migration is a change to something that holds money, so a backup should be made before it, and a migration is not reversible by the node. Hardware or software that still expects a legacy file must be updated as well." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#LegacyWallet> .
chx:S_OutputDescriptor a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Output descriptor"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Output descriptor" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 47 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Wallet ;
    skos:definition "An output descriptor is a short piece of text in a simple language that describes a collection of output scripts. Bitcoin Core's documentation introduces it in exactly these words, and says that the wallet code uses descriptors internally to reason about the outputs that belong to the wallet (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A descriptor names the kind of script by a function, such as one for a pay-to-witness-public-key-hash script, one for a multisignature script, one for any type of address and one for raw hexadecimal script, and puts the keys inside it, as in the documented example of a witness key hash with a given public key (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nBefore descriptors, a wallet's backup contained keys and left the reader to guess which scripts they had been used for, and software of different makers could not agree on what a wallet contains. A descriptor states it completely, in a form that can be copied, printed and checked. The standard that defines the general syntax is BIP 380, and further BIPs define the individual expressions, which is why a descriptor written for one program can be read by another (Bitcoin Core, 2026) (Wuille and Chow, 2021).\n\nThe reader meets descriptors in several places. The call that decodes a transaction shows one for every output: in Alice's transaction, the second output is described as an address expression with the checksum qq404gts, and the first, which pays to a taproot output, as a raw taproot expression with the checksum 38d6v6ev (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The call to list the descriptors of the demo wallet returns eight descriptors, and the call that explains a descriptor, getdescriptorinfo, returns its checksum and says whether it can be solved and whether it has private keys, which for the documented witness key hash example are the checksum 8zl0zxma, the value true and the value false (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe top level of a descriptor is a script expression, or the same followed by the number sign and a checksum of eight characters (Wuille and Chow, 2021). The checksum is an error-correcting code similar to the one of the address format called bech32, using its alphabet of 32 characters, and it can be generated from the BIP's reference code, which the worked example runs: it produces the checksum 8zl0zxma for the documented example, the one that Bitcoin Core 31.1 returned, and it reports false for a descriptor in which one character of the checksum or of the script was changed (Wuille and Chow, 2021) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The documentation states how strong the code is: as long as the errors only replace characters of the basic set by others of it, up to four errors are always detected in descriptors of up to 501 characters, and for other errors there is roughly a one in a trillion chance of missing them (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe checksum protects against accidents and not against attackers, since anybody can compute a valid one for any text, so it is no proof that a descriptor came from the person one thinks. Whether a checksum is required depends on the call: the BIP says it is optional for parsing and that applications may choose to reject descriptors without one, and Bitcoin Core requires it for only some calls, among them the one that derives addresses and the one that imports descriptors (Wuille and Chow, 2021) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A descriptor that contains private keys is as secret as the keys, and one with only public keys reveals, to whoever reads it, every address of the wallet." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#OutputDescriptor> .
chx:S_OutputType a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Output type"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Output type" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 48 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Wallet ;
    skos:definition "An output type is the form of the locking script of an output, which decides what the spender must show to spend it. Chapter 2 introduced the locking script, and the documentation of Bitcoin Core's output descriptors names the common forms. The expression pkh describes a P2PKH output, the pay-to-key-hash form, wpkh a P2WPKH output, the pay-to-witness-key-hash form, sh(wpkh) a P2SH-P2WPKH output, in which a witness key hash is wrapped in a script hash, and tr a P2TR output, the taproot form (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe output type decides the address format and the cost of the payment. The older forms put all the data in the part of the transaction that counts fully towards its size, whereas the witness forms move the signature to the witness, which is discounted, as the weight concept explained. A descriptor wallet keeps one pair of descriptors for each type, one for receiving and one for change.\n\nThe demo wallet of the chapter's evidence has eight descriptors, and the worked example reads them and finds the four function names pkh, sh, tr and wpkh, each used once for receiving and once for change (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The names pkh and wpkh are the ones the documentation describes.\n\nThe descriptor of an output type is a function that takes a key, or several, and says how the locking script is built from it. A parser reading wpkh(KEY) knows that the script is a witness program of version 0 containing the hash of KEY, whereas tr(KEY) means a witness program of version 1. The checksum that ends the descriptor protects the expression as a whole, so a typing error in the type or the key is caught.\n\nThe abbreviations are easy to confuse. The lowercase function names of the descriptors, pkh, wpkh, sh and tr, and the uppercase names of the output types, P2PKH, P2WPKH, P2SH and P2TR, belong together but are not the same thing, and sh(wpkh) is one combined type and not two. The documentation's one-line descriptions are the reliable way to look up which is which." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#OutputType> .
chx:S_Interface a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Interface"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Interface" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 49 ;
    skos:definition "The interface branch is about how other programs and people talk to a running node. A node that only validates and stores would be of little use if nothing could ask it a question, and so Bitcoin Core offers an application programming interface. The book introduces it as an interface that can be reached by a command-line helper, which lets a reader experiment interactively with the capabilities that are also available to programs, and then through code (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). This is the part of the chapter where the reader stops reading about the node and starts using it.\n\nThe interface is what turns a node into a platform. Wallets, explorers, payment processors and research tools get their information about the chain from a node they trust, and they ask it through this interface, which is also the reason why the book lists \"programmable access to the network and the blockchain\" as one of the reasons for running a node of one's own (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The same interface can also spend funds, change settings and read private data when a wallet is loaded, so the way it is protected is as important as the way it is used.\n\nThe reader meets the interface at the command line, where the helper program sends a request and prints the answer, in the examples of the chapter that decode a transaction and a block, and in the short Python programs that make the same calls through a library. The evidence of this version checks the printed examples against the real release 31.1, and it records what the release answers when asked for the list of commands, which in the current release has 151 entries in nine categories (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe branch has two groups. The first group, the RPC interface, explains the idea of an interface, the remote procedure call protocol with its JSON messages and the HTTP requests that carry them, and the two ways in which Bitcoin Core decides who may call: a random cookie and hashed credentials. The second group, the clients, explains the command line, the helper program that builds the requests for a person, and the wrapper libraries that build them for a program.\n\nThe documentation of the project is blunt about the risk. The interface allows other programs to control Bitcoin Core, including spending funds from the wallets, affecting the verification of consensus rules, and reading private data; by default only a client on the same computer can reach it, and only with a valid credential, and the documentation says not to enable connections over the public Internet because the credentials are sent as clear text (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A reader who exposes the port to learn faster is taking the largest risk in the chapter." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Interface> .
chx:S_RpcInterface a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "RPC interface"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "RPC interface" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 50 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Interface ;
    skos:definition "The RPC interface is the group of concepts that describe the programmatic doorway of Bitcoin Core. It gathers the idea of an application programming interface, the protocol that Bitcoin Core uses for it, the data format and the transport on which that protocol rides, and the two mechanisms with which the node checks the identity of a caller. The book puts it in one sentence: Bitcoin Core's API is a JSON-RPC interface, where JSON is the way to present data and RPC means a remote procedure call over a network protocol, in this case HTTP (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nChoosing an interface that rests on JSON and HTTP lets nearly every programming language use the node. The book remarks that any program that can make an HTTP request can call the interface, and that libraries in most popular programming languages wrap it so that the call looks like a function of the language (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The cost is that the node must decide whom to answer, and the group explains how it does so with a cookie that exists only while the node runs, or with credentials that are stored only in hashed form.\n\nThe first lines of the book's example are a call written for the command-line program curl, with the address 127.0.0.1, the default port 8332, a body that names the method, and a user name. The port is the node's own: the source gives 8332 for the main network, 18332 for the third test network, 48332 for the fourth and 38332 for the signet (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The log of the evidence node confirms that it bound to the address 127.0.0.1 and port 8332, after the attempt to bind the IPv6 address ::1 had failed (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe concepts are taken from the outside inwards. The first is the interface in the general sense. Then come the protocol and the data format that define what a request looks like, and the HTTP exchange that carries it. The last two concepts describe the check that precedes any answer, the cookie, which needs no configuration, and the stored credential, which an owner must set up, and which a program that connects from another machine may need." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#RpcInterface> .
chx:S_Api a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Application programming interface"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Application programming interface" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 51 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RpcInterface ;
    skos:definition "An application programming interface, or API, is the defined way in which one program can use the functions of another. It names the requests that may be made, the information each request needs, and the form of the answer, and so it hides how the work is done behind a contract. The book states the purpose in a sentence: the whole point of an API is to access functions programmatically, while the helper that a person types is only useful for exploring the API and for testing functions (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The word application stands for any program that wants a service, and programming for the fact that the interface is meant for programs.\n\nThe API is the reason why one reference implementation can serve very different applications. A wallet, an exchange, a research script and a monitoring tool ask the same node different questions, and none of them needs to know how the node stores the blocks or checks the signatures. The book calls it programmable access to the network and the blockchain, and counts building applications that must validate transactions according to Bitcoin's consensus rules among the reasons for running one's own node (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nIn Bitcoin Core the API is made of named methods, which the chapter calls RPC commands. The command help lists them in categories; in the real release 31.1 the list printed 151 entries in nine categories: blockchain, control, mining, network, raw transactions, signer, util, wallet and ZeroMQ (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A few of them appear in the rest of the chapter, among them one that returns the hash of a block at a given height, one that returns a block, and one that returns and one that decodes a transaction.\n\nA method has a name and parameters, and it returns a result or an error. The help text for a method shows all three, and ends with two examples of how to call it, one with the helper program and one with the web client curl, as the book describes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The documentation of the project adds that the interface may change from one major version to the next, so it is implicitly versioned by the major version of Bitcoin Core, and that a deprecated feature can usually be switched on again for one major version with a command-line option (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The worked example reads the result member of a typical answer.\n\nAn interface is a promise only for as long as its owner keeps it. The documentation gives the grace period of one major version for deprecated features, and the chapter's own examples show a change: the field of warnings was a string and became an array of strings in release 28.0 (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A program that depends on the exact text of an answer, as opposed to its documented fields, breaks sooner. Finally, an API gives access, not trust: an answer from a node one does not control is only as good as that node." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Api> .
chx:S_JsonRpc a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "JSON-RPC interface"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "JSON-RPC interface" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 52 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RpcInterface ;
    skos:definition "JSON-RPC is a protocol for remote procedure calls whose messages are written in JSON. Its specification describes it as a stateless, light-weight remote procedure call protocol that defines a few data structures and the rules for processing them, independent of the transport, so that the same messages may travel within a process, over sockets or over HTTP (JSON-RPC Working Group, 2013). A call is a request object with a method name, parameters and an identifier chosen by the client, and the server replies with the same identifier, which lets the client match answers to calls (JSON-RPC Working Group, 2013).\n\nThe protocol keeps the interface small. A client needs to know only the name of a method and its parameters, and the answer tells the result or the error. Because the identifier comes back unchanged, a client can send many requests and still know which answer belongs to which. A request without an identifier is a notification, to which the server must not reply, a feature that Bitcoin Core supports for the newer version of the protocol (JSON-RPC Working Group, 2013) (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe book's example is a request in the older style: the body has the members jsonrpc with the value 1.0, id with the value curltest, method with the value getblockchaininfo and an empty list of parameters, sent by curl to the address 127.0.0.1 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The documentation of the project says that the server also recognizes version 2.0 requests, identified by the member jsonrpc with the value 2.0, and otherwise follows the older protocol that was the only one available in release 27.0 and before (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Among the differences are that a version 2.0 reply carries either a result or an error and not both, and that HTTP status codes are always 200 unless the HTTP server itself failed (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe two endpoints of the server are the root and a path for each wallet, and the documentation says that the second must be used for wallet requests when two or more wallets are loaded, as the helper does when it is given the name of a wallet (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Parameters may be passed by position or by name, as the specification allows; the helper offers the named form with an option, and the newer wrapper command bitcoin rpc is described as equivalent to the helper with that option (Bitcoin Core, 2026) (JSON-RPC Working Group, 2013). The worked example builds the book's request body and reads the method name back from it.\n\nThe name remote procedure call hides a danger: the call looks like a local function and behaves like a network request, so it can fail, be slow, or be answered by something else. The documentation warns that other programs on the same machine can try to provide an interface on the same port to trick a user into revealing the credentials, and that the interface does not guarantee escaping of data beyond what JSON needs, so the data of an answer, for example a decoded string, must be handled as untrusted (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The older style is also still accepted, so a program written for it keeps working, which may hide differences in the error behavior." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#JsonRpc> .
chx:S_JsonFormat a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "JSON data format"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "JSON data format" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 53 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RpcInterface ;
    skos:definition "JSON, short for JavaScript Object Notation, is a text format for the serialization of structured data, which its standard describes as lightweight, text-based and independent of any language (Bray, 2017). It can represent four primitive types, namely strings, numbers, booleans and null, and two structured types, objects and arrays (Bray, 2017). An object is written between curly brackets and holds named members, and an array is a list of values; a value may itself be an object or an array, so a document can nest as deeply as it needs.\n\nThe book calls JSON a very convenient way to present data that both humans and programs can easily read, and in the section on status information it says that the data is returned in JSON, a format that can easily be consumed by all programming languages while remaining quite human-readable (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). This is why the same answer can be read by a person at the terminal and parsed by a program, and why the rest of the book can show results as pieces of text.\n\nEvery answer in the chapter is JSON: the status of the chain, the status of the network, a decoded transaction, a block. The answer printed in the book for the call with the cookie contains an object with a member result that holds another object with the fields of the chain information, a member error with the value null and a member id with the value of the request (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The worked example parses that very text with the standard library and reads four of its values. The first member shows the string main, the block count is the number 799278, the error is null, which becomes None in Python, and the id is the string of the request.\n\nA program turns the text into its own data structures, and back again, using a library; in Python the standard module json does both. Numbers are the delicate part. JSON makes no promise about their precision, and the value of an amount may be written with eight decimals, which a floating-point number cannot always hold exactly, so careful programs read amounts as decimal numbers or as integers of satoshis. Hexadecimal data are carried as strings, because JSON has no type for bytes, which is why the documentation recommends to show serialized data in hexadecimal form only (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nJSON is a data format, not a protocol, and by itself it says nothing about what a member means: the meaning of the member bits or of the member weight comes from the documentation of Bitcoin Core. The order of the members of an object has no meaning and should not be relied on. The format also has no comments and no way to say that a number is a hash or an amount, so a mistake in interpretation is not caught by the parser. The course returns to this point at the end of the chapter, where JSON is given a meaning by a context." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#JsonFormat> .
chx:S_HttpRequest a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "HTTP request"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "HTTP request" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 54 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RpcInterface ;
    skos:definition "The Hypertext Transfer Protocol, HTTP, is the protocol on which the web is built. Its specification describes it as a family of stateless, application-level, request and response protocols: a client sends a request, the server sends a response, and the server keeps no memory between requests unless the client sends it again (Fielding et al., 2022). A request has a method, a target address, header fields and optionally a body, and a response has a status code, header fields and optionally a body. The status code 200 means that the request has succeeded (Fielding et al., 2022).\n\nUsing HTTP as the carrier means that a call can be made by any of the tools that already speak it, from a browser to a library of any language. The book shows curl, the versatile command-line HTTP client, and says that a program may use a generic HTTP library to build the call, as the example with curl does (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The manual of curl describes it as a tool for transferring data from or to a server using URLs, with options for the user, for the data to be sent without change, and for header fields (Stenberg, 2026).\n\nA reader sees the pieces of a request in the book's example: the option for the user supplies the credentials, the option for binary data supplies the body, the option for a header sets the content type to plain text, and the address gives the host 127.0.0.1 and the port 8332 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The library python-bitcoinlib, version 0.12.2, builds the same exchange itself: it sends a POST request with a JSON body, with the member version set to 1.1, and the header field Content-type set to application/json, and it adds an Authorization field that holds the word Basic and the credentials encoded in base64 (Todd, 2023).\n\nThe POST method asks the target resource to process the representation enclosed in the request according to its own semantics, which is what a remote call needs, since the body names the method to run (Fielding et al., 2022). The Authorization header field lets the client prove who it is (Fielding et al., 2022). Bitcoin Core accepts the form that starts with the word Basic, decodes the rest from base64, splits it at the first colon into the user and the password, and then checks them, as the source shows; the worked example builds such a header value for the user alice and the password secret.\n\nBase64 is an encoding, not encryption, so anybody who can read the request can read the credentials, and plain HTTP offers no protection on its way; the documentation of the project says that the interface uses authentication but no encryption (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The status codes are also an indication of a different kind from the errors of the call: with the older protocol a failed call returns an HTTP error status, and with the newer one it returns 200 with an error member, unless the failure was in the HTTP server itself (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A program that checks only the HTTP status will misjudge one of them." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#HttpRequest> .
chx:S_CookieAuthentication a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Cookie authentication"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Cookie authentication" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 55 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RpcInterface ;
    skos:definition "Cookie authentication is the default way in which Bitcoin Core decides who may call its interface. The book says that Bitcoin Core will create a random password on each start and place it in the data directory under the name .cookie, and that the helper program can read this password file given the data directory (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A program that can read the file can make calls, and one that cannot read it cannot. The documentation calls the file a session credential: it is created when the node starts and deleted when the node ends (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe method removes the need for the owner to invent and store a password. The init documentation says that a daemon run without any configuration uses a special cookie file for authentication, that the cookie is generated with random content when the daemon starts, and that read access to the file controls who can use the interface (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The project's security notes name this the preferred authentication method, because the secret is new at every start, never typed and never put into a configuration file (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe book prints the content of the file: a user name, __cookie__, a colon, and 64 hexadecimal digits, and then passes the whole string to curl as the credential (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The log of the real node of release 31.1 shows the same events: it generated an authentication cookie in the data directory, used the permissions that allow only the owner to read and write it, and then announced that it was using random cookie authentication (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The option rpccookiefile can move the file and the option rpccookieperms can widen its permissions to the group or to all users (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe source creates 32 random bytes with the node's random-number generator, writes them as 64 hexadecimal digits after the fixed user name __cookie__ and a colon, and renames the finished file into place (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The worked example takes the book's printed secret and shows that its 64 digits are 32 bytes. At start the node treats the cookie as a plaintext credential: it hashes the password with a random 16-byte salt, as it does for a configured user and password, and keeps only the user, the salt and the hash; a request is checked against them, so for a caller the cookie is an ordinary password whose value changes with every restart (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nAnyone who can read the cookie file controls the interface. The default file mode, owner only, is set by the process-wide file creation mask of 0077, and the documentation recommends that the data directory be readable only by the user and group of the node, with access to the interface controlled by group membership (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A cookie is also no solution for a client on another computer or a service that must survive restarts, because the secret changes each time; for these cases the stored credential of the next concept is used, as the book itself says by pointing to the helper script in the share folder (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#CookieAuthentication> .
chx:S_RpcAuth a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Hashed credentials (rpcauth)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Hashed credentials (rpcauth)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 56 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RpcInterface ;
    skos:definition "The rpcauth option is the way to give the interface a fixed credential without storing the password in plain text. Its value has three parts: a user name, a salt, and a hash of the password computed with that salt, written as user:salt$hash. The book points to the helper script rpcauth.py in the share folder of the source for creating a static password (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The script prints the line to be appended to the configuration file, and the password that the client must use (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA stored password in a configuration file is a secret on a disk that is copied, backed up and sometimes shared. Storing only a salted hash means that a leaked file does not reveal the password. The source of the node logs a warning when the older options for user and password are used, saying that they are less secure because the credentials are configured in plain text, and recommends cookie-based authentication or hashed credentials instead; the project's security notes name the script as the way to generate static credentials and the plain user and password options as the last fallback (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe script's output is a line starting with rpcauth= followed by the user, a colon, the salt, a dollar sign and the hash. For the user alice, the evidence file of this version records exactly this layout, with the salt and the hash replaced by placeholders so that no real secret is kept (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The init documentation mentions the option as the way to override the cookie behavior when the node runs as a daemon, noting that this password is mostly used as a fixed token that the node and the client programs read from the configuration file (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe script makes a salt of 16 random bytes, written as 32 hexadecimal digits, and, if no password is given, a random password from 32 random bytes in a URL-safe text form; the hash is the keyed-hash message authentication code HMAC with SHA-256, using the salt as the key and the password as the message, written as 64 hexadecimal digits (Bitcoin Core, 2026). When a request arrives, the node finds the entry for the user name, computes the same HMAC from the password it was sent, and compares the two hash values in a time that does not depend on how many characters agree (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The worked example builds such a line and counts the parts: 5, 32 and 64 characters for alice, the salt and the hash.\n\nThe hash protects the file, not the connection: the client still sends the password itself in each request, and the interface does not encrypt, so the credential is only as safe as the path between client and node (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A hashed credential also does not limit what the caller may do; the option rpcwhitelist can restrict the methods a user may call, but the documentation says that such restrictions should not be seen as robust security boundaries, and recommends system-level isolation instead (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A weak password remains weak, even though the hash is not reversible: it can be guessed offline by anyone who has the line." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#RpcAuth> .
chx:S_Salt a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Salt"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Salt" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 57 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RpcInterface ;
    skos:definition "A salt is a random value that is stored next to a hashed password and mixed into the hash. The script rpcauth.py, which the book points to for creating credentials, draws a salt of 16 random bytes, writes it as 32 hexadecimal characters, computes the hash of the password using the salt as the key, and prints the three parts as user:salt$hash (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The worked example hashes the same password with two different salts and gets two unrelated values, e21e60d6 and d84d1ad6 at the start, and the same salt gives the same value every time.\n\nThe server must be able to check a password without keeping it. It keeps the user, the salt and the hash, and when a request comes it computes the hash of the supplied password with the stored salt and compares. The salt means that two users with the same password have different stored values, and that an attacker cannot use one prepared table of hashes against all users. The line in the configuration file therefore reveals neither the password nor whether two accounts share one.\n\nThe credential line of the evidence of this chapter has a user of 5 characters, a salt of 32 and a hash of 64, and the node's source reads the same three fields from the configuration, computes the hash of the supplied password with the salt, and compares the result with the stored hash by a function that takes the same time whatever the content (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The cookie, as the cookie concept explains, is treated in the same way at start: the node hashes it with a random salt and keeps only the result.\n\nThe salt does not need to be secret, since it is stored in the open, but it must be random and different for each credential. The hash function used is the keyed hash of the next concept, with the salt in the place of the key. Because the hash is one-way, the stored value cannot be turned back into the password; an attacker can only guess passwords and test them, and the salt makes each test specific to one credential.\n\nA salt protects the stored value and not the password in transit. The password is still sent with each request, and over plain HTTP it is readable by anybody who can see the traffic, as the Base64 concept explains. A salt also does not make a weak password strong; it only prevents the reuse of work across credentials. That is why the helper script offers to generate a long random password, 32 bytes in text form, instead of leaving the choice to the user." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Salt> .
chx:S_Hmac a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Keyed hash (HMAC)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Keyed hash (HMAC)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 58 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_RpcInterface ;
    skos:definition "An HMAC, the keyed-hash message authentication code, is a hash function used with a key. The standard that publishes the SHA algorithms also covers the HMACs based on them, and says that the algorithms are typically used with other cryptographic algorithms such as digital signature algorithms and keyed-hash authentication codes (Eastlake and Hansen, 2011). With SHA-256 the result is 32 bytes, 64 hexadecimal characters. The worked example computes the standard's own test, with the key Jefe and the message what do ya want for nothing?, and gets the value that the standard prints.\n\nA plain hash proves nothing about who made it, because anybody can compute it. A keyed hash can be produced only with the key, so it is evidence that the holder of the key made it. Bitcoin Core uses this in a modest way, as the way to turn a password and a salt into a stored value. The same construction appears in other parts of the system, where a secret key is needed to derive other keys.\n\nThe script rpcauth.py calls the HMAC function of python with the salt as the key, the password as the message and SHA-256 as the hash, and the node's source does the same with its own implementation of the HMAC for SHA-256, writing the output in hexadecimal and comparing it with the stored hash (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The test value of the standard, 5BDCC146BF60754E6A042426089575C75A003F089D2739839DEC58B964EC3843, is reproduced exactly by the python library, which shows that the two implementations agree on a published case (Eastlake and Hansen, 2011).\n\nThe sample code of the standard states the construction in a comment: SHA(K XOR opad, SHA(K XOR ipad, text)), two nested hashes in which the key, combined with two fixed padding bytes, is mixed in at each level (Eastlake and Hansen, 2011). The details are in the standard and are not needed to use it. What matters is the interface: the key and the message go in and a value of the length of the hash comes out, and without the key the value cannot be reproduced or forged.\n\nAn HMAC authenticates data; it does not encrypt it. The message can be read by anybody who sees it, and the HMAC proves only that the sender knew the key. In the rpcauth credential the key is the salt, which is not secret, so the HMAC is used here as a one-way function with a per-credential parameter and not as a proof of the sender's identity. A reader should keep the two uses apart." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Hmac> .
chx:S_Client a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Client"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Client" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 59 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Interface ;
    skos:definition "The client group collects the concepts about the programs that call the interface. A client is whatever makes a request to the node: a person at a terminal with the helper program, a script that builds the HTTP message itself, or an application that uses a library written for its language. The book goes through them in this order, starting with the help command of the helper, continuing with the curl command that the help prints, and ending with a Python library (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe choice of client decides how much of the protocol a user has to know. At the terminal one needs only the name of a method and its parameters. With a generic HTTP tool one must also write the body and manage the credential. With a wrapper library one writes a function call, and the library does the rest. The book remarks that the helper is very useful for exploring the API and testing functions, and that the whole point of an API is to access functions programmatically (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nAll three clients are met in the chapter. The helper is used for the first calls, to list the commands, to get help on one of them and to ask for the hash of block 1000, which the book prints as 00000000c937983704a73af28acdec37b049d214adbda81d7e2a3dd146f6ed09 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The Python programs in the last part use the wrapper library, and in the evidence of this version the book's first program was run unchanged with the library at version 0.12.2 against the node of release 31.1 (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe concepts are the command line, the helper program that runs on it, and the wrapper library. The command line is the environment in which commands are typed. The helper is the program of the project that turns what is typed into an interface request. The wrapper library does the same for a program and returns the decoded answer as a data structure of the language." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Client> .
chx:S_CommandLine a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Command line"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Command line" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 60 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Client ;
    skos:definition "The command line is a way of using a computer by typing text commands in a terminal and reading the text that the programs print. A command is the name of a program followed by arguments, separated by spaces, and the program decides what to do with them. The books and the manuals of the chapter print a dollar sign as the prompt in front of what the user types, and print the lines under it as the output. The chapter uses this form throughout, starting with the commands that build and start the node (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe command line is the common ground of the people who run nodes: it works on a server without any graphical display, over a remote connection, and in scripts that can be repeated. It also leaves a record, because the text of a command can be copied into a document or a script, which is how the book can show an exact sequence of steps. The helper program of Bitcoin Core is made for it, as is the daemon with its options.\n\nA reader meets the command line in every example of the chapter. The book's commands for starting the node, such as bitcoind with the option for the console, for reading the help, such as bitcoind with the help option, and for querying it, such as bitcoin-cli getblockchaininfo, are all typed this way (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The options of the programs follow the same conventions: a name with a leading hyphen, sometimes with an equals sign and a value, as the help text of the release shows for the options of the daemon (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe shell splits what is typed into words, the first being the program and the others its arguments, which the worked example shows with the standard library's splitting function for the command that asks for the hash of block 1000: three words, the program, the method and the height as text. The program receives them as a list of text values, so a number such as 1000 arrives as text and the program converts it. Quotation marks keep a word with spaces together, which is why the book's alert command and the credentials of the interface are written inside quotes.\n\nThe command line is exact and unforgiving: a misplaced space or quote changes the meaning, and a command copied from a page may contain characters that look the same but are not, such as typographic quotation marks. Commands also run with the privileges of the user who types them, so a command copied from an unknown source can do harm, as the documentation says about configuration changes provided by others (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The history of the terminal may keep secrets typed as arguments, such as a password, which is a reason to use a prompt where one is offered." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#CommandLine> .
chx:S_CommandLineClient a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Command-line client (bitcoin-cli)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Command-line client (bitcoin-cli)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 61 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Client ;
    skos:definition "The command-line client is the program bitcoin-cli, which the installed-files table of Bitcoin Core describes as the tool for making node and wallet RPC calls (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The book calls it a helper and says that it allows experimenting interactively with the capabilities that are also available programmatically via the API (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Given a method name and its parameters, it builds the request, finds the credential, sends the request to the node and prints the answer.\n\nThe client lets a reader try the interface before writing any code. The book starts the section with the command help, which lists all RPC commands; adding the name of a command after help gives the description, the arguments, the result and two examples (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The first example in the book is bitcoin-cli getblockhash 1000, whose result, a block hash, shows that the node is running, accepting commands and holding the block, as the book puts it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nIn the evidence of this version the list of commands printed by release 31.1 has nine categories and 151 entries, and contains the commands that the chapter uses, among them the one for the block hash at a height, the one that returns a block, the one that returns a raw transaction and the one that decodes it (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The hash that the book prints for block 1000 is the double SHA-256 of that block's 80-byte header, which the check in the worked example confirms with the header data of a public explorer: the identifier of the block of height 1000 is 00000000c937983704a73af28acdec37b049d214adbda81d7e2a3dd146f6ed09 (Blockstream, 2026).\n\nThe client reads the data directory to find the cookie, or the configured credential, and sends an HTTP request to the local port. A named form passes parameters by name, and the documentation of the project describes the new command bitcoin rpc as a substitute for the helper with the option for named parameters; the help of the wrapper program says the same (Bitcoin Core, 2026). For a wallet request, the option that selects a wallet makes the client use the wallet-specific path of the interface, which the documentation says must be used when two or more wallets are loaded (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe helper is a convenience and not a separate interface: everything it does is a call to the node, and a typing mistake becomes a request for the wrong thing. The positional form needs the parameters in the exact order, which for a method with many optional parameters is error-prone, and the named form is safer, as the documentation's example for creating a wallet shows (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The book's printed results are from a node at a given height, and a reader's results will differ; the chapter itself warns that the examples assume the node is at least at block 775,072 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#CommandLineClient> .
chx:S_WrapperLibrary a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Wrapper library"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Wrapper library" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 62 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Client ;
    skos:definition "A wrapper library is a package for a programming language that makes the node's interface look like ordinary functions of that language. The book explains that libraries exist in most popular programming languages that wrap the Bitcoin Core API in a way that makes the call a lot simpler, and it chooses python-bitcoinlib for its examples, noting that the library is not part of the Bitcoin Core project and must be installed in the usual way (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The program in the book creates a proxy object, calls getblockchaininfo on it as if it were a method, and prints one member of the result.\n\nA wrapper takes away the work of building the request, finding the credentials, sending the message and decoding the answer, so that the programmer thinks in terms of the node's methods. The book contrasts it with the alternative of building the call with a generic HTTP library as shown with curl (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The price is a dependency: the wrapper must follow the changes of the node, and it is maintained by someone else. The documentation of python-bitcoinlib says that its interface should work with Bitcoin Core 24.0 or later, and the evidence of this version confirms that the book's first program runs with version 0.12.2 on Python 3.14.4 against the release 31.1 (Todd, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe book's three programs, which read the number of blocks, list the outputs of Alice's transaction and add up the outputs of a block, all use a proxy object of this kind (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the evidence of this version the first one was run unchanged against the offline node and printed 0, the number of blocks of that node, which agrees with the answer of the call for blockchain information (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The library's proxy also reads the data directory's cookie file when it is created without arguments (Todd, 2023).\n\nThe trick is in a special method of the Python class. When a program asks the proxy for an attribute that does not exist, such as getblockchaininfo, the class receives its name and returns a function; when that function is called, it builds a JSON message with the member version set to 1.1, the method name, the arguments as parameters and a counter as identifier, sends it as an HTTP POST with the authorization header, checks the error member and returns the result member (Todd, 2023). The worked example reproduces the idea with the standard library only, for the call that asks for the hash of block 1000: it returns the message that the real class would send.\n\nA wrapper hides the protocol and so can hide failures: it raises an error of its own when the node reports one, it needs the node to be running and reachable, and it needs a credential that works, which is the usual cause of failure. A library that is not maintained may lack the newest methods or the newest fields. The version in the book's era, 0.12.2, dates from June 2023, a point the research record of this chapter checks against the package index. And a wrapper is only a client of the node: the quality of the answers is the quality of the node behind it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#WrapperLibrary> .
chx:S_Data a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Data"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Data" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 63 ;
    skos:definition "The data branch is about what a node holds and what the interface shows of it: the transactions that move value, the blocks that gather them into the shared record, and the state of the chain that results. The section of the book on exploring transactions and blocks takes one payment, Alice's purchase from Bob's store, and follows it from a serialized hexadecimal string to a decoded structure, and then goes up a level to a block, which can be referenced either by its height or by its hash (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter treats them as things to look at, and this branch explains what the fields mean.\n\nA student who understands the fields can check the node's answers, which is the point of running a node. The book says that a transaction ID is not authoritative before confirmation, that a block's confirmations tell its depth, and that the median time of the block is a time measurement harder for miners to manipulate (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Each of these statements is a claim about how the system is built, and each can be tested on data that anybody can fetch. The branch therefore pairs each field with a computation that reproduces it from the raw bytes.\n\nThe data are met whenever one asks the node about something that happened on the chain. The evidence of this version contains the real hexadecimal of Alice's transaction, the answer that release 31.1 gives when asked to decode it, the header and the list of transaction identifiers of block 123456, the times of the eleven blocks around it and the outputs of all 1,966 transactions of block 775072, taken from a public explorer, which is a third party's index and not the reader's own node (Blockstream, 2026) (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe branch has three groups. The transaction group covers the serialized form, the decoded form, the witness, the identifiers and the weight. The block group covers the height and hash, the header, the weight, the Merkle root, the subsidy, the target and difficulty, and the median time. The chain state group covers the relations between blocks: the chain with the most work, the amount of work, the confirmations and the reorganization. The computations use only the Python standard library, so that every number printed in the text can be repeated by the reader.\n\nTwo cautions apply to the whole branch. The first is that the data in the book belong to a moment: the confirmations of a block grow with every new block, and the block of the later example is so old that its figures are history only. The second is that the book contains one printed result that does not match its script. The program that adds the outputs of block 775072 prints 10,322.07722534 BTC and explains it as including a reward of 25 BTC, but the reward at that height is 6.25 BTC, and the sum of all outputs of that block is 5,863.00566521 BTC, as the executed checks of this chapter show (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Data> .
chx:S_Transaction a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Transaction"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transaction" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 64 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Data ;
    skos:definition "The transaction group collects the concepts about the objects that carry value. Chapter 2 introduced the transaction with its inputs, outputs and fee; this group looks at the same object as a node sees it, first as a string of bytes, then as a decoded structure with fields, with an identifier and with a cost in block space. The book's example is Alice's payment to Bob, retrieved by its identifier and decoded, in which the transaction used one input and generated two outputs, the payment to Bob and the change back to Alice (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nHow a transaction is stored decides how it is identified and how much it costs. A change in the serialization introduced the witness, a part of the transaction that is not included in the old identifier, and with it a second identifier and a new measure of size. A reader who knows only the simple picture of inputs and outputs will not be able to explain why Alice's transaction has a size of 194 bytes and a virtual size of 143, or why it has two identifiers. This group explains why.\n\nThe group's concepts are met in the output of the two commands of the book: the first returns the serialized transaction in hexadecimal notation, and the second decodes it into the fields shown in the chapter (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). They are met again in every block explorer and every wallet, which show the same fields under other names. The evidence of this version decodes the book's hexadecimal with both the real release 31.1 and a short Python program, and the two agree on every field (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe concepts follow the path from bytes to meaning. Serialization explains the layout of the bytes, decoding the structure that results, the witness the part that the old layout did not have, the identifiers the two hashes that name a transaction, the weight the way the cost is counted, and malleability the reason why an identifier cannot be relied on before the transaction is confirmed." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Transaction> .
chx:S_TransactionSerialization a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Transaction serialization"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transaction serialization" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 65 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Transaction ;
    skos:definition "Serialization is the writing of a structured object as a sequence of bytes, so that it can be stored or sent. The book shows the result for Alice's transaction as 388 hexadecimal digits, which the command getrawtransaction returns when it is asked for the transaction by its identifier (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The same bytes travel between nodes, are stored in the block files and are hashed to produce identifiers. Hexadecimal notation, which chapter 2 explained, writes each byte as two digits.\n\nA precise byte layout is what lets independent programs agree. The specification BIP 144 defines the layout with witness data: a four-byte version, a marker byte that must be zero, a flag byte that must be nonzero, the number of inputs, the inputs, the number of outputs, the outputs, the witnesses and a four-byte lock time (Lombrozo and Wuille, 2016). The marker is zero so that the layout is never mistaken for a valid transaction by a program that does not know about witnesses, because in the old layout a zero would mean a transaction with no inputs (Lombrozo and Wuille, 2016).\n\nA reader can take the hexadecimal in the book and read it by eye. It starts with 01000000, the version 1 written with the least significant byte first, then 00 and 01, the marker and the flag, then 01 for one input, then the 32 bytes of the previous transaction identifier written in reverse order, the four bytes of the index 1, a zero for an empty signature script and ffffffff for the sequence. Then come 02 for two outputs, each with eight bytes of value and a script, and finally the witness and the four zero bytes of the lock time (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe worked example is a parser of about twenty lines that follows this layout. It reads the integers with the least significant byte first, uses a count of one byte for the numbers of inputs, outputs and witness items, and returns the version 1, the marker 0, the flag 1, one input, two outputs and the lock time 0. The values of the two outputs come out as 20000 and 75000 satoshis, and the single witness item has 65 bytes, as the other checks of this branch use. The count that precedes a list is a compact number of one byte when it is less than 253, and uses more bytes after that.\n\nThe layout has a short and a long form. A transaction with no witness is written without the marker and the flag, and that is the form that is hashed to get the old identifier, so one transaction has two serializations. A parser that assumes one of them will misread the other, which is the reason why the call that decodes a raw transaction has an option to tell whether the hexadecimal is in the witness form, and uses a heuristic when it is not told (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The numbers are also written the unexpected way round: byte order in identifiers is reversed between the display and the bytes that are hashed." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#TransactionSerialization> .
chx:S_DecodedTransaction a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Decoded transaction"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Decoded transaction" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 66 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Transaction ;
    skos:definition "A decoded transaction is the structure that results from reading the bytes of a serialized transaction and naming each field. The command decoderawtransaction takes the hexadecimal string and returns a JSON object (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The help text of the current release describes it as returning a JSON object representing the serialized, hex-encoded transaction (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The object has the identifiers, the sizes, the version and the lock time, a list of inputs under the key vin and a list of outputs under the key vout.\n\nDecoding turns an opaque string into something a person can check. The book remarks that the decode shows all the components of the transaction, and that following the identifier of the input to the previous transaction lets a reader walk back through the chain of transactions as the coins pass from one owner to the next (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Anyone who distrusts a wallet or an explorer can run the same decoding on their own node, which is the independent check that the chapter advocates.\n\nIn Alice's transaction the input refers to output number 1 of the earlier transaction 4ac5...3aeb, has an empty signature script, one witness item and the sequence number 4294967295, which is ffffffff; the lock time is 0 and the version 1 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The first output has the value 0.0002 BTC, 20000 satoshis, and a script of the type witness_v1_taproot; the second has 0.00075 BTC, 75000 satoshis, and a script of the type witness_v0_keyhash. The two outputs correspond to the payment to Bob and the change back to Alice (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe decoding code reads the bytes as the serialization concept describes and converts what it reads: the satoshi values become amounts in bitcoin by dividing by 100,000,000, the scripts are shown as assembly and as hexadecimal, the type of each script is recognized from its pattern, and for a known type an address is derived and shown. Each output script also has a descriptor, the field desc, whose checksum the output descriptor concept explains (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The worked example takes the number, the amount and the type of each output from the answer of release 31.1.\n\nThe decoder shows what the bytes say, not whether they are valid or confirmed. A transaction may be well formed and still spend an output that does not exist, or one that is already spent. The values are given as decimal fractions of a bitcoin, with eight decimals, which a floating-point program should not use for money arithmetic. And the book's example gives the address of an output only because the node was able to derive it from the script: an output whose script has no standard form has no address. The call that fetches a transaction by identifier only works for mempool transactions unless the transaction index is on, or the block is named (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#DecodedTransaction> .
chx:S_SegregatedWitness a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Segregated witness"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Segregated witness" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 67 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Transaction ;
    skos:definition "Segregated witness, often written SegWit, is an upgrade that separates the data that prove a spending right, the signatures, from the rest of the transaction. The book's glossary describes it as a proposed upgrade that separates signature data from bitcoin transactions, and as a soft fork, a change that makes the protocol rules more restrictive (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In a transaction the separated data form the witness, a list of items for each input, which BIP 144 writes after the outputs and before the lock time (Lombrozo and Wuille, 2016).\n\nThe upgrade solved two problems. It removed the witness from the data that the transaction identifier covers, so that a third party can no longer change an identifier by changing a signature, a point that the malleability concept develops. And it changed the way block space is measured: BIP 141 defines the weight of a block as the base size times three plus the total size, and limits it to 4,000,000, so witness bytes count one unit each while all the other bytes count four (Lombrozo et al., 2015). Witness data are therefore cheaper than other data.\n\nAlice's transaction uses it. Its input has an empty signature script, and all of its proof is in the witness: one item of 65 bytes, a signature of 64 bytes followed by one byte for the way it was signed (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The decoded form shows the item under the key txinwitness. Of the 194 bytes of the transaction, 125 are the old layout and 69 are the marker, the flag, the number of items, the length of the item and the item itself. The output scripts show the two kinds that the upgrade created: a witness script of version 0 that pays to a key hash, and one of version 1, the taproot type.\n\nSoftware that knows the upgrade finds a witness program in an output script: a version number followed by a short piece of data. For version 0 the program is either 20 bytes, a key hash, or 32 bytes, a script hash; versions 1 to 16 are reserved for future extensions (Lombrozo et al., 2015). The block commits to the witnesses of its transactions through a second tree built from the witness identifiers, and the witness identifier of the coinbase transaction is taken to be all zeros (Lombrozo et al., 2015). The worked example shows the byte accounting: 194 minus 125 is 69, which is the marker and flag, the count and the length, and the 65 bytes.\n\nThe upgrade was designed to be a soft fork: old nodes see an output script with a version and a program and consider anyone able to spend it, while upgraded nodes enforce the new rules, so old nodes keep accepting the chain without understanding the witness (Lombrozo et al., 2015). This also means that a node which does not understand witnesses cannot verify the signatures of such transactions, even though it accepts them, which is a reason to run current software. The upgrade was activated at height 481824 on the main network, a constant that Bitcoin Core's chain parameters record (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#SegregatedWitness> .
chx:S_TransactionWeight a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Transaction weight"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transaction weight" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 68 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Transaction ;
    skos:definition "The weight of a transaction is a measure of the block space it uses, in weight units. BIP 141 defines it as the base transaction size times three plus the total transaction size, and defines the virtual size as the weight divided by four, rounded up to the next integer (Lombrozo et al., 2015). The base size is the size in bytes of the old serialization, without any witness data, and the total size is the size of the new one, with the witness. Equivalently, every byte outside the witness counts four units and every byte inside it counts one.\n\nWeight is the unit in which the limit of a block is set. The source of the consensus rules gives the maximum block weight as 4,000,000 and the witness scale factor as 4, so a block of only old-style bytes can contain 1,000,000 of them, and a block with witnesses can contain more (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Weight is also the basis of the fee rate that chapter 2 introduced: a fee rate in satoshis per virtual byte divides the fee by the weight over four, which is why the decoded answer shows both the size and the virtual size.\n\nThe decoded form of Alice's transaction shows size 194, vsize 143 and weight 569, the three numbers printed in the book and reproduced by release 31.1 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The Python parser of this branch finds that the old layout is 125 bytes and the full one 194. The weight is then 3 times 125 plus 194, which is 569, and the virtual size is 569 divided by 4, which is 142.25, rounded up to 143. The worked example is the same arithmetic, written as four times the base size plus the witness bytes.\n\nWeight units make the witness discount a precise rule. The 69 extra bytes of Alice's transaction, the marker, the flag and the witness, cost 69 units, whereas the 125 base bytes cost 500, so the witness is 35.6 percent of the bytes of the transaction but only 12.1 percent of its weight, which is 69 out of 569. The same transaction written without its witness would weigh 500. The rounding up is why the virtual size is a whole number, and why two transactions can have the same virtual size and different weights.\n\nVirtual bytes are not bytes, and the discount does not reduce the data that nodes must store or send: a node still downloads and stores all 194 bytes. The limit on weight is a consensus rule, whereas the policy of which transactions a node relays has other limits, one of which is the minimum fee rate, so a transaction of valid weight may still not be relayed. A last point is that older material speaks of a block limit of one megabyte, a rule that the upgrade replaced by the limit of weight, and the figure quoted in the book for the block of the later example, 4179 bytes, is far below either." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#TransactionWeight> .
chx:S_TransactionIdentifier a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Transaction identifier (txid and wtxid)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transaction identifier (txid and wtxid)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 69 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Transaction ;
    skos:definition "A transaction identifier is the name of a transaction: the double SHA-256 hash of its serialization, written with the bytes in reverse order. The transaction has two of them. BIP 141 leaves the definition of the txid unchanged, the double SHA-256 hash of the traditional serialization format, and defines a new witness identifier, the wtxid, as the double SHA-256 hash of the serialization with witness data (Lombrozo et al., 2015). In the decoded answer the first appears under txid and the second under hash.\n\nIdentifiers are how everything refers to a transaction: the inputs of later transactions, the lists in blocks, the Merkle tree, the requests to the node. Having a second identifier that covers the witness allows the network to commit to the signatures in the block without changing what the old identifier covers. If no input uses a witness, the two identifiers are equal (Lombrozo et al., 2015). The book's tip adds the warning that a txid is not authoritative, a point that the malleability concept develops (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe book gives the identifier of Alice's transaction as 466200308696215bbc949d5141a49a4138ecdfdfaa2a8029c1f9bcecd1f96177 and the node's decode shows the second one under the key hash as f7cdbc7cf8b910d35cc69962e791138624e4eae7901010a6da4c02e7d238cdac (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The Python parser of this branch computes both from the hexadecimal: it hashes the 125 bytes of the old layout twice with SHA-256 for the txid, and the 194 bytes of the full layout for the wtxid, and reverses the byte order of each result.\n\nThe reversal is the reason why identifiers, which are numbers, are written backwards: the 32-byte hash result is shown with its last byte first, the way the block hashes, which start with many zeros, are conventionally displayed. The Merkle tree of a block is built from txids, and BIP 141 adds a second tree built from wtxids, whose root is committed to in the coinbase transaction (Lombrozo et al., 2015). The coinbase transaction's wtxid is assumed to be 32 zero bytes (Lombrozo et al., 2015).\n\nA txid can be looked up in the block files only with the transaction index or with the name of the block, as the earlier concepts explain, and the book warns that the absence of a txid from the blockchain does not mean the transaction was not processed (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The wtxid is the more specific name, and is the one used when transactions are announced to peers in current protocols. It is also not secret, and not an address: two parties who know the identifier know only which transaction is meant." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#TransactionIdentifier> .
chx:S_TransactionMalleability a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Transaction malleability"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transaction malleability" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 70 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Transaction ;
    skos:definition "Transaction malleability is the possibility that a transaction can be changed by a third party before it is confirmed, so that its identifier changes while what it does stays the same. The book puts it in a tip: a transaction ID is not authoritative, the absence of a txid from the blockchain does not mean that the transaction was not processed, and this is known as transaction malleability because transactions can be modified before confirmation in a block, changing their txids (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The reason is that the identifier hashes data, such as signatures, that someone other than the sender may be able to alter.\n\nA program that watches for a payment by its txid can be misled. If a transaction is changed in transit and the changed version is the one that is confirmed, the original identifier never appears in a block, and a program that waits for it would conclude that the payment failed although the money moved. The book's advice is to treat the txid as authoritative only after confirmation: after the transaction is included in a block, its txid cannot change unless a reorganization removes that block from the best chain, and reorganizations are rare after several confirmations (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe problem was met by exchanges and by protocols that build a chain of unconfirmed transactions, since the child names the parent by its txid and breaks if the parent's txid changes. Segregated witness addresses it for the transactions that use it: because the txid does not cover the witness, a change of the signature changes the wtxid but not the txid (Lombrozo et al., 2015). The worked example shows this on Alice's transaction: it flips one bit of the signature, which makes the transaction invalid but serves to show the identifiers. The wtxid changes, the txid does not, and all the bytes before the witness are unchanged.\n\nThe old identifier covers the version, the inputs, the outputs and the lock time. For inputs that spend a witness output the signature script is empty and the signature is in the witness, outside that hash; so no one can change what the txid covers without changing the transaction's meaning, since the signatures commit to those fields. For old-style inputs the signature script is covered, and the signature encoding could be altered, which is why the BIPs of the time tightened what is accepted. The wtxid covers everything and so does change.\n\nSegregated witness does not make every transaction immune. A transaction that spends an old-style output still has its signature script in the txid, and a transaction can still be replaced by a conflicting one that spends the same inputs, which is a different matter from malleability, since it has different outputs or a different meaning. The wtxid, in turn, can still change with the witness. And the TIP of the book is still true for the careful programmer: use the transaction's confirmation, not only its identifier, to know that a payment was made." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#TransactionMalleability> .
chx:S_Block a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Block"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Block" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 71 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Data ;
    skos:definition "The block group collects the concepts about the unit in which transactions are recorded. A block holds a header, which is a small fixed-size summary, and a list of transactions, which makes up most of its size; the book of the chapter on the blockchain says that the header is 80 bytes while the transactions of a block can total much more (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the chapter on Bitcoin Core the block is explored with two commands: one finds a block's hash from its height, and the other returns the block as a JSON object.\n\nKnowing the block's fields is knowing how the chain is held together. Each block names its parent by the hash of its header, so changing anything in a block changes its hash and breaks the link to the next. The header also commits to the transactions through the Merkle root, to the work that was spent through the target and the nonce, and to the time through the timestamp. The book says of the fields of the answer that some are used for security and proof of work: merkle root, nonce, bits, difficulty and chainwork (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reader meets the group in the answer of the block command for block 123456 in the book: its hash, 13 transactions, a height, the number of confirmations, the version, the Merkle root, the time, the median time, the nonce, the bits, the difficulty, the chain work, the sizes and the list of transaction identifiers (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The evidence of this version confirms the hash of this block from its header, its Merkle root from its 13 identifiers, and its difficulty from its bits, using the public data of an explorer (Blockstream, 2026).\n\nThe concepts of the group go from the outside to the inside. The height and the hash name a block, the header holds what is hashed, the weight measures the size, the Merkle root summarizes the transactions, the subsidy is the new money it creates, the compact target and the difficulty say how hard it was to make, and the median time is the clock rule that bounds its timestamp." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Block> .
chx:S_BlockHeight a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Block height"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Block height" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 72 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Block ;
    skos:definition "The height of a block is its position in the chain: the number of blocks that precede it. The book says that the height tells how many blocks preceded the block, and the glossary of the chapter on the blockchain adds that the first block, the genesis block, is at height 0 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The description in the source code is the same: the genesis block has height 0, and the field of the chain information called blocks gives the height of the chain of most work that has been fully validated (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nHeights give people a convenient name for a block, because they are short and ordered. A hash identifies a block without doubt, but it cannot be read or ordered; a height can be said aloud and compared. The book says that blocks can be referenced either by their height or by their hash, and the first command of the exploration, getblockhash, takes a height and returns the block header hash (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The consensus rules also use heights: they decide when the reward is halved and when an upgrade becomes active.\n\nIn the book the command getblockhash 123456 returns 0000000000002917ed80650c6174aac8dfc46f5fe36480aaef682ff6cd83c3ca (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). For block 1000 it returns 00000000c937983704a73af28acdec37b049d214adbda81d7e2a3dd146f6ed09, which the evidence of this version checks by hashing the block's header (Blockstream, 2026). In the source of Bitcoin Core the heights of the rule changes are constants of the main network, for example 227931 for the rule of BIP 34, 481824 for segregated witness, and 210000 as the interval between halvings of the subsidy (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe height is not stored in the header and is not part of a block's identity: it is the length of the path to the genesis block, and the node counts it in its index. This also means that two blocks may have the same height, if there is a fork, though only one of them can be part of the best chain. The relation to depth is simple: the depth of a block is the height of the tip minus its height plus one. For block 123456, whose book printout says 651742 confirmations, the tip then has height 123456 + 651742 - 1 = 775197, as the worked example computes.\n\nHeight 0 is the first block and not the second, so the number of blocks in a chain is its height plus one. A height is also not a proof: the same height names different blocks on different chains, and BIP 34 made the height a part of the coinbase of each block precisely so that it is unambiguous (Andresen, 2012). The height of the tip changes every ten minutes or so, and any figure quoted in the book, such as the assumption that the node is at least at block 775,072, is a snapshot of that day (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#BlockHeight> .
chx:S_BlockHash a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Block hash"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Block hash" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 73 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Block ;
    skos:definition "The block hash is the identifier of a block. The book on the blockchain explains that the primary identifier of a block is its cryptographic hash, a commitment made by hashing the block header twice through the SHA-256 algorithm, and that the resulting 32-byte hash is called the block hash but is more accurately the block header hash, because only the header is used to compute it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The first block of the chain has the hash 000000000019d6689c085ae165831e934ff763ae46a2a6c172b3f1b60a8ce26f, which the log and the chain information of the release 31.1 also show (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA single hash stands for the entire block, because the header contains the Merkle root of the transactions, and the next block's header contains this hash as the previous block hash. Anyone who knows the hash of the latest block can in principle check the whole chain behind it. The book says that the hash identifies a block uniquely and unambiguously and can be independently derived by any node by hashing the header (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe hashes in the chapter all begin with a long string of zeros: 0000000000002917... for block 123456 and 00000000c9379837... for block 1000. These zeros are the proof of work that the miners did, as chapter 2 explained: the hash of a header had to be below a target (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The worked example takes the header of block 123456 from the explorer's data, with the version, the previous hash, the Merkle root, the time, the bits and the nonce, hashes it, and compares the result with the printed hash; for block 1000 the check in the next concept does the same.\n\nThe header's fields are packed in a fixed order into 80 bytes: the version as four bytes with the least significant first, the 32 bytes of the previous block hash and 32 bytes of the Merkle root, both reversed from their display order, and the time, the bits and the nonce as four bytes each. The packed bytes are put through SHA-256 twice, and the 32-byte result is reversed to produce the familiar display. The book notes that the hash is not included in the block's data structure; each node computes it as the block arrives and may store it in a separate index (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA hash is not an authority. It tells which block is meant, not whether the block is valid or part of the best chain; a block with a very small hash can still be invalid because of a transaction in it. The display order differs from the order used in computation, and a program that forgets to reverse the bytes gets a different value. And the explorer's data used here are a secondary source: the check confirms that the explorer's fields are consistent with each other, which is what the hash promises, not that the explorer is honest about the chain." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#BlockHash> .
chx:S_BlockHeader a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Block header"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Block header" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 74 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Block ;
    skos:definition "The block header is the summary at the head of every block. The book's table lists its six fields and their sizes: the version, four bytes; the previous block hash, 32 bytes, a hash of the previous block in the chain; the Merkle root, 32 bytes, the root of the Merkle tree of the block's transactions; the timestamp, four bytes, the approximate creation time as Unix epoch time; the target, four bytes, a compact encoding of the proof-of-work target; and the nonce, four bytes, arbitrary data used for the proof-of-work algorithm (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The sum, 4 + 32 + 32 + 4 + 4 + 4, is 80 bytes.\n\nThe header is small on purpose. Because it commits to everything else, a program that holds only the headers can follow the chain and its work without the transactions. This is what the node in the book does when it starts: it first fetches the block headers from its peers to find the blockchain with the most proof of work, and afterward continues to download the full blocks (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Nakamoto's paper made the same point about lighter programs: a user needs to keep only a copy of the block headers of the longest proof-of-work chain (Nakamoto, 2008).\n\nThe fields are found under the same names in the answer of the block command: version, merkleroot, time, nonce, bits and previousblockhash, together with the hash itself (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). For block 123456 the version is 1, the time 1305200806 and the nonce 2436437219; its header is the 80-byte string whose double hash is the block hash (Blockstream, 2026). The chain information of the node shows bits and target for the best block as well (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nEach field has a role in the chain. The previous block hash links the block to its parent. The Merkle root ties the transactions to the header. The target says how small the hash has to be, and the nonce is what the miner changes to try again, together with the timestamp and, indirectly, the contents of the coinbase transaction. The timestamp is a Unix time, a count of seconds since the first of January 1970, and it must obey the rules of the median time and of the two-hour limit (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe version field no longer means a version. The book's table says it was originally a version field and its use has evolved; the upgrade mechanism of BIP 9 uses its bits to signal readiness, as the soft fork concept explains (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Wuille et al., 2015). The header's time is set by the miner and is only roughly right. And the header does not contain the number of transactions, the block's height or its hash, which are all derived." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#BlockHeader> .
chx:S_BlockWeight a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Block weight"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Block weight" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 75 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Block ;
    skos:definition "The weight of a block is its size in weight units, calculated like the weight of a transaction: BIP 141 defines it as the base size times three plus the total size, where the base size is the size without witness data and the total size is the size with it (Lombrozo et al., 2015). The block command returns three measurements, which the book describes as the legacy stripped size, the full size, and the size in weight units (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The rule is that the block weight must not exceed 4,000,000 (Lombrozo et al., 2015).\n\nThe limit decides how many transactions a block can hold, and so how much the network can do in ten minutes. It replaced the earlier limit of 1,000,000 bytes of total size with a combined measure, so that witness data, which is cheaper to verify and does not burden the set of unspent outputs, is charged less (Lombrozo et al., 2015). The limit is also a constant in the source: the maximum block weight is 4,000,000, and the witness scale factor is 4 (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nFor block 123456, which was mined years before the upgrade, the book's answer shows a stripped size of 4179, a size of 4179 and a weight of 16716 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). When a block has no witness data, the base size and the total size are the same, so the weight is 3 times 4179 plus 4179, or simply 4 times 4179, which is 16716, as the worked example computes. The explorer's data for the block agree: 13 transactions, size 4179 and weight 16716 (Blockstream, 2026).\n\nThe node computes the weight by serializing the block twice, once without and once with witness data, and combining the sizes; the source shows the three results being filled into the answer as strippedsize, size and weight (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A block with witness data has a total size larger than its stripped size, and a weight between four times the stripped size and the total size times 4, so a full block of heavy-witness transactions can have a total size well above one million bytes while staying below the limit in weight units.\n\nWeight is not the same as size and the two should not be mixed. A tool that reports a block size in bytes and one that reports it in virtual bytes give different numbers for the same block, and a reader who compares them with the limit of 4,000,000 must use the weight. The limit is also a rule for miners, not for relay nodes, which means that a block over the limit is invalid, whereas a transaction is only rejected by policy before that." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#BlockWeight> .
chx:S_MerkleRoot a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Merkle root"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Merkle root" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 76 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Block ;
    skos:definition "The Merkle root is the single hash in a block header that stands for all the transactions of the block. The glossary of the book describes a Merkle tree as a tree constructed by hashing paired data, the leaves, then pairing and hashing the results until a single hash remains, the Merkle root; in Bitcoin the leaves are almost always the transactions of one block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The root is a descendant of all the hashed pairs in the tree, and a block header must include a valid root for the transactions in the block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe root makes the header a commitment to the contents. Changing, adding, removing or reordering any transaction changes the root, which changes the header and so the block hash, and the proof of work would have to be redone. It also allows a short proof of membership: to show that one transaction is in a block, one needs only the hashes along the path from the transaction to the root, not the whole block, a property that Nakamoto's paper describes when it explains how payments can be verified without running a full node (Nakamoto, 2008).\n\nThe answer of the block command shows the root under merkleroot and lists the transaction identifiers under tx; for block 123456 it lists 13 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The evidence of this version takes these 13 identifiers from the explorer, builds the tree and obtains 0e60651a9934e8f0decd1c5fde39309e48fca0cd1c84a21ddfde95033762d86c, which is the Merkle root in the header of the block and the one printed in the book (Blockstream, 2026) (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the header it occupies 32 bytes.\n\nThe tree is built from the bottom. The identifiers of the transactions are the leaves, taken in their bytes in the internal order, which is the reverse of the display order; each pair is joined and hashed twice with SHA-256, giving a level with half as many nodes; and the process repeats until one hash remains. When a level has an odd number of nodes, the last one is paired with itself. With 13 leaves the levels have 13, 7, 4, 2 and 1 nodes, because the lists of 13 and 7 are each extended by a copy of the last element before pairing, as the worked example does.\n\nThe rule of duplicating the last element has a known weakness: two different lists of transactions can give the same root if the later one repeats the last transaction, and for this reason a node must check a block's transactions for duplication in addition to the root. The root is also computed from txids, not wtxids, so it does not commit to the witnesses; the second tree of BIP 141 does (Lombrozo et al., 2015). And a root is not evidence of validity: it proves only that the listed transactions are those that the miner hashed." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#MerkleRoot> .
chx:S_BlockSubsidy a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Block subsidy"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Block subsidy" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 77 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Block ;
    skos:definition "The block subsidy is the amount of new bitcoin that a block creates, which the miner may claim in the first transaction of the block, the coinbase transaction. Bitcoin Core computes it in a short function: the number of halvings is the height divided by the halving interval of 210,000 blocks, the subsidy starts at 50 bitcoins, and it is shifted right once for each halving, which divides it by two each time (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Chapter 2 introduced the block reward, which is this subsidy plus the fees of the block's transactions.\n\nThe schedule is what makes the supply of bitcoin finite and predictable. The amounts are counted in satoshis, 100,000,000 to a bitcoin, so each shift right halves an integer; the sum of all the subsidies over the 64 possible periods is 2,099,999,997,690,000 satoshis, which is slightly below 21 million bitcoins by 2,310,000 satoshis, because of the rounding in the shifts, as the worked checks of this branch compute (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The consensus constant for the maximum amount of money is 21,000,000 times the number of satoshis in a bitcoin (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe book's program that adds the outputs of block 775072 says that the total includes a reward of 25 bitcoins (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The subsidy at that height is not 25: 775072 divided by 210000 is 3 with a remainder, so there have been three halvings and the subsidy is 50 shifted right three times, which is 6.25 bitcoins, or 625000000 satoshis, as the worked example computes. The coinbase transaction of that block in the explorer's data pays out 633799108 satoshis, which is 625000000 plus fees of 8799108, and the sum of the fees of the other 1,965 transactions is exactly that number (Blockstream, 2026).\n\nThe coinbase transaction cannot create more than the subsidy plus the fees of the block, and the amount is checked by every node. The function ends in zero for a height of 64 halvings or more, because a shift by 64 or more is undefined in the language of the program and the code returns zero instead (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A coinbase output also cannot be spent until it is 100 blocks deep, a constant named the coinbase maturity in the consensus source (Bitcoin Core, 2026). For block 775072 the numbers fit: 633,799,108 less 625,000,000 is 8,799,108.\n\nThe book's text mentions a reward of 25 BTC and 0.0909 BTC in fees for the block of its example, and the total of 10,322.07722534 BTC. Neither matches block 775072: its reward is 6.25 BTC, its fees are 0.08799108 BTC, and the sum of all its outputs is 5,863.00566521 BTC, so the printed result does not belong to that block, and a student who compares with an explorer, as the book suggests, will find the difference (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Blockstream, 2026). 25 BTC was the reward for blocks 420,000 to 629,999. The error shows why printed numbers should be checked." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#BlockSubsidy> .
chx:S_CompactTarget a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Compact target (bits)"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Compact target (bits)" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 78 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Block ;
    skos:definition "The compact target is the way in which the header records the proof-of-work target in only four bytes. The target is a 256-bit number that the hash of a valid block must not exceed, as chapter 2 explained for the difficulty target, and a 256-bit number would need 32 bytes. The header's field, called bits, holds instead an exponent in the first byte and a mantissa in the other three. The book's table calls the field a compact encoding of the proof-of-work target (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nFour bytes were enough because the target needs only a few digits of precision: it changes in small steps, and the lower digits would not matter. A compact form keeps the header at 80 bytes. The chain information of the current release shows both the compact form, in the field bits, and the target in full, which the book's printout of 2023 does not have (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The form also has a sign bit, a legacy of the number type it was borrowed from, which Bitcoin Core rejects as a target when it is set.\n\nThe block printed in the book has the bits 1a6a93b3, which is 443192243 in decimal and the value the explorer gives for block 123456 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Blockstream, 2026). The first byte, 0x1a, is 26, and the other three bytes are 0x6a93b3. For the first block the value is 1d00ffff, and the chain information of the evidence node shows it together with the full target, 00000000ffff followed by zeros (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe source decodes the field with two lines: the size is the value shifted right by 24 bits, the word is the value masked by 0x007fffff, and the target is the word shifted left by 8 times the size minus 3 bits, that is, by size minus three bytes (Bitcoin Core, 2026). For 0x1a6a93b3 this puts the three bytes 6a 93 b3 followed by 23 zero bytes, which written in 64 hexadecimal digits is 0000000000006a93b300...00, with 12 leading zero digits, as the worked example prints. The reverse conversion rounds a target down to three significant bytes, so not every number is the target of a block.\n\nBecause only three bytes of the target are kept, the true target that the hash is compared with is the decoded one, not the one the retargeting formula first computed, and a table of difficulties from a program that uses the full-precision number can differ by a tiny amount from one that uses bits. A second caution is the sign bit: the code reduces the mantissa and raises the size if the top bit of the three bytes is set, so values such as 0x1d00ffff and not 0x1d0100.. are seen in practice. And the header's field is named bits in the answer but target in the book's table (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#CompactTarget> .
chx:S_Difficulty a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Difficulty"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Difficulty" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 79 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Block ;
    skos:definition "The difficulty is a number that tells how much harder it is to produce a valid block than it was at the start. The glossary of the book defines it as a network-wide setting that controls how much computation is required to produce a proof of work (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It is defined relative to the easiest allowed target, that of the first block, so the first block has difficulty 1, as the chain information of the evidence node shows, and a block with a target a hundred times smaller has difficulty 100.\n\nThe network keeps the time between blocks near ten minutes by changing the difficulty. The consensus parameters give a target spacing of 10 minutes and a target timespan of two weeks, which is 2016 blocks; after every 2016 blocks the target is multiplied by the time the last 2016 blocks actually took and divided by two weeks, and the change in one step is limited to a factor of four in either direction (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The book says that every node retargets independently, every 2,016 blocks, and notes a small bias of 0.05 percent toward higher difficulty because the calculation uses 2,015 intervals (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nFor block 123456 the answer of the block command shows the difficulty 157416.4018436489 and the bits 1a6a93b3 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The worked example computes the ratio of the two targets: 0xffff times 256 to the power 26 over 0x6a93b3 times 256 to the power 23, which is 157416.4018436489. The explorer gives the same value with one more digit, 157416.40184364893, since the book's printout is rounded to its sixteen significant digits (Blockstream, 2026). In the chain information of release 31.1 the first block has difficulty 1 (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe program in Bitcoin Core computes it from the bits with floating-point arithmetic: it takes 0x0000ffff divided by the mantissa, and multiplies or divides by 256 until the exponent is 29, the value for the easiest target (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Written as an exact ratio the formula is the same as the example. The retargeting code in pow.cpp clamps the timespan to between a quarter and four times the target timespan, multiplies the old target by the timespan, divides by two weeks, and never allows a target above the proof-of-work limit (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nDifficulty is a result and not a setting that anyone chooses: it follows the hash rate of miners with a delay of up to two weeks, so a sudden fall in mining power slows blocks until the next adjustment. It is also relative: a difficulty of 157,416 at one time and one of 53,911,173,001,054 at another, the figure printed in the book's cookie example, say how the work grew, not how much work either was (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A change in a test network may follow different rules, since the parameters allow a minimum-difficulty exception there (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Difficulty> .
chx:S_MedianTimePast a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Median time past"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Median time past" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 80 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Block ;
    skos:definition "The median time past, often written MTP, is a clock that the chain defines for itself. For a block it is the median, the middle value in sorted order, of the timestamps of that block and the ten before it. Bitcoin Core's source states the number of blocks as the constant 11, sorts the times and returns the element in the middle (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The book of the chapter on mining says that no node will accept a block with a time less than or equal to the median time of the last 11 blocks, called the median time past (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe timestamps in headers are set by the miners, who have some latitude, and the book explains the danger: a miner could set the time in the future to lower the difficulty, or in the past to make it look as if blocks were slow, or to collect the fees of transactions with time locks that have not matured (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The median of eleven blocks is hard for one miner to move; BIP 113 made it the time that the checks on time locks use, so that the incentive to lie disappears (Kerin and Friedenbach, 2015).\n\nThe answer of the block command shows the median under mediantime. For block 123456 it is 1305197900, while the block's own timestamp is 1305200806, which is 2906 seconds later (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Blockstream, 2026). The chain information of the node shows the same field for the best block, and for the first block, which has no predecessors, the median equals its own time (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The source applies the median of the previous block to check a new block's time, with the error time-too-old (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe worked example takes the times of blocks 123446 to 123456, the eleven blocks ending with block 123456, sorts them and picks the sixth, which is 1305197900, the time of block 123451. This is exactly the value the node reports. The time-lock rule works one block later: the median used when checking a new block is that of its parent, and a block whose time is not greater than it is rejected (Bitcoin Core, 2026) (Kerin and Friedenbach, 2015). The consensus time is thus usually about an hour behind the wall clock, as the book notes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe book describes the field as the median time of the 11 blocks that precede the block, but the code includes the block itself. The eleven blocks that precede block 123456 are blocks 123445 to 123455, whose median is 1305195947, not the 1305197900 that the node prints, so the sentence of the chapter is slightly off, and the median of the chapter on mining is the one that the field shows (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Timestamps are also not required to increase from block to block, only to exceed the median, so a sorted list of times can differ from the order of blocks." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#MedianTimePast> .
chx:S_ChainState a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Chain state"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Chain state" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 81 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Data ;
    skos:definition "The chain state group collects the concepts that describe the blocks as a whole: which chain is the true one, how much work it represents, how deep a block is in it, and what happens when the node changes its mind. The word has a second meaning in Bitcoin Core, where the chain state is also the name of the database of unspent outputs, as the earlier concept on the set of unspent outputs explained; here the word means the shape of the chain (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA blockchain is not a single list but a tree, since two miners may find blocks at about the same time. The rule that resolves the tree is simple, and everything a user can trust depends on it. The book says that by selecting the greatest-cumulative-work valid chain all nodes eventually achieve network-wide consensus, and that temporary discrepancies between chains are resolved as more work is added (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The concepts of this group make that rule precise.\n\nThe group shows in the answers of the node: the chain work in the chain information and in the block data, the confirmations in the block data and the transaction data, and the reorganization only when the best chain changes, an event that the log records. The book's tip about transaction IDs refers to it, since a txid can change only if a reorganization removes the block that holds the transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe order is from the rule to its consequences. The best chain is the one with the most work, the chain work is the measure of that work, the confirmations measure how many blocks lie above a transaction, and a reorganization is the switch from one chain to another. Together they explain why six confirmations are considered enough, as the glossary of the book states, and why a transaction in a recent block is not final (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#ChainState> .
chx:S_BestChain a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Best chain"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Best chain" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 82 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ChainState ;
    skos:definition "The best chain is the chain of valid blocks that a node regards as the true record. Nakamoto's paper states the rule as the longest proof-of-work chain, the one that represents the greatest effort (Nakamoto, 2008). The book of the chapter on mining restates it precisely as the greatest-cumulative-work valid chain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The end of the best chain is called its tip, and the node's chain information reports the height of the most-work fully validated chain as the number of blocks (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nWithout such a rule nodes would not agree, and without agreement there would be no single record of who owns what. Because blocks are found by a random process, two miners sometimes find a block at nearly the same time, and the chain forks: the glossary of the book describes this accidental fork as the situation when two or more blocks have the same height (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The rule tells every node which branch to follow, and as the next block is found on one of them, that branch gains work and the other is abandoned.\n\nA node applies the rule whenever a new block arrives. The book describes it: the node looks at the block's previous block hash and tries to find that parent in the existing blockchain; most of the time the parent is the tip of the best chain, and the new block extends it; sometimes it does not, and then the node attaches the header to a secondary chain and compares the work of the two (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The setting of the block header on a branch and the fields of the answer, such as confirmations equal to -1 for a block that is not on the main chain, show this to the user (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nWork, not length, decides. The chain work concept shows how to measure it: a block with a smaller target represents more expected hashes. The worked example compares an invented chain A of three blocks at the first block's target with an invented chain B of two blocks at a target four times smaller. Chain A has three units of work and chain B has two blocks of four units each, a ratio of 8 to 3, so B has about 2.67 times the work, although it is shorter, and a node would follow B. These numbers are made up for the illustration.\n\nThe best chain is a local judgment that nodes make with the same rule, not an announcement. At any moment two honest nodes can briefly hold different tips, and a payment in a block at the tip is not final. The longest-chain wording of the paper is a shorthand that was precise in a time when every block had the same difficulty, and the more exact wording is the one with the most work. Also, the rule applies only to valid blocks, and a chain with more work that contains an invalid block is not the best chain." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#BestChain> .
chx:S_ChainWork a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Chain work"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Chain work" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 83 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ChainState ;
    skos:definition "The chain work is the total amount of proof of work behind a block, expressed as the expected number of hashes needed to produce the chain up to it. The help text of the block command defines it as the expected number of hashes required to produce the chain up to this block, in hexadecimal (Bitcoin Core, 2026). It is the sum, over all blocks from the first to this one, of the work of each block, and the work of a block is the expected number of hashes needed to find a hash below its target.\n\nThis is the quantity that nodes compare to choose the best chain. The source of Bitcoin Core stores it in the index entry of each block, described as the total amount of work, the expected number of hashes, in the chain up to and including that block (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Because it adds numbers that depend only on the targets in the headers, it is the same in every node, and a node can compute the work of a chain from its headers alone, before having any transactions, which is why the node of the book fetches headers first (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe chain information of the evidence node shows chainwork 0000...0100010001 for the first block, and the book's printout has the same ending for a node that had only the first block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The block data of block 123456 in the book shows a much longer string ending in 541788211ac227bc, abbreviated in the printout (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The unit is hashes, so the value 0x100010001, which is 4295032833, means that about 4.3 billion hashes are expected to find the first block.\n\nThe work of one block is computed in the source as 2 to the power 256 divided by the target plus one, with a trick to avoid the number 2 to the power 256, which a 256-bit integer cannot hold: it computes the complement of the target divided by the target plus one, and adds one (Bitcoin Core, 2026). For the first block's target, 0xffff times 256 to the power 26, the result is 4295032833, which is hexadecimal 100010001, exactly the chain work in the node's answer, as the worked example and the checks show. The work of a block with a target of 0x6a93b3 shifted by 23 bytes is about 16.7 trillion hashes.\n\nThe chain work counts expected work and not work actually done: the miner of a block may have been lucky or unlucky. It is also not a measure of the value of the chain, only of its cost to reproduce. The number is large and is shown in hexadecimal with leading zeros, which makes it easy to misread; a comparison of two such strings is a comparison of numbers with the same number of digits. And it is a quantity that the node computes itself from the headers, so it is not something that a peer tells it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#ChainWork> .
chx:S_Confirmation a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Confirmation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Confirmation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 84 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ChainState ;
    skos:definition "A confirmation is a block added at or above the block that contains a transaction. The glossary of the book says that once a transaction is included in a block it has one confirmation, as soon as another block is mined on the same blockchain it has two, and so on (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The block command shows the number under confirmations and the book explains that it tells the depth of the block, how many blocks have been built on top of it, indicating the difficulty of changing any of the transactions in it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nConfirmations are how a user decides when a payment is safe enough. A transaction in the mempool has none and can be replaced or dropped; one in a block at the tip has one and can still be undone by a reorganization; with each block on top, undoing it needs more work. The glossary says that six or more confirmations is considered sufficient proof that a transaction cannot be reversed, and the book's tip says that reorganizations are rare after a transaction has several confirmations (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). How many are enough depends on the value.\n\nIn the book the block 123456 has 651742 confirmations, and the source shows how the node computes the number: the height of the tip minus the height of the block plus one, or -1 if the block is not on the main chain (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The worked example reverses it: if the tip is at height 775197, the block at 123456 has 775197 - 123456 + 1 = 651742 confirmations, and the tip itself has one. The call for a raw transaction also reports confirmations for a transaction in a block (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nEach new block extends the chain that already contains the transaction, so the number grows by one with every block on the best chain. If a different branch overtakes the chain, the block that held the transaction leaves the best chain and its confirmations become -1 in the block data, until the transaction is included again in a block of the new chain. An attacker who wants to reverse a transaction with k confirmations has to produce a chain with more work than the honest chain from the block before it, which is the argument that Nakamoto's paper makes with probabilities (Nakamoto, 2008).\n\nA confirmation is a count, not a guarantee; its meaning grows with the work of the blocks, and a confirmation in a low-difficulty chain is worth less. The conventional six is a convention of the book and of its era, not a rule of the protocol, and a large payment may warrant waiting for more. Another trap is that different programs count differently: some start from zero for a transaction in the tip block, while Bitcoin Core counts that block as the first confirmation (Bitcoin Core, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Confirmation> .
chx:S_Reorganization a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Chain reorganization"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Chain reorganization" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 85 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ChainState ;
    skos:definition "A reorganization is the event in which a node changes which chain it regards as the best. The book of the chapter on mining describes it: when the new block does not extend the best chain, the node attaches its header to a secondary chain and compares the work; if the secondary chain is now the best chain, the node will reorganize its view of confirmed transactions and available UTXOs, and a miner will construct a candidate block extending the new chain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The blocks that were on the old chain and are not on the new one leave the best chain.\n\nReorganizations are the mechanism by which the network resolves forks without any authority. They matter to a user because they are the only way in which a confirmed transaction can become unconfirmed. The book's tip says the txid of a confirmed transaction cannot change unless there is a reorganization that removes the block from the best chain, and that such reorganizations are rare after a transaction has several confirmations (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Most reorganizations are only one block deep.\n\nA reader meets the term in the book's discussion of identifiers and of mining, in the documentation of the node's log, which records the switch, and in wallets, which may show a payment as unconfirmed again. It is also the reason why a node keeps for each block data to undo it, the files called rev in the data directory, which Bitcoin Core's file documentation describes as block undo data (Bitcoin Core, 2026). And the alert command can be triggered by a related event, when the node finds an invalid chain with more work than its own (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nWhen the new chain has more work, the node disconnects the blocks of the old branch from the tip down to the point where the branches split, which restores the outputs those blocks spent and removes the ones they created in the set of unspent outputs, and then connects the blocks of the new branch. The transactions of the disconnected blocks that are not in the new blocks go back to the mempool, where they may be mined again. The worked example represents the two branches by invented sets of transactions: tx2 and tx3 lose their confirmation, tx4 gains one, and tx1 is confirmed in both, and so never changes.\n\nA reorganization does not undo a payment by itself: the transaction usually returns to the pool and is confirmed again in a later block, unless it conflicts with a transaction in the new chain, for instance one that spent the same output. The dangerous case is a double spend, in which the sender pays twice and tries to make the second payment win, and this is the attack that the number of confirmations guards against. A last caution is the scale: after an attack, a deeper reorganization needs more work than the honest network, and the argument depends on no single party controlling most of it (Nakamoto, 2008)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Reorganization> .
chx:S_Fork a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Fork"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Fork" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 86 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ChainState ;
    skos:definition "A fork is a point where the chain of blocks divides into two branches. The glossary of the book defines the fork, also called an accidental fork, as the case in which two or more blocks have the same block height, forking the blockchain, which typically occurs when two or more miners find blocks at nearly the same time (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The worked example has a genesis block G with two children A1 and B1 at height 1 and a further block A2 on top of A1, so that the chain has two tips, A2 and B1, and the longer branch is the one that ends in A2.\n\nForks are how a distributed network without a central clock behaves, and they are resolved by the rule that a node follows the branch with the most proof of work. The shorter branch is abandoned, its blocks become stale, and the transactions in them return to the pool, which is what a reorganization does. Understanding the accidental fork is what makes the advice about waiting for confirmations sensible, since the block that ends a payment's journey is safe only when a branch has grown on top of it.\n\nThe word is used in two other senses in the chapter. A soft fork is a change of the rules that tightens them, so that the nodes that have not upgraded still accept the blocks of those that have. A hard fork, in the glossary's words, is a permanent divergence in the blockchain, which commonly occurs when non-upgraded nodes cannot validate blocks created by upgraded nodes that follow newer consensus rules (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The glossary adds that it must not be confused with a software fork or a Git fork, the copy of a repository, which has nothing to do with the chain.\n\nAt a fork each node has seen the blocks in a different order and may have a different tip until one branch is extended. The node stores both branches in its block index and tracks the work of each, and when a branch overtakes the other it switches, as the concept of reorganization explains. In the permanent case, nodes on the two sides follow different rules, so that no branch can overtake the other from the point of view of the other side, and the chain stays divided.\n\nNot every split is a fork of the chain. Two nodes can disagree for a moment about the tip because a block has not reached one of them yet, and this is normal, not a fork. And a software fork, in which somebody copies the code and changes it, creates a new program but not a new chain unless its rules are incompatible with the old ones and someone runs it on the same network." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Fork> .
chx:S_Ecosystem a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Ecosystem"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Ecosystem" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 87 ;
    skos:definition "The ecosystem branch is about the software around the reference implementation and about how a person can trust what they download. The last section of the chapter, on alternative clients, libraries and toolkits, says that there are many alternative clients, libraries, toolkits and even full-node implementations in the Bitcoin ecosystem, written in a variety of programming languages that offer programmers native interfaces in their preferred language (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter ends by sending the reader back to their own running node, and this branch asks two questions that follow from that: how do I know that the program I run is the program the project published, and what else could I use to talk to Bitcoin if the reference implementation does not fit my work.\n\nA node is only as trustworthy as the software it runs, because the node is the thing that checks the rules on the owner's behalf. If the program has been altered on its way to the computer, every answer it gives is worthless, however carefully the owner reads the output. The branch therefore treats the download as the first link of the chain of verification, before the first block is validated, and treats the choice of a library as a decision that can be examined with data rather than with taste.\n\nThe evidence for this branch comes from the real release 31.1 of Bitcoin Core: the file of checksums that the project publishes next to its programs, the file of signatures on it, and the documentation of the project's own tools for checking them (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The second kind of evidence is the data of the package registries on the toolkits that the book lists, which were queried on the day this chapter was prepared (Package registries, 2026). Both are saved in the repository of the course, so that the statements of the branch can be recomputed instead of believed.\n\nThe branch is organised in one group, assurance, with seven concepts. A checksum and an OpenPGP signature are the two mechanisms; the verification of a release combines them; a reproducible build is the project's way of letting several people arrive at the same files independently; a package registry is where the libraries of the ecosystem are published; and the alternative toolkits are the libraries themselves. Each concept is explained with a computation that anybody can run on the saved files." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Ecosystem> .
chx:S_Assurance a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Assurance"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Assurance" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 88 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Ecosystem ;
    skos:definition "Assurance is the group of concepts that answer the question how do I know. In this chapter it covers the evidence one can gather about a program before trusting it, namely the checksum published for the download, the signatures on that checksum and the build that makes the files reproducible, and the evidence one can gather about a library before depending on it, namely its latest release in a package registry. The common feature is that each statement can be checked by a computation on saved files and does not depend on the reputation of whoever makes it.\n\nThe distinction between checking and trusting is the course's theme in miniature. The documentation of the build tooling of Bitcoin Core puts it as the wish to audit and reproduce the toolchain instead of blindly trusting binary downloads (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A reader who has taken this chapter's own advice and runs a node has made the same choice for the data: instead of trusting a block explorer, the reader verifies the chain. Assurance extends the choice to the software that does the verifying, and it also shows where the choice ends, since some trust always remains and the useful question is which trust is left.\n\nThe group is met when somebody downloads a release, when a project publishes one, and when a developer chooses a library. The saved check of release 31.1 recomputed the checksum of the Linux program archive and counted 11 good signatures on the file of checksums, using 38 builder keys taken from the signature repository of the project (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The registry data separates the toolkits of the book that published a release within a year of the query from those that did not (Package registries, 2026). These are small and concrete results, and the individual concepts of the group show how they are obtained and what they do not prove.\n\nNone of the results of this group is a proof of safety. A matching checksum shows that two files are the same, not that either is harmless, and a good signature shows that a key signed, not who holds the key. A recent release in a registry shows activity, not quality. The concepts below state the limit of each check next to the check." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Assurance> .
chx:S_ReleaseVerification a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Release verification"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Release verification" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 89 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Assurance ;
    skos:definition "Release verification is the check of a downloaded program against what the project published, done before the program is run. The project publishes, for each release, a file named SHA256SUMS that lists a checksum for every file of the release, and next to it a file of signatures on that list (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The check has two steps: first that the list is genuine, which is decided by the signatures, and second that the downloaded file has the checksum listed for it. The book's chapter does not contain this step, since the name of the checksum file does not occur in it, so the material here comes from the documentation of the project.\n\nA downloaded program runs with the rights of the person who starts it, so a file that was altered on the way is the most direct way to compromise a node. The documentation of the project explains that, as of version 22.0, releases are signed by a number of public keys on the basis of the signature repository, that the end user decides which of these keys to trust, and that the downloaded programs are then hashed and compared to the signed list of checksums (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The user, and not the web site that hosts the file, is the party who decides whom to believe, which is why the check protects against a web site or a mirror that has been tampered with.\n\nThe saved check of this chapter was made on release 31.1 and recomputed on the day of preparation. The file of checksums lists 28 files, from archives for several processor families and for Windows and macOS, to debug archives and the source archive, and the Linux archive for the common 64-bit processors is listed with the checksum b80d9c3e04da78fb6f0569685673418cf686fadba9042d926d13fb87ff503f9e. The file downloaded and hashed again has the same checksum and the size 90293352 bytes. The file of signatures holds 11 signature blocks, and the signature-checking program of the saved run reported 11 good signatures after importing 38 builder keys (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The worked example reads the saved files and prints the comparison.\n\nThe project ships a script for the whole procedure, which first checks whether the file of checksums is valid based upon a plurality of signatures and then downloads the files named in it and checks that their hashes are as expected (Bitcoin Core, 2026). It can be told to require a number of good signatures, for example at least 10 from a given set of trusted keys, and then the list is accepted only if enough of the people the user chose have signed the same list (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Done by hand, the procedure is the same in substance: compute the checksum of the archive, find its line in the list, and verify the signatures on the list.\n\nThe check has a limit that the saved evidence states itself: the keys used for the 11 good signatures came from the signature repository of the project and not from an independent source, so the result shows consistency and does not establish trust in the keys. A careful user obtains the keys of people they have reason to trust by a different path from the one that delivered the file, and then insists on a threshold. A matching checksum alone, from the same web page as the file, would prove only that the page is consistent with itself. The check also says nothing about whether the software has bugs, only that it is the software the builders produced." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#ReleaseVerification> .
chx:S_Checksum a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Checksum"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Checksum" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 90 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Assurance ;
    skos:definition "A checksum is a short value computed from the whole content of a file, which can be compared with a value computed elsewhere to decide whether the two files are the same. The checksums used for Bitcoin Core's releases are the SHA-256 hash function that chapter 2 explained, applied to the bytes of the file. The standard that defines it describes the output as a message digest, and says that the algorithms are secure because it is computationally infeasible to find a message that corresponds to a given digest or two different messages that produce the same digest (Eastlake and Hansen, 2011). For SHA-256 the digest has 32 bytes, which is shown as 64 hexadecimal digits.\n\nA checksum makes comparison cheap. Two files of ninety million bytes cannot be compared by looking at them, and sending one to a person who holds the other would be as costly as downloading it again, but their two digests of 64 characters can be compared in a moment, in a message or on a page. The same standard states the property that makes this useful: any change to a message in transit will, with very high probability, result in a different message digest (Eastlake and Hansen, 2011). A flipped bit in a bad download is therefore detected as surely as a deliberate change.\n\nThe file SHA256SUMS of release 31.1 has one line for each of its 28 files, with the checksum, a gap and the name of the file, and the saved run of this chapter computed the checksum of the Linux archive with the program sha256sum (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The archive has the checksum b80d9c3e04da78fb6f0569685673418cf686fadba9042d926d13fb87ff503f9e, which was computed again for this chapter. The standard's own test vector is the message abc, whose digest begins ba7816bf8f01cfea, and the worked example computes it with the python hashing module and compares it to the standard's text.\n\nThe way a checksum behaves is best seen on a small change. The digest of abc begins ba7816bf8f01cfea, whereas that of abd, which differs in one character, begins a52d159f262b2c6d, and 58 of the 64 hexadecimal digits of the two digests differ, close to the 60 that would be expected to differ between two unrelated values, since each digit differs with probability 15 in 16. There is no way to predict which digits will change, and no way to make a file with a chosen digest, which is what the words computationally infeasible in the standard mean.\n\nA checksum detects change; it does not say who made the file. If an attacker can replace the file, the attacker can replace the line of checksums that sits on the same server, and the check will pass. That is why the project signs the list of checksums, and why the signature, not the checksum, is what ties the list to people. The second thing to watch is the comparison: a checksum is compared as text, and a copy that changed a letter or lost a character will fail, while a glance at the first few characters is not a check at all, since the standard's guarantee holds for the whole value." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Checksum> .
chx:S_OpenPgpSignature a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "OpenPGP signature"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "OpenPGP signature" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 91 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Assurance ;
    skos:definition "OpenPGP is a standard for the message formats used in secure messages and signed files. Its specification says that OpenPGP provides encryption with public key or symmetric cryptographic algorithms, digital signatures, compression and key management (Wouters et al., 2024). An OpenPGP signature is the digital signature of chapter 2 in this format: the signer uses a secret key to produce a value from the content that anybody holding the matching public key can check. In this chapter the content is the list of checksums of a release, and the signers are the builders who built the release.\n\nThe signature gives the checksum list an author. Without it the list is an assertion by whoever controls the web server, and with it the list is an assertion by a key, which the user can decide to trust or not. Several signatures on the same list add something more: a list that is signed by several independent builders is accepted only if each of them produced the same files, so that an attacker would have to compromise many of them. The documentation of the project describes this as a check based upon a plurality of signatures (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe file SHA256SUMS.asc of release 31.1 contains 11 signature blocks, each starting with a line of the form BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE and ending with a matching END line, and the saved run reported 11 good signatures after the 38 builder keys of the signature repository were imported (Bitcoin Core, 2026). These are detached signatures: the specification explains that some applications use so-called detached signatures, where a file travels with a second file that holds one or more signature packets stored separately from the data for which they are a signature (Wouters et al., 2024). The file of checksums is the data and the file with the ending asc is the signatures.\n\nThe saved run counted, for the signatures on the list, the checks that reported a good signature, and the count was 11. A good signature is a mathematical statement, that the holder of the secret key signed exactly these bytes, and it is checked in the way chapter 2 described for transactions: the public key confirms that the value was produced from this content. It says nothing yet about who the holder is. The step from key to person is the trust decision, which in this design is made by each user for themselves, as the documentation of the project's script says when it tells the user to decide which keys to trust (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA good signature from a key that was imported from the same place as the file proves little about authenticity, and the saved check of this chapter states this limit in its own output. Keys also expire or are revoked, and the specification of the format has fields and packets for both, so a signature by a revoked key should not be counted (Wouters et al., 2024). The third thing to watch is the difference between this signature and the one in a bitcoin transaction: both use public-key cryptography and both are called signatures, but the OpenPGP one follows a general file format, whereas the transaction signature is a part of the rules of the network and is checked by every node." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#OpenPgpSignature> .
chx:S_ReproducibleBuild a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Reproducible build"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Reproducible build" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 92 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Assurance ;
    skos:definition "A reproducible build is a way of compiling a program such that everybody who follows the same steps on the same source gets exactly the same files, byte for byte. The build files of Bitcoin Core are described by the project as bootstrappable builds that allow the user to audit and reproduce the toolchain instead of blindly trusting binary downloads, and they achieve this by using Guix, which the documentation calls a functional package manager (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The toolchain, the compiler and its libraries, is itself part of what is reproduced, so that the result does not depend on the machine of the builder.\n\nReproducibility turns a download into something that can be verified from the source. If the release files could depend on the builder's machine, nobody could say whether a file differed from the source because of the machine or because of an attack. If they cannot, then a builder who rebuilds the release and obtains the same checksums as the project has confirmed that the published files correspond to the published source, and the more builders do it, the harder it is for any single one of them to be tricked or bribed.\n\nThe documentation describes the steps the builders follow. After a successful build the checksums of the outputs are gathered into files named SHA256SUMS in the output directories; each builder then attests to the outputs, which means that the signed lists go into the signature repository, and other builders can check them against their own with a verification command (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The release process documents the same sequence, including the cloning of the signature repository and the commit of each builder's signed list to it (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The signature repository is thus both the store of the attestations and the place where the keys of the builders sit.\n\nThe reproducibility rests on removing every source of difference between builds. The documentation mentions, among the options of the build, an override of the reference timestamp used for bit-for-bit reproducibility, which fixes the one value that would otherwise change with every run of the build (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Each builder then signs the checksums of what they obtained, and the project's verification script counts the signatures of a list: a list with many good signatures from different builders is a list that many people obtained independently from the same source.\n\nReproducibility is evidence about the build and not about the source: the builders confirm that the binary matches the source, and they do not read the source for bugs. The check also depends on the keys: if the keys of the builders are not independent of the project, the attestations are weaker than they appear. Finally, a reproducible build is expensive to do, and most users will not do it; they rely on the signatures of the few that do, which is a delegation of trust, though one that can be checked by any user who is willing to spend the time." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#ReproducibleBuild> .
chx:S_PackageRegistry a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Package registry"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Package registry" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 93 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Assurance ;
    skos:definition "A package registry is a public service where the authors of a programming language's libraries publish them, so that others can install them by name and version. The queries of this chapter used six: npm for the JavaScript toolkits, PyPI for the Python ones, the module proxy of the Go language for btcd, the crates.io registry for the Rust crate, Maven Central for the two projects of the Java and Scala headings, and NuGet for NBitcoin (Package registries, 2026). The registry records the versions of a library and, in most cases, the dates at which they were published.\n\nThe registry is the place to look when choosing a library, because it holds the facts that a list in a book cannot keep up to date. The book's list was written at one moment, and the registry shows what has happened since: whether the library still publishes releases, how recent the latest one is, and which version a new project would get by default. A query to a registry is a computation on public data, which can be repeated by anybody, and it replaces an impression such as the library seems abandoned with a date.\n\nThe saved query of this chapter was made on 2 October 2026 and covers ten entries, one for each project of the book's list except Bitcoin Core itself. For python-bitcoinlib the latest version is 0.12.2, from 3 June 2023, and the package index shows the same date as the one in the project's metadata read earlier (Package registries, 2026) (Todd, 2023). The Rust crate named bitcoin has the version 0.32.102 dated 22 July 2026, and the Python package pycoin has the version 0.92718.20260405, whose number contains its date. The worked example lists the registry and latest version for two of the registries.\n\nEach registry has an interface that answers a request with a document, usually in the JSON format, listing a library's versions and times, and the script of the chapter's evidence reads the latest version and its date from it. The names of the fields differ between registries: npm lists the latest version under a tag, PyPI lists the files of the release with an upload time, the Go proxy returns a version with a time, and Maven Central returns a timestamp for the newest artifact (Package registries, 2026). The script therefore has one small function for each registry, and it records an error rather than guessing when a registry does not answer.\n\nThe date of a registry entry is the date of the registry's record and may differ from the date of the work: the NuGet listing used here gives only the list of versions, so the entry for NBitcoin has the latest version 10.0.14 and no date, and the chapter makes no claim about its age. A recent release also does not mean that a library is correct, secure or maintained for the use the reader has in mind. The registries themselves are trusted intermediaries, and an install from a registry is an act of trust of the same kind as the download of a release, though without the signatures the Bitcoin Core project provides." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#PackageRegistry> .
chx:S_AlternativeToolkit a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Alternative toolkit"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Alternative toolkit" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 94 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Assurance ;
    skos:definition "An alternative toolkit is a library, a client or even a full-node implementation, other than Bitcoin Core, that lets a programmer work with Bitcoin in a language of their choice. The last section of the chapter lists them by language: Bitcoin Core for C and C++, bcoin, Bitcore and BitcoinJS for JavaScript, bitcoinj for Java, python-bitcoinlib and pycoin for Python, btcd for Go, rust-bitcoin for Rust, bitcoin-s for Scala and NBitcoin for C#, which makes eleven projects under eight language headings (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book adds that many more libraries exist and more are created all the time (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA toolkit saves the programmer from writing the serialization, the hashing and the signing again. The wrapper library of the interface branch is one kind; the others on the list do more, such as building and signing transactions without a node. The choice among them matters because a bug in the code that serializes a transaction or computes a hash is a bug in the money handling of the program, and because a toolkit that no longer follows the rules of the network will fail at some upgrade.\n\nThe registries show that the upkeep of the listed toolkits varies. On the day of the query, the latest release of bitcoinjs-lib was dated 7 September 2026, of btcd 24 July 2026, of the Rust bitcoin crate 22 July 2026 and of pycoin 5 April 2026, all within a year; the latest releases of the Bitcore library, bitcoinj and bitcoin-s were between January and July 2025; python-bitcoinlib's latest was from June 2023, and the latest npm release of bcoin was from 13 July 2018 (Package registries, 2026). The worked example turns three of the dates into ages in days.\n\nTo judge a toolkit with data, one reads from the registry the latest version and the date, from the project's documentation what version of Bitcoin Core it supports, and from its repository how recently it changed. The saved metadata of python-bitcoinlib states that its RPC interface should work with Bitcoin Core v24.0 or later, and the run of release 31.1 in this chapter confirms that for the calls tried (Todd, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The age of the latest release is therefore a starting point for a question and not a verdict, since a small, stable library may need few releases.\n\nThree limits apply to the figures. The registry shows the packages that carry a name, and the book's names of the projects do not always match the package names: BitcoinJS appears here as bitcoinjs-lib and Bitcore as bitcore-lib, and the choice of the package is the author's. The data are from one day, and the figures for age will be different when the query is repeated. And the book's list is a list of existing libraries and not a recommendation; the book itself says only that it lists some of the best libraries, clients and toolkits (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#AlternativeToolkit> .
chx:S_Semantics a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Semantics"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Semantics" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 95 ;
    skos:definition "The semantics branch connects the chapter to the course's wider theme, the meaning of data. A node answers every request in the JSON text format, a structure of names and values, which a program can read but whose names carry no meaning of their own; the field called height in a block's answer means what the documentation says it means, and nothing in the text states it. The technologies of the semantic web were designed for this gap, and the branch uses the chapter's data to show two of their ideas, that data can be given an identity that others can refer to, and that the origin of a piece of data can be described.\n\nA reader who has run a node knows why the node's answer is worth trusting: it is the answer of a program that verified the data itself. Chapter 2 introduced the provenance graph for describing where data came from, and the node is the clearest case of a source whose provenance can be stated in a sentence. The branch states it, and also shows how the JSON that the node returns can be turned into linked data by adding a context, which is a small step that makes the same text interpretable by programs that know nothing about Bitcoin.\n\nThe branch is met in the examples of this chapter, where the node's answers to the block and transaction commands are JSON texts whose keys the reader has to interpret. The standards involved are the JSON-LD 1.1 recommendation of the World Wide Web Consortium for the format and the same consortium's PROV ontology for provenance, and both were read for this chapter (World Wide Web Consortium, 2020) (Lebo et al., 2013).\n\nThe branch has one group, linked data, with three concepts. Verified data is the idea that makes a node's answer authoritative, and is described in the vocabulary of provenance. The context of a JSON-LD document is the mechanism that maps the names of a JSON text to identifiers. The identifier itself, the IRI, is the third concept, since linking data means that two documents use the same identifiers for the same things." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Semantics> .
chx:S_LinkedData a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Linked data"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Linked data" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 96 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Semantics ;
    skos:definition "Linked data is a way of publishing data so that its parts can be referred to from other data. Each thing that is described, and each kind of property it can have, is given a global identifier, and statements are made by connecting identifiers; the JSON-LD specification describes the idea as being all about describing the relationships between different resources, which may be defined in different documents on the web or within the same document (World Wide Web Consortium, 2020). In this chapter the things are blocks, transactions and nodes, and the statements are the fields that the node returns.\n\nShared identifiers are what lets data from different sources be combined without guessing. The specification of JSON-LD explains the problem with plain names: tokens such as name and homepage are ambiguous, and the solution is to use IRIs for unambiguous identification, so that developers do not accidentally step on each other's terms (World Wide Web Consortium, 2020). The same argument applies to the Bitcoin data: the word hash in one program's output may mean the hash of a block and in another's the hash of a transaction including its witness, as the chapter's own transaction decoding showed.\n\nThe concepts of this group are met in the way the node's output could be published as a dataset. A block, with its height, hash, time and the identifiers of its transactions, is a natural description of a resource; a context can be added to it while the result can still be read by every program that reads JSON, since the format was designed to integrate easily into deployed systems that already use JSON (World Wide Web Consortium, 2020).\n\nLinked data does not make data true. It makes data interpretable, and what a statement says about the world is still up to the source of the statement. For Bitcoin, the truth of a statement about a block comes from the verification the node performed, not from the identifiers used to publish it, and that is why the verified data concept is placed in this group beside the technical ones." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#LinkedData> .
chx:S_VerifiedData a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Verified data"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Verified data" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 97 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_LinkedData ;
    skos:definition "Verified data is data that the party using it has checked against the rules instead of taking on someone's word. A full node downloads the blocks, checks each against the consensus rules and keeps only those that pass, so every answer it gives about the chain is the answer of a program that checked the chain; that is the property that the book relies on when it has the reader ask the node, and not a block explorer, about block 123456. The block explorer of chapter 2 shows the same data and can be right, but the reader cannot check it.\n\nThe property can be described in the vocabulary of provenance. The PROV ontology of the World Wide Web Consortium provides classes for representing and exchanging information about how data was produced (Lebo et al., 2013). It describes an entity as a thing, physical, digital or conceptual, an activity as something that occurs over a period of time and acts upon or with entities, and an agent as something that bears some form of responsibility for an activity taking place (Lebo et al., 2013). In these terms the answer of a node is an entity that was generated by an activity, the verification, and attributed to an agent, the node that the reader runs.\n\nThe worked example shows verification in miniature. The header of block 123456 is a set of six fields: the version, the hash of the previous block, the Merkle root, the time, the bits and the nonce. Packed into 80 bytes and hashed twice with SHA-256, the header gives a value which, written backwards, equals the hash that the node printed as the identifier of the block; the example computes this and prints True. A reader who does this has not trusted the node's printed identifier but recomputed it from the fields.\n\nThe chain of verification has a precise shape. The identifier of a block is the hash of its header, the header contains the Merkle root, which commits to every transaction of the block, and the header also contains the hash of the previous block, so that checking one header ties it to all the earlier ones. A node does all of this for every block from the first one, and adds the check of the proof of work and of each transaction's signatures; when it then says that a block has a given height or a transaction a given identifier, the statement rests on the checks that it made.\n\nVerified data is authoritative only for the party who verified it, and only to the extent of the node's software and its keeping of the data. A node that was altered, as the assurance group warned, would verify nothing. Verified data also does not travel: if the reader copies the node's answer into a document, the document's readers see only a number, and the provenance has to be added deliberately, for example with the vocabulary above, to tell them where the number came from." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#VerifiedData> .
chx:S_JsonLdContext a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "JSON-LD context"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "JSON-LD context" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 98 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_LinkedData ;
    skos:definition "A JSON-LD context is the part of a JSON document that says what its keys mean. The specification of JSON-LD 1.1 defines the format as a JSON-based format to serialize linked data, whose syntax is designed to easily integrate into deployed systems that already use JSON, and explains that a context is introduced using an entry with the key @context (World Wide Web Consortium, 2020). In its own words, a context is used to map terms to IRIs (World Wide Web Consortium, 2020). The terms are the keys of the JSON text, such as height and hash, and the IRIs are global identifiers of the concepts they stand for.\n\nA context lets the same document be read by a person as ordinary JSON and by a program as linked data. The specification gives the reason in a short phrase: the terms should expand to IRIs so that developers do not accidentally step on each other's terms (World Wide Web Consortium, 2020). For the data of a Bitcoin node this solves the ambiguity of names. The field hash of a block and the field hash of a decoded transaction are both called hash, and a context for each document can map the same key to different identifiers, so that a program combining the two sees that they are different properties.\n\nThe worked example is a small model of the mechanism and not an implementation of JSON-LD. It takes two fields of the answer for block 123456, the height and the hash, and a context that maps each key to an identifier in a vocabulary that was made up for the exercise, and prints the pairs of identifier and value. A real processor does much more, such as handling nested objects, types and relative identifiers, and the specification describes all that, but the mapping from term to identifier is the part that matters here.\n\nIn the specification the context sits under the key @context, either at the top of the document or inside an object, and its entries map a term to an IRI or to a map, which the specification calls an expanded term definition and which can add a type or other details (World Wide Web Consortium, 2020). The processing of a document replaces each term by the IRI that the context assigns, and the result is a set of statements about identified things. The document needs no change in its shape: a program that reads the JSON as before sees one extra key, @context, and a program that understands linked data uses it.\n\nAdding a context does not check anything. A context that maps height to an identifier for a different meaning would still be a valid context, so the quality of the vocabulary behind the identifiers is what matters, and a vocabulary of Bitcoin terms that the community has agreed on is not something this chapter relies on. The identifiers in the worked example are invented for the example and point to nothing. A context is also written against a particular set of keys, so it has to be revisited if the keys of the answer are renamed." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#JsonLdContext> .
chx:S_Iri a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "IRI"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "IRI" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 99 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_LinkedData ;
    skos:definition "An IRI, short for internationalized resource identifier, is a global name written like a web address. The JSON-LD specification defines the absolute form of an IRI as one that contains a scheme along with a path and optional query and fragment segments, and says that Linked Data, and the Web in general, uses IRIs for unambiguous identification (World Wide Web Consortium, 2020). Chapter 2 introduced the uniform resource identifier, with which the payment links of that chapter are written; the IRI is the identifier used by the web standards on which JSON-LD builds.\n\nIdentity is the foundation of linking: two documents can only be about the same thing if they use the same name for it. The specification says that IRIs are a fundamental concept of Linked Data, and that for nodes to be truly linked, dereferencing the identifier should result in a representation of that node (World Wide Web Consortium, 2020). Dereferencing means using the identifier to fetch something, so an IRI can serve at once as a name and as the address of a page that tells what the name means.\n\nIRIs appear in the context of a JSON-LD document, where each term is mapped to one, and as the identifiers of nodes marked by the keyword @id (World Wide Web Consortium, 2020). They appear in the vocabularies that data is described with, such as the PROV ontology, whose terms all have a common namespace beginning http://www.w3.org/ns/prov# (Lebo et al., 2013). In the worked example the IRI is split at the symbol # into the address of a vocabulary and the name of a term in it.\n\nAn IRI is made of parts, in the form of a scheme such as https, a path, and optionally a query and a fragment, which is the part after the symbol #. In a vocabulary, the part before the fragment names the vocabulary and the fragment names the term, so that many terms share one address. The specification calls an IRI whose parts are all given absolute, and it turns a relative reference into one by using a base IRI (World Wide Web Consortium, 2020).\n\nAn IRI looks like a web address but need not lead anywhere, and the worked example's identifiers do not. Two IRIs that differ in one character name different things, so the capitalization and the final slash or fragment have to be exactly the same in every document that uses the identifier. A well-formed IRI is also not a promise that the thing is well defined: the quality of the definition is a matter of the people who maintain the vocabulary." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Iri> .
chx:S_Computing a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Computing foundations"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Computing foundations" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 100 ;
    skos:definition "The computing foundations branch collects the everyday vocabulary of programs and computers that the other branches use without stopping to explain it: bytes and bit shifts, times and random numbers, files and processes, libraries and databases, protocols and tests. A student of this course is expected to know Bitcoin's own ideas from the first two chapters, and the first two chapters introduced hashes, hexadecimal notation and public-key cryptography, but they did not need the plain machinery on which Bitcoin Core runs. Chapter 3 is the first chapter in which the reader works with the software itself, so the machinery has to be named.\n\nWithout this vocabulary the commands and logs of the chapter are noise: a log that says the signature cache has 16 MiB and the server was started with worker threads means nothing to a reader for whom a mebibyte, a cache and a thread are undefined. With it, each line of the output becomes a small fact that can be checked, and the reader can see why the project chose, say, a fixed number of bytes for a cookie or a database for the set of unspent outputs. The branch is deliberately plain, and each concept is tied to a line or a file of the chapter's own evidence.\n\nThe concepts are met in the start-up log, in the data directory, in the documentation of the build and in the source code that the chapter asks the reader to read. The branch has two groups. The first, representation, explains how information is written down: as bytes, as shifted bits, as a time, as a random value and in the Base64 text form. The second, systems, explains the places where programs run and meet each other: files and directories, processes, libraries, databases, platforms, network protocols and software tests.\n\nEvery concept of the branch has a worked example that runs on the python standard library alone and prints a value that the text then uses. Where the concept was introduced by a document, the document is quoted and the quotation is checked against the saved copy of the document, so that the foundations rest on the same kind of evidence as the Bitcoin concepts that depend on them." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Computing> .
chx:S_Representation a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Representation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Representation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 101 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Computing ;
    skos:definition "Representation is the group of concepts about how a program writes information down. Bitcoin Core's data is numbers and text, but on a computer both are sequences of bytes, and the same number can be written as bytes in two orders, as hexadecimal text or as Base64 text. A time is a number of seconds, a secret is a number that nobody can predict, and an amount is an integer that the program halves by moving its bits. The five concepts of the group are the byte, the bit shift, Unix time, the random number and Base64.\n\nMany apparent puzzles in the chapter are only questions of representation. Why does the hash of a block, printed by the node, look reversed compared with the bytes that were hashed? Why is the halving of the subsidy a shift and not a division? Why does the cookie file hold 64 characters when the cookie is 32 bytes? In each case the answer is that the same information has two representations, and that a program converts between them at a known point. A reader who sees this stops being confused by the conversions and can check them.\n\nThe group is met in the serialized transaction, in the fields of the block header, in the files of the data directory and in the headers of the HTTP requests of the interface branch. The saved evidence of the chapter contains each of these: the 80 bytes of a header, the 4-byte time, the 32 random bytes of the cookie, and the Basic scheme of the web request, all of which are used by the concepts below.\n\nA representation is not the information. The text 1a6a93b3 and the four bytes it stands for are the same value, but a program that compares text with bytes will find them different, and a number written with its bytes in the other order is a different number. The concepts of the group therefore state, next to each representation, the rule for converting it and a computation that shows the conversion." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Representation> .
chx:S_Byte a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Bit and byte"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Bit and byte" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 102 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Representation ;
    skos:definition "A bit is a single binary digit, which is either 0 or 1. A byte is a group of eight bits, so it can take 256 different values, and a computer stores and sends information as a sequence of bytes. Chapter 2 introduced hexadecimal notation, in which each digit stands for 4 bits, and the connection is that one byte is exactly two hexadecimal digits: sixteen times sixteen is 256, which is two to the power eight. The worked example reads the text 0a0b as two bytes and the text ff as the byte with the value 255.\n\nSizes in Bitcoin are given in bytes, and the chapter's figures only make sense when the unit is clear. A block header is 80 bytes, a hash is 32 bytes, which is 64 hexadecimal digits, a cookie is 32 random bytes, and the chapter's examples talk about caches of 16 MiB and a pruning target of 550 MiB. The size of a transaction in bytes decides how much of a block it takes, and the cost of running a node is counted in the bytes it stores and transfers, so a reader needs to move between the units easily.\n\nThe units appear in the start-up log, where the book prints that 16 MiB out of 16 MiB were requested for the signature cache, and in the output of the clone command, which reports 217.74 MiB received at 13.05 MiB per second (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The source of Bitcoin Core defines the unit in a small file of conversions, where a mebibyte is written with a shift of 20 bits and a gibibyte with a shift of 30 (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A mebibyte is therefore 1,048,576 bytes, the 550 MiB of the pruning minimum are 576,716,800 bytes, and the 217.74 MiB of the clone are about 228 million bytes.\n\nThe prefix mebi means a power of two, 2 to the power 20, and differs by about five percent from the million bytes of the prefix mega, which is why the file with the conversions uses its own names. Several bytes can also be read as one number, and then the order matters. The block header stores the bits field as the four bytes b3 93 6a 1a, which read with the last byte first, called little-endian, give the number 0x1a6a93b3, whereas the same four bytes read with the first byte first would give a different number. The worked example prints the 4-byte form of the number 1 in both orders, and the documentation of SQLite notes the same distinction for its files, which can be copied between architectures that differ in it (SQLite, 2026).\n\nTwo confusions are frequent. A bit and a byte are different units, and a link speed given in bits per second must be divided by eight to give bytes. And a mebibyte is not a megabyte, so a figure in one unit must not be compared with a limit in the other without converting it; the download page of Bitcoin Core speaks of gigabytes, and the log speaks of mebibytes, so the reader should not expect the figures to match exactly." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Byte> .
chx:S_BitShift a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Bit shift"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Bit shift" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 103 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Representation ;
    skos:definition "A bit shift moves all the bits of an integer by a number of places. Shifting to the left by one place doubles the number and shifting to the right by one place halves it, with the bit that falls off the right end lost. Shifting by n places multiplies or divides by two to the power n. The operation is written with the signs << and >> in the C++ of Bitcoin Core and in python, and the worked example applies it to the initial subsidy of 5,000,000,000 satoshis, which gives the amounts of the successive eras: 5,000,000,000, 2,500,000,000, 1,250,000,000 and 625,000,000.\n\nBitcoin's rules use shifts where one would otherwise write a division or a power. The function that computes the block subsidy starts from 50 coins and shifts the amount right by the number of halvings, and it first returns zero if the number of halvings is 64 or more, because the shift would then be undefined (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The compact form of the target in a block header multiplies a three-byte number by a power of 256, which is a shift left by a whole number of bytes. BIP 9 shifts a block's version right by 29 bits to see whether its top three bits are 001 (Wuille et al., 2015).\n\nIn the source, the halving is the line nSubsidy >>= halvings in the function GetBlockSubsidy (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The target of block 123456 is the number 0x6a93b3 shifted to the left by 23 bytes, as the worked example of the compact target does, and the version check of BIP 9 turns the value 0x20000002 into 1 with a right shift of 29 bits, as the soft fork concept shows. Shifting by 8 places moves a number by one byte, so that 0x6a93b3 shifted by 8 is 0x6a93b300, two hexadecimal digits longer.\n\nThe shift is exact as long as no non-zero bit falls off. The amount 5,000,000,000 is divisible by 2 to the power 9, so nine shifts to the right are exact and give 9,765,625; this number is odd, so the tenth shift does not divide exactly, and it gives 4,882,812, which drops half a satoshi. The consensus rules accept this rounding because the rule is the code: the subsidy of every era is whatever the shift gives, and every node computes the same value.\n\nShifts on integers of limited size have limits. The comment in the source says that the explicit check, which returns zero for 64 or more halvings, exists to force the block reward to zero when the right shift is undefined (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A shift whose result is undefined is a hazard of fixed-size integers, and the authors guard against it with a check. The python integers of the worked examples have no fixed size, so the examples of this chapter do not meet that limit, and the reader should not assume that every language behaves in the same way." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#BitShift> .
chx:S_UnixTime a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Unix time"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Unix time" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 104 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Representation ;
    skos:definition "Unix time is a way of writing a moment as a single number, the count of seconds since the start of 1 January 1970 in universal time. The book's table of the block header describes the time field as the approximate creation time of the block in Unix epoch time (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The worked example shows that the value 0 is that start and that the largest value of a 4-byte unsigned number, 4,294,967,295, is a moment in February 2106, which is the date at which the header's time field runs out.\n\nA single number is easy to store, compare and calculate with. The consensus rules compare times of blocks with other times, such as the median time past of the previous blocks, and BIP 9 measures the start and the timeout of a deployment in the same unit (Wuille et al., 2015). A time in the header takes only four bytes, which is the reason the format was chosen, and the node can use ordinary integer comparison to enforce the rules about it.\n\nThe times of the chapter are Unix times. The first block carries the time 1231006505, which the worked example of the founder concept converts to 3 January 2009 18:15:05, and block 123456 carries 1305200806, with a median time past of 1305197900 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The release process of Bitcoin Core uses the same notion when it fixes a reference UNIX timestamp so that builds are reproducible (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The nodes also reject blocks whose time is too far ahead of their own clock, and the limit in the source is 2 hours, 7,200 seconds (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nUnix time counts seconds from one fixed moment, so the number is the same everywhere and a program turns it into a local date only when it prints it; the worked example prints its dates in universal time. Because a block's time is a number, a rule about time is a comparison of numbers: the node compares the time of a block with a limit of 2 hours, 7,200 seconds, beyond its own current time, and with the median time past of the previous blocks.\n\nThe time of a block is a claim by the miner and not a measurement. The header of block 123456 has a time that is 2,906 seconds later than the median time past of its predecessors, and both numbers are correct for their purposes. A student who reads the time of a block as the exact moment of its creation will be wrong by minutes, and a student who forgets that a 4-byte unsigned number ends in February 2106 will misjudge how long the field can serve." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#UnixTime> .
chx:S_RandomNumber a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Random number"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Random number" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 105 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Representation ;
    skos:definition "A random number, in the sense that security needs, is a value that an attacker cannot predict. It is not enough that the value looks irregular; the value must come from a source whose output cannot be guessed, such as the sources of randomness that the operating system and the processor provide. Bitcoin Core uses such values for the cookie that protects its interface and for the salt of stored credentials, and the helper script for credentials draws them from the secrets module of python.\n\nEvery secret in the chapter is a random number whose strength is its length. The cookie is 32 random bytes, which is 256 bits, and the salt of a stored credential is 16 random bytes. If the source were predictable, the attacker could compute the secret instead of guessing it, and the interface, which has the power to spend from a wallet, would be open. Randomness is therefore a security matter, and the log of the node reports where it gets it.\n\nThe log of release 31.1 contains the lines that say the node uses RdSeed and RdRand as additional entropy sources (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The source of the authentication cookie declares the size of 32 bytes and fills it with the function that draws random bytes (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The comment in the header of the random number code states that the node maintains a single global 256-bit state for all high-quality randomness, and describes a second generator that is seeded once with secure random data but is then completely deterministic, for the cases where speed matters more than secrecy (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The script rpcauth.py asks for 16 bytes of salt and 32 bytes of password from the secrets module (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA source of randomness collects unpredictable events and mixes them into a state from which it produces output. The worked example calls the same function twice, and gets two different 32-character values, each the hexadecimal text of 16 random bytes. The test that the two differ cannot prove randomness, but a failure would prove a fault, and the chance that two truly random 128-bit values agree is negligible.\n\nA value that merely looks random, such as the output of a deterministic generator started from a known number, is not secure. The cookie or the salt must come from the secure source, and reusing the same value for two purposes would defeat it. Another trap is the length: a short random value is easy to guess even if the source is perfect, which is why the project chose 32 bytes for the cookie." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#RandomNumber> .
chx:S_Base64 a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Base64"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Base64" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 106 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Representation ;
    skos:definition "Base64 is an encoding that writes any sequence of bytes using only 64 printable characters. The standard that defines it describes the commonly used base 64, base 32 and base 16 encoding schemes, of which base 16 is the hexadecimal text of chapter 2 (Josefsson, 2006). The worked example encodes the text user:pass into dXNlcjpwYXNz and decodes it back to the original, so nothing is lost and nothing is hidden.\n\nMany carriers of data, such as the headers of a web request, can hold only text. Base64 lets a program put arbitrary bytes into such a carrier. The Basic authentication scheme of HTTP, which Bitcoin Core accepts for its interface, transmits credentials as a user and a password separated by a colon, encoded using Base64 (Reschke, 2015).\n\nThe book's calls to the interface and the helper libraries use the form: the Authorization header holds the word Basic and the Base64 text of the user and the password (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Todd, 2023). The helper script rpcauth.py creates a password with the function token_urlsafe(32), whose comment in the source says that it creates a 32-byte password in Base64 form (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The encoded text is longer than the data: 30 bytes become 40 characters, and 31 bytes become 44, because the output is written in groups of four characters, three bytes at a time.\n\nThe standard describes the process: the encoding represents 24-bit groups of input bits as output strings of 4 encoded characters, formed by concatenating three 8-bit input groups and treating the 24 bits as four 6-bit groups, each of which is used as an index into an array of 64 printable characters, and special processing, the padding, is performed if fewer than 24 bits are available at the end (Josefsson, 2006). Decoding reverses the steps. The process uses no key and no secret, which is why the encoding is reversible by anybody, and why it is called an encoding and not an encryption.\n\nThe most common mistake is to treat the encoded text as protected. The standard of the Basic scheme itself warns that it is not secure on its own and should be used with a secure system, since the user and the password are passed over the network as cleartext (Reschke, 2015). For Bitcoin Core this is the reason the project advises against exposing the interface over the public Internet, and the reason a cookie or a hashed credential is kept in a file with restricted access." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Base64> .
chx:S_Systems a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Systems"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Systems" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 107 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Computing ;
    skos:definition "Systems is the group of concepts about where programs run and how they meet. A program lives in files in a directory, runs as a process with threads, uses libraries written by others, keeps data in a database, is built for a platform, talks to other programs through a protocol, and is checked by tests. All of these appear in the chapter, from the data directory and the daemon to the libraries and the unit tests of the source.\n\nBitcoin Core is a large program, and the chapter shows a reader how to build it, run it and talk to it. Each of those activities touches one of the concepts of this group, and a reader who can name them can read the documentation of the project, for example the file that lists what the data directory contains or the notes on what each platform needs. The group also helps with the final part of the chapter, where the choice of a library or a node implementation depends on the language and the platform.\n\nThe concepts are met in the chapter's evidence. The 28 files of release 31.1 are named for the platform that they were built for. The data directory holds the databases of the chain state and the index. The log records the threads of the server. The README of the project describes the tests that every change must pass, and the build notes name the package managers of the supported systems.\n\nThese are general concepts, and the explanations here only go as far as the chapter needs. A reader who wants more about operating systems or databases will find that each concept is the subject of a large field; the aim here is to be precise about the facts the chapter rests on." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Systems> .
chx:S_FileAndDirectory a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "File and directory"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "File and directory" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 108 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Systems ;
    skos:definition "A file is a named piece of stored data, and a directory is a place that holds files and other directories, so that names form a tree. A path is the list of names from the top of the tree to a file. The example from the book's log is the path /lotsofspace/bitcoin/.cookie, which the worked example splits into the top, the directories lotsofspace and bitcoin, and the file name .cookie. Directories are also called folders, and the release archive of the chapter has a folder bin and a folder libexec.\n\nAlmost everything that Bitcoin Core keeps is a file in a directory, and the documentation of the project describes the layout. The data directory of the node holds the blocks, the chain state, the log, the configuration file and the cookie, and the reader must know which of them can be deleted, which must be backed up and which must be protected from other users. A path is also how the node is told where to put its data, with an option that names a directory.\n\nThe project's file documentation lists the entries of the data directory in a table, with the block index in blocks/index/, the chain state in chainstate/ and the optional transaction index in indexes/txindex/, each marked as a LevelDB database (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The evidence of the chapter lists the entries that a fresh node created, from banlist.json and bitcoin.conf to peers.dat and settings.json, and the book's log names the cookie file by its full path (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe directory tree is resolved from the top: to find the cookie, the system reads the top directory to find lotsofspace, reads that to find bitcoin, and reads that to find .cookie. Each file has attributes, among them who may read and write it. The cookie is created with permissions that depend on a mask applied at creation time, and the project sets the mask so that, by default, only the owner can read the file.\n\nA path is relative or absolute, and a relative path depends on the directory the program was started in, which is a common cause of a program not finding its configuration. The project's documentation gives a different default location of the data directory for each of the three common systems, so a path that works on one machine is not a path that works on another." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#FileAndDirectory> .
chx:S_Process a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Process and thread"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Process and thread" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 109 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Systems ;
    skos:definition "A process is a program in execution: the computer loads the program, gives it an area of memory of its own and runs it. A thread is a sequence of steps inside a process, and a process can have several threads, which share its memory and can run at the same time on different processor cores. The worked example divides the numbers below 1000 into four quarters, as four threads would share the work, adds up each quarter and combines the four partial sums into the total 499,500; the browser that runs the example cannot start threads, so the quarters are added one after another, which gives the same sums.\n\nThe distinction explains several facts about a running node. The daemon of the chapter is a single process that runs for days. Within it, the log of the book says that script verification uses 3 additional threads and that the interface started 4 worker threads, while the current log reports 16 worker threads, so that the node can check signatures and answer requests in parallel (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The project's documentation presents the split of the program into several processes as a gain in isolation (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe documentation of the multiprocess build describes the future division of the program in these terms: the node, the wallet and the graphical interface would run in separate address spaces for better isolation, with the node starting the wallet as another process and communicating with it over a socket pair (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The command that starts an external program, the one that the alert notification uses, runs in a thread of its own so that a slow script cannot hold up the node, as the source of the notification shows.\n\nThe operating system schedules processes and threads onto the processor cores, and gives each process its memory, so that the memory use of a node is the sum of what its parts request. That is why the guide to reducing memory speaks of the sizes of the caches and of the number of connections, and why the build notes of the project warn that a compilation needs at least 1.5 GB of memory. Threads share data and must coordinate their access; processes share nothing unless they ask, through a channel such as a socket.\n\nA thread does not make a program faster in every case, and more threads use more memory. For a reader the practical rule is that the number of threads, like the cache sizes, is a setting with a default, and the default of one release is not the default of another, as the two logs, with 4 and 16 worker threads, show." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Process> .
chx:S_Library a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Software library"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Software library" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 110 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Systems ;
    skos:definition "A software library is a collection of ready-made code that other programs call. The program does not copy the code into its own text; it names the library and uses its functions. The worked example calls the hashing library of python to compute the SHA-256 digest of the text abc, and prints the first eight characters, ba7816bf. The same word is used for the libraries a build needs, the shared library of the project itself, and the libraries for other languages in the last section of the chapter.\n\nLibraries make large programs possible and are also a source of risk. The build notes of Bitcoin Core list the libraries that must be installed before a build, such as the Boost libraries for general C++ code, SQLite for the wallet, ZeroMQ for messaging and Cap'n Proto for communication between processes (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Each of them is code that the project did not write and that guards money all the same, which is why the project documents its dependencies and offers a way to build them from source.\n\nThe reference implementation is itself a library as well as a program. The file documentation of the project lists a shared library, libbitcoinkernel, which contains the core consensus and validation code, so that other programs can reuse the rules without taking the rest of the node (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The book glossary describes LevelDB, the database in which the chain state is kept, as a light-weight, single-purpose library for persistence (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The wrapper library of the interface branch and the toolkits of the ecosystem branch are libraries for programmers who talk to a node.\n\nA library is used in one of two ways. It can be linked into the program when it is built, so that the finished program contains it, or it can be a shared file that the program loads when it starts, which is the form of libbitcoinkernel.so and which lets several programs use one copy. In both cases the program needs the library's version to be compatible, which is the reason the build documentation names the packages for each system.\n\nA library is trusted by the program that uses it. A flaw in a library affects every program that includes it, and a library that is no longer maintained, like the toolkits that the registry data of the ecosystem branch shows to be old, can fail when the rules of the network change. The reader should therefore ask of a library, as of a program, where it comes from and who looks after it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Library> .
chx:S_Database a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Database"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Database" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 111 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Systems ;
    skos:definition "A database is a store of data organised so that a program can find and change particular items quickly and safely. Two kinds appear in the chapter. A key-value store keeps one value under each key, and the book glossary describes LevelDB as an open source on-disk key-value store (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A relational database keeps tables of rows and is queried with SQL, and the SQLite project describes its product as an in-process library that implements a self-contained, serverless, zero-configuration, transactional SQL database engine (SQLite, 2026).\n\nA node must look up the unspent output that a new transaction spends, without reading the whole chain, and must do so for every input of every transaction. A database makes this lookup quick and, as SQLite's own summary of its features puts it, offers ACID transactions, even after power loss (SQLite, 2026). The same need arises for a wallet, which keeps keys, descriptors and transactions that it must never lose.\n\nThe project's file documentation names the databases of the data directory: the block index in blocks/index/, the chain state in chainstate/, and the optional transaction index in indexes/txindex/ are all LevelDB databases (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The wallet of the current release is a single SQLite file, as the descriptor wallet concept explains, and SQLite's own description says that it reads and writes directly to ordinary disk files and has no separate server process (SQLite, 2026). The older wallet format used a third database, BerkeleyDB, which the book's log of the wallet check names with its version (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The worked example holds the two outputs of Alice's transaction in a python dictionary, which is a small key-value store kept in memory, sums them to 95,000 satoshis, and looks one up by its key; a dictionary does what a key-value store does, but without a file and without the guarantees against power loss.\n\nA key-value store is searched by exact key, as the lookup by outpoint in the example, and is very fast for it; it cannot answer a question about all values at once without reading them. A SQL database can sum, sort and join with a single query, where the example needs a line of its own for the sum. The cost of that flexibility is a more complex engine. Both kinds write changes so that, after a crash, the data is either in the state before the change or the state after it, which is what the word transactional means for SQLite, and which is a different use of the word from a Bitcoin transaction.\n\nA database is not the chain. The chain state is derived from the blocks, and the help text of the node describes the option -reindex-chainstate as wiping the chain state and rebuilding it from the block files on disk (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Also, the word transaction means two different things in this chapter, a transfer of bitcoin and an atomic change of a database, and the two must not be mixed up." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Database> .
chx:S_Platform a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Platform"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Platform" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 112 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Systems ;
    skos:definition "A platform is the kind of computer a program is built for. It is described by the processor family, which decides the machine instructions, and by the operating system, which is the program that starts other programs and gives them access to memory, files and the network. A program compiled for one platform does not run on another. The names of the files in the release of Bitcoin Core show it: the list of checksums of release 31.1 has archives for eight platforms, among them aarch64-linux-gnu, x86_64-linux-gnu, arm64-apple-darwin, x86_64-apple-darwin and win64.\n\nThe user has to choose the right file, and the builder has to build one for each. The names combine, by their form, the processor family at the start with the operating system after it: x86_64-linux-gnu names the x86_64 family with Linux, arm64-apple-darwin the arm64 family with the system of Apple's computers, whose name in the book is macOS, and win64 names Windows on a 64-bit processor. The download for the wrong platform will not run, and the checksum list is organised by these names so that a user can find the line for the file they have.\n\nThe chapter itself names the platforms in two places. The build instructions are separate for each: the book names Android, FreeBSD, NetBSD, OpenBSD, macOS, Unix and Windows, and the current tree has a file of build notes for each of the systems (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The release evidence of this chapter lists 28 files of release 31.1, of which the Linux archive for the 64-bit processors was downloaded, checked and run. The continuous integration of the project, as its README says, makes sure that every pull request is tested on Windows, Linux and macOS (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe worked example extracts the platform name from each file name of the checksum list and finds eight. The same platform can appear in several files: the checksum list has, besides the ordinary archives, debug archives, archives whose names contain unsigned or codesigning, and the source archive, which belongs to no platform. The platform also decides the defaults of the program, for example the location of the data directory, which the project's documentation gives separately for each system.\n\nThe word platform is used loosely. In the first chapter it was used for kinds of wallet, such as desktop and mobile, and here it means the processor and the operating system. A program built for a platform may depend on features of the operating system, such as the way it handles files or the network, which is one more reason to test on each of them." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#Platform> .
chx:S_NetworkProtocol a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Network protocol"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Network protocol" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 113 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Systems ;
    skos:definition "A network protocol is an agreement between programs about what messages look like and in what order they are sent. HTTP, for example, is described by its standard as a stateless request and response protocol for exchanging messages across a connection, in which a client sends requests to a server and the server answers (Fielding et al., 2022). A client and a server are roles, not kinds of machine: the program that asks is the client and the program that answers is the server, and a single program, like a node, can be a server for one party and a client of another.\n\nThe protocols decide who can talk to the node and how. The interface of the chapter uses JSON-RPC messages carried by HTTP, and the specification of JSON-RPC says that it is independent of the transport, so that the same messages may travel within a process, over sockets or over HTTP (JSON-RPC Working Group, 2013). The network of nodes uses its own protocol, whose spreading of data chapter 2 described as gossiping, and the network information of a node reports a protocol version, 70016 in the book's example (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA network address has two parts: the machine and the port. The worked example splits the address http://127.0.0.1:8332/ into the scheme http, the host 127.0.0.1, the port 8332 and the path, which are the parts of the address in the book's curl call. The port number selects the service on the machine, and the source gives a different default for each network, so that a main-network node and a test-network node can run on one computer. The start-up log of the chapter records that the interface was bound to the address 127.0.0.1 and the port 8332 (Bitcoin Core, 2026). It also shows that the node first tried the address ::1 on the same port, which failed with a warning: the first is an address of the fourth version of the internet protocol and the second of the sixth, the two versions that the network information of a node lists as reachable (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA connection starts with the client opening a channel to the address and the port, over which the two sides exchange the messages of the protocol. Because the protocol fixes the format, any program that speaks it can take part: the book's example uses curl, a general program for HTTP, to talk to the node, and the specification notes that the carrier does not matter. The release notes of version 30.0 add that a node started through the new command can listen on a Unix socket for client connections (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA protocol says nothing about trust. HTTP in the form used here sends the credentials as cleartext, so the interface should not be reached over an open network, and the evidence node bound the interface to 127.0.0.1, which is why the chapter treats opening the port to the network as the largest risk. Two programs that speak the same protocol may also differ in the version they support, as the protocol version in the network information shows." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#NetworkProtocol> .
chx:S_SoftwareTesting a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Software testing"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Software testing" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 114 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Systems ;
    skos:definition "A software test is a small program that runs another program, or a part of it, on chosen inputs and checks that the result is the expected one. The README of Bitcoin Core describes two kinds: unit tests for the C++ code, which developers are strongly encouraged to write for new code, and regression and integration tests, which are written in python and exercise the whole node (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The worked example is a test in miniature: it computes the subsidy at the blocks around each halving and prints the values that a correct program must give.\n\nThe README itself gives the reason: testing and code review is the bottleneck for development, and this is a security-critical project where any mistake might cost people lots of money (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The software holds no money itself, but it decides which transactions are valid, and a bug in the rules could split the network or let a transaction through that should be refused. For a reader, the tests are also a source of runnable examples of the behaviour that the chapter describes.\n\nThe tests are met in the project and in the release. The README says that the CI systems make sure that every pull request is tested on Windows, Linux and macOS (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The release of version 30.0 installs the unit-test executable in the folder libexec and makes it available through the new command as bitcoin test (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The worked example shows that the subsidy at block 775,072 is 625,000,000 satoshis, which is 6.25 bitcoin: this differs from the 25 BTC reward that the book's output states for that block, which is a discrepancy that a test of the rule exposes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA test has three parts: the input, the expected output and the comparison. Tests are most useful at the edges of a rule, so the example has the blocks 209,999 and 210,000, on either side of the first halving. A failing test shows that either the code or the expectation is wrong, and finding which is the work of the developer. The tests are run automatically on every change, and the order of the project's process, with review by someone other than the author, adds a second kind of check.\n\nPassing tests do not prove that a program is correct; they show that it behaves as expected on the cases that someone thought of. The project's own statement that review is the bottleneck acknowledges that tests and review complement each other. A reader who relies on a release should also remember that the release was tested on the platforms of the project's continuous integration, and not necessarily on the reader's own." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#SoftwareTesting> .
chx:S_ProgrammingLanguage a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Programming language"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Programming language" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 115 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Systems ;
    skos:definition "A programming language is a formal notation in which a person writes the instructions of a program, together with the rules that give each form of writing its meaning. The text of a program in such a notation is its source code, and the worked example shows the point on a single number: in python the hexadecimal literal 0xff, the text 'ff' read in base 16 and the text '11111111' read in base 2 all give the value 255, and the literal 0b11111111 equals 0xff. The language decides which notations exist and what they mean.\n\nThe choice of language shapes what a program can do and what its authors have to take care of. The files of Bitcoin Core's source tree that the chapter reads have the ending cpp or h, such as validation.cpp and pow.cpp, which is the C++ in which the node is written, and the build notes warn that C++ compilers are memory-hungry (Bitcoin Core, 2026). Python appears where convenience matters more than speed: the project's README says that its regression and integration tests are written in Python, and the helper script for credentials, rpcauth.py, is a python program (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nA reader meets the languages at three places in the chapter. The node and its tools are built from C++ source with the compilation step of the build branch. The tests and helper scripts are run directly by the python interpreter, and every worked example of this chapter is a python expression, which the chapter's builder executes so that the printed values are the real ones. The last section of the chapter lists the toolkits by language, under eight headings from C and C++ to JavaScript, Java, Python, Go, Rust, Scala and C#, so that a programmer can talk to Bitcoin in the language they already use (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA program in a compiled language, such as C++, is translated before it runs into machine instructions for one platform, which is why a release has an archive for each platform. A program in an interpreted language, such as python, is read and carried out by another program, the interpreter, at the time it runs. The difference explains why a python script runs unchanged on the platforms that have an interpreter, whereas a C++ program has to be built again for each of them, and why the compiled node is the part that must be fast.\n\nA language is not a library and not a platform, although the three are often mentioned together. Knowing the language of a toolkit does not tell the reader whether the toolkit is maintained or correct, which is a question for the package registry and the project, as the ecosystem branch shows. And the same idea looks different in each language: the shift, the byte and the hash of the earlier concepts exist in all of them, but their spelling and limits differ, so a result computed in python is a statement about the rule and not about how another language would write it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch03#ProgrammingLanguage> .
